{"parentUuid":"bcf1216d-06a1-4ae5-b1a1-f821bd468e47","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfNrJfnWLVrNvD5YVBNkX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Q28s63kBJS8AMy18u9ak36","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen/js/rider.js","offset":900,"limit":500},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10993,"cache_read_input_tokens":255435,"output_tokens":125,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10993,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":125,"cache_read_input_tokens":255435,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10993,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10993},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.2.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"}],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Q28s63kBJS8AMy18u9ak36":{"file_path":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen/js/rider.js","offset":900,"limit":500}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfNrJXK2mv8Uiq6L1dXF2","type":"assistant","uuid":"318da5d9-606e-4f0a-982f-69c5abf648bb","timestamp":"2026-09-24T19:54:29.622Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen","sessionId":"f31fbc8b-fac8-4283-8f36-bb5424b19347","version":"2.1.281","gitBranch":"main","slug":"enchanted-prancing-stroustrup"}
{"parentUuid":"318da5d9-606e-4f0a-982f-69c5abf648bb","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c41cd6de-57c3-441a-96f7-ed621873bfe7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q28s63kBJS8AMy18u9ak36","type":"tool_result","content":"900\t       what keeps the handling honest; below it, a rider who has stopped is\n901\t       allowed to stay pointed wherever they stopped pointing.\n902\t\n903\t       AND IT IS MEASURED ON THE SNOW, which is the whole correctness of it.\n904\t\n905\t       The error used to be `atan2(vel.x, -vel.z)` against `this.yaw` — a\n906\t       bearing taken in the world horizontal plane against a bearing taken in\n907\t       the world horizontal plane, which sounds like it must be right and is\n908\t       not. The velocity this rule reads is rebuilt further down as `h·vFwd`,\n909\t       and `h` is the board's heading *projected onto the slope*: on any\n910\t       cross-slope that projection comes out at a different compass bearing\n911\t       from the raw yaw it was built from. So the rule saw a steering error\n912\t       that was nothing but the projection, rotated the board to remove it,\n913\t       and thereby tilted the heading further — which made the projection\n914\t       difference bigger on the next step. Positive feedback, on a rider\n915\t       touching nothing: measured on the opening pitch, the board came round\n916\t       at up to eleven degrees a second and a hands-off run left the twenty-\n917\t       metre corridor inside five seconds and was a hundred and thirty metres\n918\t       out at half a minute, on every seed tried.\n919\t\n920\t       `atan2(vLat, vFwd)` is the same question asked in the board's own\n921\t       tangent frame — the slip angle, the thing a rider actually feels — and\n922\t       a pure projection difference is invisible to it. A real wash-out still\n923\t       has real lateral velocity, so the board still comes back into line\n924\t       after a nudge, which is what the rule is for. */\n925\t    if (!brakeActive && Math.abs(input.turn) < 0.05 && speed > 3.5) {\n926\t      // In switch the direction of travel is the board's tail, so both\n927\t      // components flip and the slip angle is the same small number.\n928\t      const stance = this.switchStance ? -1 : 1;\n929\t      const slip = Math.atan2(vLat * stance, vFwd * stance);\n930\t      this.yaw += slip * (1 - Math.exp(-RIDER.selfCentre * dt));\n931\t    } else if (!brakeActive && Math.abs(input.turn) < 0.05 && speed < RIDER.fallLineSpeed) {\n932\t      /* …and below that, towards the fall line rather than towards the\n933\t         direction of travel, because a rider who has almost stopped has no\n934\t         meaningful direction of travel to line up with.\n935\t\n936\t         This is the other half of not getting stuck. The board comes to rest\n937\t         pointing across the hill — that is where a carve leaves it — and the\n938\t         velocity it would otherwise take its heading from is a few\n939\t         centimetres a second of nothing. Easing towards downhill is what a\n940\t         rider does with their back foot when they have stopped on a slope and\n941\t         want to be moving again, and without it the run can end in a place\n942\t         the player cannot get out of, which nothing in this game is allowed\n943\t         to do.\n944\t\n945\t         AND IT HAS TO ASK FOR A DIRECTION THE COURSE RULE WILL GRANT, or the\n946\t         two of them cancel and it is the trap it was written to prevent.\n947\t\n948\t         The fall line is a fact about the ground and it does not care about\n949\t         the course. On a bank steep enough across the run, it points further\n950\t         round than `courseLimit` allows the board to be — so this rule swung\n951\t         the board towards it, `limitCourseYaw` swung it straight back the\n952\t         same amount on the same step, and the net rotation was zero. The\n953\t         rider stayed pointed up the hill with gravity taking away what little\n954\t         speed the skate strokes returned. Measured on one seed: twenty-six\n955\t         seconds motionless at a dead stop, rear foot still working, and no\n956\t         way out of it — the exact failure described above, reached by a\n957\t         different road.\n958\t\n959\t         So the target is the nearest legal downhill: the true fall line if\n960\t         the course rule would allow it, and the edge of the cone it permits\n961\t         if not. Both rules then want the same thing and the board turns. */\n962\t      const dl = Math.hypot(n.x, n.z);\n963\t      if (dl > 1e-4) {\n964\t        const flow = this.courseFrame(n);\n965\t        const courseYaw = Math.atan2(flow.x, -flow.z);\n966\t        const rawFall = Math.atan2(n.x / dl, -(n.z / dl));\n967\t        const legalFall = courseYaw + clamp(\n968\t          wrapPi(rawFall - courseYaw), -RIDER.courseLimit, RIDER.courseLimit,\n969\t        );\n970\t        const fallYaw = legalFall + (this.switchStance ? Math.PI : 0);\n971\t        const swing = wrapPi(fallYaw - this.yaw) * (1 - Math.exp(-1.8 * dt));\n972\t        this.yaw += swing;\n973\t        /* …and whatever speed is left comes round with the board.\n974\t\n975\t           Rotating the board without the velocity is what \"he sometimes\n976\t           drives backwards\" actually was. At the old threshold this began at\n977\t           twelve km/h — still genuinely moving — so the board swung towards\n978\t           the fall line while the momentum carried on the way it was already\n979\t           going, and the rider ended up travelling one way at a hundred and\n980\t           fifty degrees to the way he was pointing. Turning the velocity by\n981\t           the same angle keeps the two together: he pivots, rather than\n982\t           sliding backwards while facing downhill. It is safe to do at this\n983\t           speed and only at this speed, because there is almost no momentum\n984\t           left to falsify. */\n985\t        const cs = Math.cos(swing);\n986\t        const sn = Math.sin(swing);\n987\t        const vx = vel.x;\n988\t        const vz = vel.z;\n989\t        /* In this file's yaw convention (yaw = atan2(vx, -vz)) a +swing on\n990\t           the heading is vx' = vx·cos − vz·sin, vz' = vx·sin + vz·cos. The\n991\t           signs were flipped, so the momentum pivoted *against* the board —\n992\t           doubling the exact divergence this rotation exists to remove. */\n993\t        vel.x = vx * cs - vz * sn;\n994\t        vel.z = vx * sn + vz * cs;\n995\t      }\n996\t    }\n997\t\n998\t    /* AND THE BOARD DOES NOT HOLD A LINE IN DEEP SNOW.\n999\t\n1000\t       This is the term that finally contains the boundary mountains, and it\n1001\t       took three attempts to find because the first two were answers to the\n1002\t       wrong question. Refusing the climb and taking the edge away both stop a\n1003\t       rider *gaining* height, and neither stops the thing that was actually\n1004\t       happening: a board held on a straight line along the bank keeps its\n1005\t       altitude while the run underneath it descends nearly a third of a metre\n1006\t       for every metre of z. Six seconds of that is sixty metres up the wall,\n1007\t       and not one centimetre of it was climbed.\n1008\t\n1009\t       What is missing from that picture is that the board is a metre and a\n1010\t       half of stiff plank sitting *in* the snow rather than on it. Pointed\n1011\t       across the fall line it has a metre of buried edge on the uphill side\n1012\t       and nothing under the downhill one; it does not hold, it pivots — which\n1013\t       is the first thing anybody learns the hard way about traversing deep\n1014\t       snow. So the yaw is eased towards the local fall line at a rate set by\n1015\t       how deep the board is, before the skid clamp below arbitrates between\n1016\t       that and the direction of travel, and the ordinary carve then brings\n1017\t       the velocity round behind it. On the containment wall the fall line\n1018\t       points home, so this is a rider being turned downhill by the snow\n1019\t       rather than a rider being pushed anywhere by the game.\n1020\t\n1021\t       Nothing here touches the quarterpipe: `offPiste` is zero everywhere\n1022\t       inside the lip, so a wall ride is still steered by the player alone. */\n1023\t    if (this.offPiste > 0 && speed > 1) {\n1024\t      // A perfectly level patch has no fall line to be turned towards.\n1025\t      if (Math.hypot(n.x, n.z) > 1e-4) {\n1026\t        const fallYaw = Math.atan2(n.x, -n.z)\n1027\t          + (this.switchStance ? Math.PI : 0);\n1028\t        this.yaw += wrapPi(fallYaw - this.yaw)\n1029\t          * (1 - Math.exp(-RIDER.wallPivot * this.offPiste * dt));\n1030\t      }\n1031\t    }\n1032\t\n1033\t    /* And a hard limit on how far the board is ever allowed to get from the\n1034\t       direction it is actually travelling.\n1035\t\n1036\t       This is the fix for the board ending up sideways. Nothing in the model\n1037\t       forbids it on its own: the yaw is driven by the carve and the velocity\n1038\t       is driven by the grip, and any moment those two disagree — a wash-out,\n1039\t       a graze, a bad landing, a brake held through a compression — the gap\n1040\t       between them opens and there is no term that closes it again except the\n1041\t       self-centring, which is deliberately gentle and switches off the instant\n1042\t       the rider touches the steering. So a player holding an edge through a\n1043\t       slide could end up travelling one way and pointing ninety degrees off\n1044\t       it, which is not a snowboard, it is a sledge.\n1045\t\n1046\t       Sixty degrees is about as far across their travel as anyone can hold a\n1047\t       board and still be riding it; past that the excess is taken back\n1048\t       immediately rather than smoothly, because there is no physical process\n1049\t       that would let it persist. A brake gets almost eighty-two degrees —\n1050\t       enough to throw a wall of snow and stop — but not enough to cross over\n1051\t       and silently change which end of the board is leading. */\n1052\t    /* …and a press is exactly the thing that rule is describing the absence\n1053\t       of. Sixty degrees is how far a board with both contact points buried can\n1054\t       be held off its own travel; a board standing on its nose has no tail in\n1055\t       the snow to hold anything, which is why a butter works at all. So the\n1056\t       clamp opens with the pressure rather than being suspended by it, and a\n1057\t       half-hearted press still cannot spin.\n1058\t\n1059\t       Passing ninety degrees is not a wider skid, it is a change of stance:\n1060\t       the end of the board that was leading is now trailing, and from there\n1061\t       the rider is riding switch. Flipping the reference at that crossing is\n1062\t       also what makes the clamp hysteretic — the error jumps from just over a\n1063\t       right angle to just under one and cannot flap. */\n1064\t    // The same ground-speed gate as the brake pivot above, and for the same\n1065\t    // reason: the skid clamp must not measure stance against a bearing that\n1066\t    // does not exist.\n1067\t    if (Math.hypot(vel.x, vel.z) > 0.6) {\n1068\t      const travelYaw = Math.atan2(vel.x, -vel.z);\n1069\t      let stanceYaw = travelYaw + (this.switchStance ? Math.PI : 0);\n1070\t      let off = wrapPi(this.yaw - stanceYaw);\n1071\t      if (this.press > 0.02 && Math.abs(off) > Math.PI / 2) {\n1072\t        this.switchStance = !this.switchStance;\n1073\t        stanceYaw = travelYaw + (this.switchStance ? Math.PI : 0);\n1074\t        off = wrapPi(this.yaw - stanceYaw);\n1075\t      }\n1076\t      const limit = this.press > 0.02\n1077\t        ? RIDER.maxSkid + (RIDER.pressSkid - RIDER.maxSkid) * this.press\n1078\t        : brakeActive ? RIDER.brakeSkid : RIDER.maxSkid;\n1079\t      /* Bounded correction rather than a hard subtract. Steering grows the\n1080\t         excess by less than `skidRecover·dt` a step, so in continuous play\n1081\t         this binds identically — but when the LIMIT itself drops in one\n1082\t         input frame (a butter released, a brake let go) the board now swings\n1083\t         back over a few frames instead of teleporting to legal. */\n1084\t      const maxCorr = RIDER.skidRecover * dt;\n1085\t      if (off > limit) this.yaw -= Math.min(off - limit, maxCorr);\n1086\t      else if (off < -limit) this.yaw -= Math.max(off + limit, -maxCorr);\n1087\t    }\n1088\t\n1089\t    // A carve can cross almost the entire hill, but it cannot become a U-turn.\n1090\t    // Killing the stored lip rate when the limit catches also prevents the\n1091\t    // invisible excess rotation reappearing on the next jump.\n1092\t    /* A press opens this one too, and it is safe to open because of what it\n1093\t       limits. This rule is about the *board*: it stops ordinary steering from\n1094\t       pointing the rider back up the mountain. What keeps the run going\n1095\t       downhill is `limitCourseVelocity` below, and that is untouched — so\n1096\t       during a butter the board may swing past the course while the momentum\n1097\t       carries straight on, which is exactly the trick and nothing else. */\n1098\t    const courseLimit = this.press > 0.02\n1099\t      ? RIDER.courseLimit + (RIDER.pressSkid - RIDER.courseLimit) * this.press\n1100\t      : brakeActive ? RIDER.brakeCourseLimit : RIDER.courseLimit;\n1101\t    if (this.limitCourseYaw(n, courseLimit, dt)) this.spinVel = 0;\n1102\t\n1103\t    /* What the press actually carried round, booked once and here.\n1104\t       `stepYaw` is from before any of the rotation rules ran and this is\n1105\t       after all of them, so what is counted is the rotation that survived —\n1106\t       a butter cannot be paid for a spin the skid clamp took straight back\n1107\t       off it, and it cannot be paid twice for the same radians either. */\n1108\t    if (this.press > 0.02) this.pressSpin += wrapPi(this.yaw - stepYaw);\n1109\t\n1110\t    /* What the edge could not hold becomes sideways speed — the wash-out —\n1111\t       and what it did hold is scrubbed out of the lateral velocity, which is\n1112\t       what \"the edge is gripping\" physically means. `carveLoad` is then the\n1113\t       honest fraction of the available grip the turn is using, so everything\n1114\t       downstream that reads it — the spray, the leg spring, the trail's\n1115\t       width, the camera's shake — is reading how hard the board is actually\n1116\t       working rather than how hard the key is pressed. */\n1117\t    vLat -= side * spill * dt;\n1118\t    const bite = gripNow * dt;\n1119\t    const held = clamp(vLat, -bite, bite);\n1120\t    vLat -= held;\n1121\t    this.slide = Math.abs(vLat);\n1122\t    this.lateral = vLat;\n1123\t    this.carveLoad = clamp(carve / gripNow, 0, 1);\n1124\t\n1125\t    // Redirecting momentum costs a little; failing to costs a lot. These are\n1126\t    // losses from the magnitude of forward speed, not pushes towards the\n1127\t    // board's tail: switch riding has negative vFwd, so subtracting them\n1128\t    // directly would accelerate it.\n1129\t    /* THE ONE THING A PRESS HAS TO BE PROTECTED FROM, which is this model's\n1130\t       own idea of what a sideways board means.\n1131\t\n1132\t       `vFwd` and `vLat` are the velocity written in the board's frame, so the\n1133\t       moment the board pivots away from its travel the whole of the momentum\n1134\t       is booked as lateral — and lateral is what the scrub and the damping\n1135\t       below exist to destroy, because everywhere else in this game a board\n1136\t       across its own travel is a wash-out or a speed check and destroying it\n1137\t       is the entire point. Measured: a butter held for a second and a bit\n1138\t       took ninety-four km/h down to nine, which is not a trick, it is a wall.\n1139\t\n1140\t       A press is the one case where that reading is wrong. The board has\n1141\t       pivoted but it has not dug in — one end is out of the snow, the base\n1142\t       is still flat on it, and the rider is still going the way they were\n1143\t       going. So both terms are scaled out with the pressure. What is left is\n1144\t       the plough below, which is the honest cost and is a fifth of the size. */\n1145\t    const pressGlide = 1 - 0.86 * this.press;\n1146\t    let forwardLoss = Math.abs(held) * RIDER.carveDrag\n1147\t      + this.slide * RIDER.slideScrub * pressGlide * dt;\n1148\t    /* And cutting the trench is not free either. A carved edge is slicing\n1149\t       through snow rather than gliding over it, and the further it is rolled\n1150\t       over the more of it is buried — which is why a run held on a hard edge\n1151\t       the whole way down a pitch arrives slower than one that let the board\n1152\t       run flat between turns. That trade is most of what makes a line a\n1153\t       decision rather than a shape. */\n1154\t    forwardLoss += RIDER.edgeDrag * sinEdge * this.carveLoad * dt;\n1155\t\n1156\t    // …and a board standing on one end of itself is ploughing with that end.\n1157\t    // A butter is a slow trick everywhere it has ever been done, and this is\n1158\t    // the whole of why: there is nothing gliding.\n1159\t    forwardLoss += RIDER.pressDrag * this.press * dt;\n1160\t\n1161\t    /* And climbing costs more than gravity alone charges for.\n1162\t\n1163\t       g·sin θ is the whole of the real physics and, on a hill this shallow,\n1164\t       it is not very much — five or six m/s² on the average pitch. What it\n1165\t       misses is that a board driven up a slope is also being driven *into*\n1166\t       it, and an edge loaded like that in soft snow scrubs. This is that\n1167\t       term, and it is the difference between a quarterpipe wall you have to\n1168\t       commit speed to and a free way to change lanes. */\n1169\t    if (this.climbRate > 0 && speed > 1) {\n1170\t      forwardLoss += RIDER.climbScrub * clamp(this.climbRate / speed, 0, 1) * dt;\n1171\t    }\n1172\t    /* …and out past the lip there is no piste under the board at all. Deep\n1173\t       unconsolidated snow does not scrub a board driven up it, it swallows\n1174\t       it: the base ploughs instead of gliding. Charged against the climb for\n1175\t       the reason given beside `RIDER.wallDrag` — a flat term big enough to\n1176\t       stop a fast rider also strands a slow one. */\n1177\t    if (this.offPiste > 0 && this.climbRate > 0 && speed > 1) {\n1178\t      forwardLoss += RIDER.wallDrag * this.offPiste * this.offPiste\n1179\t        * clamp(this.climbRate / speed, 0, 1) * dt;\n1180\t    }\n1181\t    const forwardSign = Math.sign(vFwd) || (this.switchStance ? -1 : 1);\n1182\t    vFwd = forwardSign * Math.max(0, Math.abs(vFwd) - forwardLoss);\n1183\t\n1184\t    // A deliberate braking sideslip remains broad and controllable; an\n1185\t    // accidental wash catches its edge and aligns much faster. Kinetic base\n1186\t    // friction below supplies the rest of the stop.\n1187\t    const slideDamping = (2.6\n1188\t      + (RIDER.brakeSlideDamping - 2.6) * braking) * pressGlide;\n1189\t    vLat *= Math.exp(-slideDamping * dt);\n1190\t\n1191\t    // Pressure progressively moves from a waxed base to an edged speed check.\n1192\t    const mu = RIDER.friction\n1193\t      + (RIDER.brakeFriction - RIDER.friction) * braking;\n1194\t    /* Deep snow is not a texture, it is work. Powder was 1.15x a groomed\n1195\t       run, which at any speed worth having is indistinguishable from the\n1196\t       piste; it ploughs properly now, and the corduroy is a touch slicker\n1197\t       than neutral so the ribbon actually rewards being on it. */\n1198\t    const matDrag = surf.groomed * 0.88 + surf.powder * 2.30\n1199\t      + surf.ice * 0.68 + surf.rock * 2.05;\n1200\t    const dec = mu * (this.world.surfaceDrag ?? 1) * matDrag\n1201\t      * RIDER.gravity * Math.max(0.2, n.y);\n1202\t    // The initial tail kick is already cutting snow before the board has\n1203\t    // reached full transverse velocity; afterwards real vLat dominates this.\n1204\t    this.slide += speed * 0.16 * braking;\n1205\t\n1206\t    vel.copy(h).multiplyScalar(vFwd).addScaledVector(r, vLat);\n1207\t\n1208\t    // Kinetic friction, then aerodynamic drag. A powered tuck bypasses the\n1209\t    // latter entirely; its speed floor is applied after the board has been\n1210\t    // brought back into a physically valid direction below.\n1211\t    const sp = vel.length();\n1212\t    if (sp > 1e-4) vel.multiplyScalar(Math.max(0, sp - dec * dt) / sp);\n1213\t    if (!this.tucking) {\n1214\t      const over = Math.max(0, sp - RIDER.dragKnee);\n1215\t      const drag = RIDER.drag * (1 + over * 0.35);\n1216\t      // Stable integration of dv/dt = -drag·v². Unlike the former Euler\n1217\t      // factor, this cannot turn negative and erase all momentum in one frame\n1218\t      // when W is released after an extreme-speed run.\n1219\t      vel.multiplyScalar(1 / (1 + drag * sp * dt));\n1220\t    }\n1221\t\n1222\t    /* One impulse per planted rear-foot stroke.\n1223\t\n1224\t       The phase is simulation state rather than an animation clock: the\n1225\t       powder puff, the boot plant and the speed gain therefore happen on the\n1226\t       same fixed 120 Hz step. The direction blends the board's legal leading\n1227\t       end with the generated course, so skating can free a stopped traverse\n1228\t       without ever pushing the rider uphill or tail-first. */\n1229\t    if (this.pushing) {\n1230\t      const before = this.pushPhase;\n1231\t      const advanced = before + RIDER.pushCadence * dt;\n1232\t      const wrapped = advanced >= 1;\n1233\t      this.pushPhase = advanced - Math.floor(advanced);\n1234\t\n1235\t      if (before < RIDER.pushPlant && advanced >= RIDER.pushPlant) {\n1236\t        const flow = this.courseFrame(n);\n1237\t        const stroke = this._g.set(Math.sin(this.yaw), 0, -Math.cos(this.yaw));\n1238\t        stroke.addScaledVector(n, -stroke.dot(n)).normalize();\n1239\t        if (this.switchStance) stroke.multiplyScalar(-1);\n1240\t        stroke.multiplyScalar(0.65).add(flow).normalize();\n1241\t        vel.addScaledVector(stroke, RIDER.pushImpulse);\n1242\t        this.pushStroke += 1;\n1243\t        this.emit('push', RIDER.pushImpulse, this.pushStroke);\n1244\t      }\n1245\t\n1246\t      if (wrapped && finishPush) {\n1247\t        this.pushing = false;\n1248\t        this.pushPhase = 0;\n1249\t      }\n1250\t    }\n1251\t\n1252\t    // Gravity, a collision or a severe wash-out may have arrived from outside\n1253\t    // the board frame. Keep those exceptional vectors in the forward half of\n1254\t    // the generated course as well.\n1255\t    this.limitCourseVelocity(vel, n, courseLimit);\n1256\t\n1257\t    // The two forward constraints above still allow opposite traverses: both\n1258\t    // can be down-course while one is tail-first relative to the board. Remove\n1259\t    // only that impossible component. A mismatched near-stop becomes a clean\n1260\t    // sideways skid or a stop, never a brief backwards ride.\n1261\t    const boardTravel = this._p.set(Math.sin(this.yaw), 0, -Math.cos(this.yaw));\n1262\t    boardTravel.addScaledVector(n, -boardTravel.dot(n)).normalize();\n1263\t    if (this.switchStance) boardTravel.multiplyScalar(-1);\n1264\t    const tailFirst = vel.dot(boardTravel);\n1265\t    if (tailFirst < 0) vel.addScaledVector(boardTravel, -tailFirst);\n1266\t\n1267\t    /* Guarantee continuous acceleration for every grounded step W is held.\n1268\t\n1269\t       Applying this after course and stance correction matters at very high\n1270\t       speed: it preserves the direction produced by the snow instead of\n1271\t       letting a forward impulse slowly steer the rider. Scaling also keeps\n1272\t       the rule exact through arbitrarily large finite speeds; there is no\n1273\t       target, curve, or clamp for it to asymptotically approach. */\n1274\t    if (poweredSpeed > 0) {\n1275\t      const drivenSpeed = vel.length();\n1276\t      if (drivenSpeed > 1e-5) {\n1277\t        if (drivenSpeed < poweredSpeed) vel.multiplyScalar(poweredSpeed / drivenSpeed);\n1278\t      } else {\n1279\t        vel.copy(boardTravel).multiplyScalar(poweredSpeed);\n1280\t      }\n1281\t    }\n1282\t\n1283\t    /* THE ONE THING THE BOUNDARY MOUNTAINS ARE NOT ALLOWED TO PERMIT.\n1284\t\n1285\t       Everything above is a cost, and full flow carries enough speed to pay\n1286\t       any finite amount tuned for ordinary riding. This is the invariant, and it\n1287\t       has to live *after* the powered floor for exactly that reason — the\n1288\t       floor is the last thing that writes a speed, so anything hoping to\n1289\t       bound the climb has to come after it.\n1290\t\n1291\t       What it removes is only the component of travel pointing up the\n1292\t       fall line, and only in proportion to how deep into the unpisted snow\n1293\t       the board is. Downhill motion, the traverse, a slide along the bank —\n1294\t       all untouched. `t` is the steepest-ascent direction in the surface's\n1295\t       own tangent plane, which is world up with the surface normal projected\n1296\t       out of it, so the scrub stays on the snow rather than fighting the\n1297\t       ground contact. On the run itself `offPiste` is zero and none of this\n1298\t       executes. */\n1299\t    if (this.offPiste > 0) {\n1300\t      const ny = n.y;\n1301\t      let tx = -n.x * ny;\n1302\t      let ty = 1 - ny * ny;\n1303\t      let tz = -n.z * ny;\n1304\t      const tl = Math.hypot(tx, ty, tz);\n1305\t      if (tl > 1e-4) {\n1306\t        tx /= tl; ty /= tl; tz /= tl;\n1307\t        /* The sluff first: the snow the board is ploughing collapses and\n1308\t           runs down the fall line, and the board goes with it. Depth-squared,\n1309\t           so the transition at the lip is untouched and the deepest snow is\n1310\t           where the whole of it arrives. */\n1311\t        const depth = this.offPiste * this.offPiste;\n1312\t        const sluff = RIDER.wallSluff * depth * dt;\n1313\t        vel.x -= tx * sluff;\n1314\t        vel.y -= ty * sluff;\n1315\t        vel.z -= tz * sluff;\n1316\t\n1317\t        const up = vel.x * tx + vel.y * ty + vel.z * tz;\n1318\t        if (up > 0) {\n1319\t          /* The share of the climb that survives a step. At the lip it is\n1320\t             nearly all of it, so the transition still rides exactly as it\n1321\t             did; at full depth it is none, which is the invariant — deep\n1322\t             snow does not merely slow a climb, it refuses to support one.\n1323\t             A carve pointed at the wall therefore re-creates the uphill\n1324\t             component every step and has it taken away again every step,\n1325\t             which is what ploughing looks like from inside. */\n1326\t          const hold = Math.exp(-RIDER.wallGrab * dt)\n1327\t            * (1 - this.offPiste * this.offPiste);\n1328\t          const k = 1 - hold;\n1329\t          vel.x -= tx * up * k;\n1330\t          vel.y -= ty * up * k;\n1331\t          vel.z -= tz * up * k;\n1332\t        }\n1333\t      }\n1334\t    }\n1335\t\n1336\t    // Charging an ollie loads the legs; releasing it unloads them\n1337\t    if (input.jump) {\n1338\t      this.charging = true;\n1339\t      this.charge = Math.min(1, this.charge + dt / RIDER.chargeTime);\n1340\t      this.compressionVel += 5.5 * dt * 60 * 0.016;\n1341\t    }\n1342\t\n1343\t    /* A low-speed rider who has already committed to the final approach of a\n1344\t       lip must not be parked there by the uphill gravity and snow scrub.\n1345\t       Preserve only the speed they carried into this step — never add speed —\n1346\t       until the support ends. The resulting weak hop is still weak; it just\n1347\t       happens instead of becoming a permanent edge trap. */\n1348\t    const lipSpeed = vel.length();\n1349\t    if (this._contact.edgeAhead && enteringSpeed < RIDER.lipCommitSpeed\n1350\t      && lipSpeed > 1e-5 && lipSpeed < enteringSpeed) {\n1351\t      vel.multiplyScalar(enteringSpeed / lipSpeed);\n1352\t    }\n1353\t\n1354\t    /* --- does the hill fall away faster than gravity can follow it? -----\n1355\t\n1356\t       The whole jump system, and no jump system at all. The rider's\n1357\t       ballistic path is projected ninety milliseconds forward and compared\n1358\t       against where the ground will actually be at that point. If the hill\n1359\t       has dropped out from under it by more than a boot's depth, the rider\n1360\t       is airborne — with whatever vertical speed the ground was handing\n1361\t       them at that instant.\n1362\t\n1363\t       The horizon is what makes it work. Comparing the ground's local\n1364\t       curvature against gravity, which is the obvious way to write this,\n1365\t       cannot tell a kicker's lip from the six-metre chatter octave: at\n1366\t       150 km/h both curve away far harder than gravity, and the rider\n1367\t       spends the run flickering in and out of the air a dozen times a\n1368\t       second. Sampling a fixed distance ahead answers the question that\n1369\t       actually matters — will I still be off the ground when it counts. */\n1370\t    // Ground velocity was rebuilt from the local contact tangent above, so\n1371\t    // its vertical component is the velocity that will actually be released.\n1372\t    // The predictor is deliberately more conservative: a trailing directional\n1373\t    // chord cannot peek over the discontinuous lip or let a centred normal's\n1374\t    // forward half make a convex ramp look steeper than it has yet become.\n1375\t    const surfaceVy = vel.y;\n1376\t    const horizontalSpeed = Math.hypot(vel.x, vel.z);\n1377\t    let predictorVy = surfaceVy;\n1378\t    if (horizontalSpeed > 0.25) {\n1379\t      const fx = vel.x / horizontalSpeed;\n1380\t      const fz = vel.z / horizontalSpeed;\n1381\t      const here = this.world.height(pos.x, pos.z);\n1382\t      const behind = this.world.height(\n1383\t        pos.x - fx * CONTACT_EPS, pos.z - fz * CONTACT_EPS,\n1384\t      );\n1385\t      predictorVy = ((here - behind) / CONTACT_EPS) * horizontalSpeed;\n1386\t    }\n1387\t\n1388\t    /* …and it has to be asked about the whole horizon, not just its far end.\n1389\t\n1390\t       Sampling one point ninety milliseconds ahead was the bug that made a\n1391\t       fast run jump *worse* than a slow one, and it is worth spelling out\n1392\t       because the symptom pointed the other way. Ninety milliseconds at 45\n1393\t       m/s is four metres, which is further than a small kicker is long — so\n1394\t       the moment the rider touched the ramp's foot, the single sample was\n1395\t       already past the lip and looking at ground that had dropped away, and\n1396\t       the rider was launched from the bottom of the ramp having climbed none\n1397\t       of it. The faster they arrived, the further ahead the sample looked,\n1398\t       the earlier they were thrown, and the less of the ramp they got to\n1399\t       use: at 160 km/h the launch fired three metres before the lip with"}]},"uuid":"202a8101-adcd-46be-af43-6909a1b0e72f","timestamp":"2026-09-24T19:54:29.949Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen/js/rider.js","content":"       what keeps the handling honest; below it, a rider who has stopped is\n       allowed to stay pointed wherever they stopped pointing.\n\n       AND IT IS MEASURED ON THE SNOW, which is the whole correctness of it.\n\n       The error used to be `atan2(vel.x, -vel.z)` against `this.yaw` — a\n       bearing taken in the world horizontal plane against a bearing taken in\n       the world horizontal plane, which sounds like it must be right and is\n       not. The velocity this rule reads is rebuilt further down as `h·vFwd`,\n       and `h` is the board's heading *projected onto the slope*: on any\n       cross-slope that projection comes out at a different compass bearing\n       from the raw yaw it was built from. So the rule saw a steering error\n       that was nothing but the projection, rotated the board to remove it,\n       and thereby tilted the heading further — which made the projection\n       difference bigger on the next step. Positive feedback, on a rider\n       touching nothing: measured on the opening pitch, the board came round\n       at up to eleven degrees a second and a hands-off run left the twenty-\n       metre corridor inside five seconds and was a hundred and thirty metres\n       out at half a minute, on every seed tried.\n\n       `atan2(vLat, vFwd)` is the same question asked in the board's own\n       tangent frame — the slip angle, the thing a rider actually feels — and\n       a pure projection difference is invisible to it. A real wash-out still\n       has real lateral velocity, so the board still comes back into line\n       after a nudge, which is what the rule is for. */\n    if (!brakeActive && Math.abs(input.turn) < 0.05 && speed > 3.5) {\n      // In switch the direction of travel is the board's tail, so both\n      // components flip and the slip angle is the same small number.\n      const stance = this.switchStance ? -1 : 1;\n      const slip = Math.atan2(vLat * stance, vFwd * stance);\n      this.yaw += slip * (1 - Math.exp(-RIDER.selfCentre * dt));\n    } else if (!brakeActive && Math.abs(input.turn) < 0.05 && speed < RIDER.fallLineSpeed) {\n      /* …and below that, towards the fall line rather than towards the\n         direction of travel, because a rider who has almost stopped has no\n         meaningful direction of travel to line up with.\n\n         This is the other half of not getting stuck. The board comes to rest\n         pointing across the hill — that is where a carve leaves it — and the\n         velocity it would otherwise take its heading from is a few\n         centimetres a second of nothing. Easing towards downhill is what a\n         rider does with their back foot when they have stopped on a slope and\n         want to be moving again, and without it the run can end in a place\n         the player cannot get out of, which nothing in this game is allowed\n         to do.\n\n         AND IT HAS TO ASK FOR A DIRECTION THE COURSE RULE WILL GRANT, or the\n         two of them cancel and it is the trap it was written to prevent.\n\n         The fall line is a fact about the ground and it does not care about\n         the course. On a bank steep enough across the run, it points further\n         round than `courseLimit` allows the board to be — so this rule swung\n         the board towards it, `limitCourseYaw` swung it straight back the\n         same amount on the same step, and the net rotation was zero. The\n         rider stayed pointed up the hill with gravity taking away what little\n         speed the skate strokes returned. Measured on one seed: twenty-six\n         seconds motionless at a dead stop, rear foot still working, and no\n         way out of it — the exact failure described above, reached by a\n         different road.\n\n         So the target is the nearest legal downhill: the true fall line if\n         the course rule would allow it, and the edge of the cone it permits\n         if not. Both rules then want the same thing and the board turns. */\n      const dl = Math.hypot(n.x, n.z);\n      if (dl > 1e-4) {\n        const flow = this.courseFrame(n);\n        const courseYaw = Math.atan2(flow.x, -flow.z);\n        const rawFall = Math.atan2(n.x / dl, -(n.z / dl));\n        const legalFall = courseYaw + clamp(\n          wrapPi(rawFall - courseYaw), -RIDER.courseLimit, RIDER.courseLimit,\n        );\n        const fallYaw = legalFall + (this.switchStance ? Math.PI : 0);\n        const swing = wrapPi(fallYaw - this.yaw) * (1 - Math.exp(-1.8 * dt));\n        this.yaw += swing;\n        /* …and whatever speed is left comes round with the board.\n\n           Rotating the board without the velocity is what \"he sometimes\n           drives backwards\" actually was. At the old threshold this began at\n           twelve km/h — still genuinely moving — so the board swung towards\n           the fall line while the momentum carried on the way it was already\n           going, and the rider ended up travelling one way at a hundred and\n           fifty degrees to the way he was pointing. Turning the velocity by\n           the same angle keeps the two together: he pivots, rather than\n           sliding backwards while facing downhill. It is safe to do at this\n           speed and only at this speed, because there is almost no momentum\n           left to falsify. */\n        const cs = Math.cos(swing);\n        const sn = Math.sin(swing);\n        const vx = vel.x;\n        const vz = vel.z;\n        /* In this file's yaw convention (yaw = atan2(vx, -vz)) a +swing on\n           the heading is vx' = vx·cos − vz·sin, vz' = vx·sin + vz·cos. The\n           signs were flipped, so the momentum pivoted *against* the board —\n           doubling the exact divergence this rotation exists to remove. */\n        vel.x = vx * cs - vz * sn;\n        vel.z = vx * sn + vz * cs;\n      }\n    }\n\n    /* AND THE BOARD DOES NOT HOLD A LINE IN DEEP SNOW.\n\n       This is the term that finally contains the boundary mountains, and it\n       took three attempts to find because the first two were answers to the\n       wrong question. Refusing the climb and taking the edge away both stop a\n       rider *gaining* height, and neither stops the thing that was actually\n       happening: a board held on a straight line along the bank keeps its\n       altitude while the run underneath it descends nearly a third of a metre\n       for every metre of z. Six seconds of that is sixty metres up the wall,\n       and not one centimetre of it was climbed.\n\n       What is missing from that picture is that the board is a metre and a\n       half of stiff plank sitting *in* the snow rather than on it. Pointed\n       across the fall line it has a metre of buried edge on the uphill side\n       and nothing under the downhill one; it does not hold, it pivots — which\n       is the first thing anybody learns the hard way about traversing deep\n       snow. So the yaw is eased towards the local fall line at a rate set by\n       how deep the board is, before the skid clamp below arbitrates between\n       that and the direction of travel, and the ordinary carve then brings\n       the velocity round behind it. On the containment wall the fall line\n       points home, so this is a rider being turned downhill by the snow\n       rather than a rider being pushed anywhere by the game.\n\n       Nothing here touches the quarterpipe: `offPiste` is zero everywhere\n       inside the lip, so a wall ride is still steered by the player alone. */\n    if (this.offPiste > 0 && speed > 1) {\n      // A perfectly level patch has no fall line to be turned towards.\n      if (Math.hypot(n.x, n.z) > 1e-4) {\n        const fallYaw = Math.atan2(n.x, -n.z)\n          + (this.switchStance ? Math.PI : 0);\n        this.yaw += wrapPi(fallYaw - this.yaw)\n          * (1 - Math.exp(-RIDER.wallPivot * this.offPiste * dt));\n      }\n    }\n\n    /* And a hard limit on how far the board is ever allowed to get from the\n       direction it is actually travelling.\n\n       This is the fix for the board ending up sideways. Nothing in the model\n       forbids it on its own: the yaw is driven by the carve and the velocity\n       is driven by the grip, and any moment those two disagree — a wash-out,\n       a graze, a bad landing, a brake held through a compression — the gap\n       between them opens and there is no term that closes it again except the\n       self-centring, which is deliberately gentle and switches off the instant\n       the rider touches the steering. So a player holding an edge through a\n       slide could end up travelling one way and pointing ninety degrees off\n       it, which is not a snowboard, it is a sledge.\n\n       Sixty degrees is about as far across their travel as anyone can hold a\n       board and still be riding it; past that the excess is taken back\n       immediately rather than smoothly, because there is no physical process\n       that would let it persist. A brake gets almost eighty-two degrees —\n       enough to throw a wall of snow and stop — but not enough to cross over\n       and silently change which end of the board is leading. */\n    /* …and a press is exactly the thing that rule is describing the absence\n       of. Sixty degrees is how far a board with both contact points buried can\n       be held off its own travel; a board standing on its nose has no tail in\n       the snow to hold anything, which is why a butter works at all. So the\n       clamp opens with the pressure rather than being suspended by it, and a\n       half-hearted press still cannot spin.\n\n       Passing ninety degrees is not a wider skid, it is a change of stance:\n       the end of the board that was leading is now trailing, and from there\n       the rider is riding switch. Flipping the reference at that crossing is\n       also what makes the clamp hysteretic — the error jumps from just over a\n       right angle to just under one and cannot flap. */\n    // The same ground-speed gate as the brake pivot above, and for the same\n    // reason: the skid clamp must not measure stance against a bearing that\n    // does not exist.\n    if (Math.hypot(vel.x, vel.z) > 0.6) {\n      const travelYaw = Math.atan2(vel.x, -vel.z);\n      let stanceYaw = travelYaw + (this.switchStance ? Math.PI : 0);\n      let off = wrapPi(this.yaw - stanceYaw);\n      if (this.press > 0.02 && Math.abs(off) > Math.PI / 2) {\n        this.switchStance = !this.switchStance;\n        stanceYaw = travelYaw + (this.switchStance ? Math.PI : 0);\n        off = wrapPi(this.yaw - stanceYaw);\n      }\n      const limit = this.press > 0.02\n        ? RIDER.maxSkid + (RIDER.pressSkid - RIDER.maxSkid) * this.press\n        : brakeActive ? RIDER.brakeSkid : RIDER.maxSkid;\n      /* Bounded correction rather than a hard subtract. Steering grows the\n         excess by less than `skidRecover·dt` a step, so in continuous play\n         this binds identically — but when the LIMIT itself drops in one\n         input frame (a butter released, a brake let go) the board now swings\n         back over a few frames instead of teleporting to legal. */\n      const maxCorr = RIDER.skidRecover * dt;\n      if (off > limit) this.yaw -= Math.min(off - limit, maxCorr);\n      else if (off < -limit) this.yaw -= Math.max(off + limit, -maxCorr);\n    }\n\n    // A carve can cross almost the entire hill, but it cannot become a U-turn.\n    // Killing the stored lip rate when the limit catches also prevents the\n    // invisible excess rotation reappearing on the next jump.\n    /* A press opens this one too, and it is safe to open because of what it\n       limits. This rule is about the *board*: it stops ordinary steering from\n       pointing the rider back up the mountain. What keeps the run going\n       downhill is `limitCourseVelocity` below, and that is untouched — so\n       during a butter the board may swing past the course while the momentum\n       carries straight on, which is exactly the trick and nothing else. */\n    const courseLimit = this.press > 0.02\n      ? RIDER.courseLimit + (RIDER.pressSkid - RIDER.courseLimit) * this.press\n      : brakeActive ? RIDER.brakeCourseLimit : RIDER.courseLimit;\n    if (this.limitCourseYaw(n, courseLimit, dt)) this.spinVel = 0;\n\n    /* What the press actually carried round, booked once and here.\n       `stepYaw` is from before any of the rotation rules ran and this is\n       after all of them, so what is counted is the rotation that survived —\n       a butter cannot be paid for a spin the skid clamp took straight back\n       off it, and it cannot be paid twice for the same radians either. */\n    if (this.press > 0.02) this.pressSpin += wrapPi(this.yaw - stepYaw);\n\n    /* What the edge could not hold becomes sideways speed — the wash-out —\n       and what it did hold is scrubbed out of the lateral velocity, which is\n       what \"the edge is gripping\" physically means. `carveLoad` is then the\n       honest fraction of the available grip the turn is using, so everything\n       downstream that reads it — the spray, the leg spring, the trail's\n       width, the camera's shake — is reading how hard the board is actually\n       working rather than how hard the key is pressed. */\n    vLat -= side * spill * dt;\n    const bite = gripNow * dt;\n    const held = clamp(vLat, -bite, bite);\n    vLat -= held;\n    this.slide = Math.abs(vLat);\n    this.lateral = vLat;\n    this.carveLoad = clamp(carve / gripNow, 0, 1);\n\n    // Redirecting momentum costs a little; failing to costs a lot. These are\n    // losses from the magnitude of forward speed, not pushes towards the\n    // board's tail: switch riding has negative vFwd, so subtracting them\n    // directly would accelerate it.\n    /* THE ONE THING A PRESS HAS TO BE PROTECTED FROM, which is this model's\n       own idea of what a sideways board means.\n\n       `vFwd` and `vLat` are the velocity written in the board's frame, so the\n       moment the board pivots away from its travel the whole of the momentum\n       is booked as lateral — and lateral is what the scrub and the damping\n       below exist to destroy, because everywhere else in this game a board\n       across its own travel is a wash-out or a speed check and destroying it\n       is the entire point. Measured: a butter held for a second and a bit\n       took ninety-four km/h down to nine, which is not a trick, it is a wall.\n\n       A press is the one case where that reading is wrong. The board has\n       pivoted but it has not dug in — one end is out of the snow, the base\n       is still flat on it, and the rider is still going the way they were\n       going. So both terms are scaled out with the pressure. What is left is\n       the plough below, which is the honest cost and is a fifth of the size. */\n    const pressGlide = 1 - 0.86 * this.press;\n    let forwardLoss = Math.abs(held) * RIDER.carveDrag\n      + this.slide * RIDER.slideScrub * pressGlide * dt;\n    /* And cutting the trench is not free either. A carved edge is slicing\n       through snow rather than gliding over it, and the further it is rolled\n       over the more of it is buried — which is why a run held on a hard edge\n       the whole way down a pitch arrives slower than one that let the board\n       run flat between turns. That trade is most of what makes a line a\n       decision rather than a shape. */\n    forwardLoss += RIDER.edgeDrag * sinEdge * this.carveLoad * dt;\n\n    // …and a board standing on one end of itself is ploughing with that end.\n    // A butter is a slow trick everywhere it has ever been done, and this is\n    // the whole of why: there is nothing gliding.\n    forwardLoss += RIDER.pressDrag * this.press * dt;\n\n    /* And climbing costs more than gravity alone charges for.\n\n       g·sin θ is the whole of the real physics and, on a hill this shallow,\n       it is not very much — five or six m/s² on the average pitch. What it\n       misses is that a board driven up a slope is also being driven *into*\n       it, and an edge loaded like that in soft snow scrubs. This is that\n       term, and it is the difference between a quarterpipe wall you have to\n       commit speed to and a free way to change lanes. */\n    if (this.climbRate > 0 && speed > 1) {\n      forwardLoss += RIDER.climbScrub * clamp(this.climbRate / speed, 0, 1) * dt;\n    }\n    /* …and out past the lip there is no piste under the board at all. Deep\n       unconsolidated snow does not scrub a board driven up it, it swallows\n       it: the base ploughs instead of gliding. Charged against the climb for\n       the reason given beside `RIDER.wallDrag` — a flat term big enough to\n       stop a fast rider also strands a slow one. */\n    if (this.offPiste > 0 && this.climbRate > 0 && speed > 1) {\n      forwardLoss += RIDER.wallDrag * this.offPiste * this.offPiste\n        * clamp(this.climbRate / speed, 0, 1) * dt;\n    }\n    const forwardSign = Math.sign(vFwd) || (this.switchStance ? -1 : 1);\n    vFwd = forwardSign * Math.max(0, Math.abs(vFwd) - forwardLoss);\n\n    // A deliberate braking sideslip remains broad and controllable; an\n    // accidental wash catches its edge and aligns much faster. Kinetic base\n    // friction below supplies the rest of the stop.\n    const slideDamping = (2.6\n      + (RIDER.brakeSlideDamping - 2.6) * braking) * pressGlide;\n    vLat *= Math.exp(-slideDamping * dt);\n\n    // Pressure progressively moves from a waxed base to an edged speed check.\n    const mu = RIDER.friction\n      + (RIDER.brakeFriction - RIDER.friction) * braking;\n    /* Deep snow is not a texture, it is work. Powder was 1.15x a groomed\n       run, which at any speed worth having is indistinguishable from the\n       piste; it ploughs properly now, and the corduroy is a touch slicker\n       than neutral so the ribbon actually rewards being on it. */\n    const matDrag = surf.groomed * 0.88 + surf.powder * 2.30\n      + surf.ice * 0.68 + surf.rock * 2.05;\n    const dec = mu * (this.world.surfaceDrag ?? 1) * matDrag\n      * RIDER.gravity * Math.max(0.2, n.y);\n    // The initial tail kick is already cutting snow before the board has\n    // reached full transverse velocity; afterwards real vLat dominates this.\n    this.slide += speed * 0.16 * braking;\n\n    vel.copy(h).multiplyScalar(vFwd).addScaledVector(r, vLat);\n\n    // Kinetic friction, then aerodynamic drag. A powered tuck bypasses the\n    // latter entirely; its speed floor is applied after the board has been\n    // brought back into a physically valid direction below.\n    const sp = vel.length();\n    if (sp > 1e-4) vel.multiplyScalar(Math.max(0, sp - dec * dt) / sp);\n    if (!this.tucking) {\n      const over = Math.max(0, sp - RIDER.dragKnee);\n      const drag = RIDER.drag * (1 + over * 0.35);\n      // Stable integration of dv/dt = -drag·v². Unlike the former Euler\n      // factor, this cannot turn negative and erase all momentum in one frame\n      // when W is released after an extreme-speed run.\n      vel.multiplyScalar(1 / (1 + drag * sp * dt));\n    }\n\n    /* One impulse per planted rear-foot stroke.\n\n       The phase is simulation state rather than an animation clock: the\n       powder puff, the boot plant and the speed gain therefore happen on the\n       same fixed 120 Hz step. The direction blends the board's legal leading\n       end with the generated course, so skating can free a stopped traverse\n       without ever pushing the rider uphill or tail-first. */\n    if (this.pushing) {\n      const before = this.pushPhase;\n      const advanced = before + RIDER.pushCadence * dt;\n      const wrapped = advanced >= 1;\n      this.pushPhase = advanced - Math.floor(advanced);\n\n      if (before < RIDER.pushPlant && advanced >= RIDER.pushPlant) {\n        const flow = this.courseFrame(n);\n        const stroke = this._g.set(Math.sin(this.yaw), 0, -Math.cos(this.yaw));\n        stroke.addScaledVector(n, -stroke.dot(n)).normalize();\n        if (this.switchStance) stroke.multiplyScalar(-1);\n        stroke.multiplyScalar(0.65).add(flow).normalize();\n        vel.addScaledVector(stroke, RIDER.pushImpulse);\n        this.pushStroke += 1;\n        this.emit('push', RIDER.pushImpulse, this.pushStroke);\n      }\n\n      if (wrapped && finishPush) {\n        this.pushing = false;\n        this.pushPhase = 0;\n      }\n    }\n\n    // Gravity, a collision or a severe wash-out may have arrived from outside\n    // the board frame. Keep those exceptional vectors in the forward half of\n    // the generated course as well.\n    this.limitCourseVelocity(vel, n, courseLimit);\n\n    // The two forward constraints above still allow opposite traverses: both\n    // can be down-course while one is tail-first relative to the board. Remove\n    // only that impossible component. A mismatched near-stop becomes a clean\n    // sideways skid or a stop, never a brief backwards ride.\n    const boardTravel = this._p.set(Math.sin(this.yaw), 0, -Math.cos(this.yaw));\n    boardTravel.addScaledVector(n, -boardTravel.dot(n)).normalize();\n    if (this.switchStance) boardTravel.multiplyScalar(-1);\n    const tailFirst = vel.dot(boardTravel);\n    if (tailFirst < 0) vel.addScaledVector(boardTravel, -tailFirst);\n\n    /* Guarantee continuous acceleration for every grounded step W is held.\n\n       Applying this after course and stance correction matters at very high\n       speed: it preserves the direction produced by the snow instead of\n       letting a forward impulse slowly steer the rider. Scaling also keeps\n       the rule exact through arbitrarily large finite speeds; there is no\n       target, curve, or clamp for it to asymptotically approach. */\n    if (poweredSpeed > 0) {\n      const drivenSpeed = vel.length();\n      if (drivenSpeed > 1e-5) {\n        if (drivenSpeed < poweredSpeed) vel.multiplyScalar(poweredSpeed / drivenSpeed);\n      } else {\n        vel.copy(boardTravel).multiplyScalar(poweredSpeed);\n      }\n    }\n\n    /* THE ONE THING THE BOUNDARY MOUNTAINS ARE NOT ALLOWED TO PERMIT.\n\n       Everything above is a cost, and full flow carries enough speed to pay\n       any finite amount tuned for ordinary riding. This is the invariant, and it\n       has to live *after* the powered floor for exactly that reason — the\n       floor is the last thing that writes a speed, so anything hoping to\n       bound the climb has to come after it.\n\n       What it removes is only the component of travel pointing up the\n       fall line, and only in proportion to how deep into the unpisted snow\n       the board is. Downhill motion, the traverse, a slide along the bank —\n       all untouched. `t` is the steepest-ascent direction in the surface's\n       own tangent plane, which is world up with the surface normal projected\n       out of it, so the scrub stays on the snow rather than fighting the\n       ground contact. On the run itself `offPiste` is zero and none of this\n       executes. */\n    if (this.offPiste > 0) {\n      const ny = n.y;\n      let tx = -n.x * ny;\n      let ty = 1 - ny * ny;\n      let tz = -n.z * ny;\n      const tl = Math.hypot(tx, ty, tz);\n      if (tl > 1e-4) {\n        tx /= tl; ty /= tl; tz /= tl;\n        /* The sluff first: the snow the board is ploughing collapses and\n           runs down the fall line, and the board goes with it. Depth-squared,\n           so the transition at the lip is untouched and the deepest snow is\n           where the whole of it arrives. */\n        const depth = this.offPiste * this.offPiste;\n        const sluff = RIDER.wallSluff * depth * dt;\n        vel.x -= tx * sluff;\n        vel.y -= ty * sluff;\n        vel.z -= tz * sluff;\n\n        const up = vel.x * tx + vel.y * ty + vel.z * tz;\n        if (up > 0) {\n          /* The share of the climb that survives a step. At the lip it is\n             nearly all of it, so the transition still rides exactly as it\n             did; at full depth it is none, which is the invariant — deep\n             snow does not merely slow a climb, it refuses to support one.\n             A carve pointed at the wall therefore re-creates the uphill\n             component every step and has it taken away again every step,\n             which is what ploughing looks like from inside. */\n          const hold = Math.exp(-RIDER.wallGrab * dt)\n            * (1 - this.offPiste * this.offPiste);\n          const k = 1 - hold;\n          vel.x -= tx * up * k;\n          vel.y -= ty * up * k;\n          vel.z -= tz * up * k;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    // Charging an ollie loads the legs; releasing it unloads them\n    if (input.jump) {\n      this.charging = true;\n      this.charge = Math.min(1, this.charge + dt / RIDER.chargeTime);\n      this.compressionVel += 5.5 * dt * 60 * 0.016;\n    }\n\n    /* A low-speed rider who has already committed to the final approach of a\n       lip must not be parked there by the uphill gravity and snow scrub.\n       Preserve only the speed they carried into this step — never add speed —\n       until the support ends. The resulting weak hop is still weak; it just\n       happens instead of becoming a permanent edge trap. */\n    const lipSpeed = vel.length();\n    if (this._contact.edgeAhead && enteringSpeed < RIDER.lipCommitSpeed\n      && lipSpeed > 1e-5 && lipSpeed < enteringSpeed) {\n      vel.multiplyScalar(enteringSpeed / lipSpeed);\n    }\n\n    /* --- does the hill fall away faster than gravity can follow it? -----\n\n       The whole jump system, and no jump system at all. The rider's\n       ballistic path is projected ninety milliseconds forward and compared\n       against where the ground will actually be at that point. If the hill\n       has dropped out from under it by more than a boot's depth, the rider\n       is airborne — with whatever vertical speed the ground was handing\n       them at that instant.\n\n       The horizon is what makes it work. Comparing the ground's local\n       curvature against gravity, which is the obvious way to write this,\n       cannot tell a kicker's lip from the six-metre chatter octave: at\n       150 km/h both curve away far harder than gravity, and the rider\n       spends the run flickering in and out of the air a dozen times a\n       second. Sampling a fixed distance ahead answers the question that\n       actually matters — will I still be off the ground when it counts. */\n    // Ground velocity was rebuilt from the local contact tangent above, so\n    // its vertical component is the velocity that will actually be released.\n    // The predictor is deliberately more conservative: a trailing directional\n    // chord cannot peek over the discontinuous lip or let a centred normal's\n    // forward half make a convex ramp look steeper than it has yet become.\n    const surfaceVy = vel.y;\n    const horizontalSpeed = Math.hypot(vel.x, vel.z);\n    let predictorVy = surfaceVy;\n    if (horizontalSpeed > 0.25) {\n      const fx = vel.x / horizontalSpeed;\n      const fz = vel.z / horizontalSpeed;\n      const here = this.world.height(pos.x, pos.z);\n      const behind = this.world.height(\n        pos.x - fx * CONTACT_EPS, pos.z - fz * CONTACT_EPS,\n      );\n      predictorVy = ((here - behind) / CONTACT_EPS) * horizontalSpeed;\n    }\n\n    /* …and it has to be asked about the whole horizon, not just its far end.\n\n       Sampling one point ninety milliseconds ahead was the bug that made a\n       fast run jump *worse* than a slow one, and it is worth spelling out\n       because the symptom pointed the other way. Ninety milliseconds at 45\n       m/s is four metres, which is further than a small kicker is long — so\n       the moment the rider touched the ramp's foot, the single sample was\n       already past the lip and looking at ground that had dropped away, and\n       the rider was launched from the bottom of the ramp having climbed none\n       of it. The faster they arrived, the further ahead the sample looked,\n       the earlier they were thrown, and the less of the ramp they got to\n       use: at 160 km/h the launch fired three metres before the lip with","numLines":500,"startLine":900,"totalLines":2415}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"318da5d9-606e-4f0a-982f-69c5abf648bb","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen","sessionId":"f31fbc8b-fac8-4283-8f36-bb5424b19347","version":"2.1.281","gitBranch":"main","slug":"enchanted-prancing-stroustrup"}
