{"parentUuid":"8928ce05-746d-4ac7-804c-c12903641e84","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c41cd6de-57c3-441a-96f7-ed621873bfe7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GLWXQYJMackmh4qaYZwToE","type":"tool_result","content":"1919\t    const landedVel = this._p.copy(vel).addScaledVector(n, -vel.dot(n));\n1920\t    this.limitCourseVelocity(landedVel, n, RIDER.courseLimit);\n1921\t    const travelYaw = landedVel.lengthSq() > 1e-5\n1922\t      ? Math.atan2(landedVel.x, -landedVel.z)\n1923\t      : Math.atan2(this._flow.x, -this._flow.z);\n1924\t    const off = wrapPi(this.yaw - travelYaw);\n1925\t    const forward = Math.abs(off);\n1926\t    const backward = Math.abs(Math.abs(off) - Math.PI);\n1927\t    const spinErr = Math.min(forward, backward);\n1928\t    const isSwitch = backward < forward;\n1929\t    const flipErr = Math.abs(wrapPi(this.flip));\n1930\t\n1931\t    // A short hop is not a trick, but it is still a physical landing. Trick\n1932\t    // eligibility gates only the rotation tests; impact severity always\n1933\t    // applies, so a hard, short drop cannot be rewritten as CLEAN.\n1934\t    const judged = this.airTime >= RIDER.minJudgedAir\n1935\t      || Math.abs(this.spinAccum) > 0.8 || Math.abs(this.flipAccum) > 0.8;\n1936\t    const w = RIDER.landWindow;\n1937\t    const p = RIDER.landPitchWindow;\n1938\t    let verdict = CLEAN;\n1939\t    /* A sideways board costs the landing, not the whole run.\n1940\t\n1941\t       Because the nearest regular/switch stance is used above, `spinErr` can\n1942\t       only ever reach ninety degrees. The old bail threshold began at eighty-\n1943\t       two, leaving an arbitrary eight-degree sliver where steering through an\n1944\t       unnoticed natural hop caused a wipeout while a nearly identical landing\n1945\t       was merely sketchy. Keep yaw mismatch in the sketchy branch below; hard\n1946\t       impact and a rider arriving upside-down remain genuine bails. */\n1947\t    if (impact > RIDER.hardImpact\n1948\t      || (judged && flipErr > p * 1.6)) {\n1949\t      verdict = BAIL;\n1950\t    } else if (impact > RIDER.softImpact * 1.9\n1951\t      || (judged && (spinErr > w || flipErr > p))) {\n1952\t      verdict = SKETCHY;\n1953\t    }\n1954\t\n1955\t    // The grab has to be let go before the snow, which is what the start\n1956\t    // screen says. Held through touchdown it was free points and a strictly\n1957\t    // dominant strategy. It costs a sketchy landing rather than a fall,\n1958\t    // because nothing here should end a run.\n1959\t    if (judged && this.grabbing) verdict = Math.max(verdict, SKETCHY);\n1960\t\n1961\t    // Rotations are quantised to the *nearest* half turn, not the floor.\n1962\t    // The landing window has already accepted anything inside ±53°, so a\n1963\t    // clean 170 is a 180 and a clean 350° flip is a backflip. The label and\n1964\t    // the score both read these, so they can never disagree.\n1965\t    const halfTurns = Math.round(Math.abs(this.spinAccum) / Math.PI);\n1966\t    const flipTurns = Math.round(Math.abs(this.flipAccum) / TAU);\n1967\t\n1968\t    const summary = {\n1969\t      verdict,\n1970\t      airTime: this.airTime,\n1971\t      spin: this.spinAccum,\n1972\t      flips: this.flipAccum,\n1973\t      halfTurns,\n1974\t      flipTurns,\n1975\t      grabTime: this.grabTime,\n1976\t      grabKind: this.grabKind,\n1977\t      switchStance: isSwitch,\n1978\t      impact,\n1979\t      judged,\n1980\t      lipPop: this.lipPop,\n1981\t      takeoffSpeed: this.takeoffSpeed,\n1982\t    };\n1983\t\n1984\t    this.grounded = true;\n1985\t    this.state = 'ride';\n1986\t    this.extension = 0;\n1987\t    // The pitch the landing forgives, kept for the renderer. The yaw snap\n1988\t    // below hands its residual to `yawGlide`; the flip's residual — up to\n1989\t    // ~78° on the sketchiest landed flip — was zeroed in one write, and the\n1990\t    // board's nose visibly teleported level. Same cure, same drain.\n1991\t    const flipResidual = wrapPi(this.flip);\n1992\t    this.flip = 0;\n1993\t    this.flipVel = 0;\n1994\t    this.spinVel = 0;\n1995\t    this.grab = 0;\n1996\t    this.grabTime = 0;\n1997\t    this.grabbing = false;\n1998\t    this.compressionVel += Math.min(impact, 30) * 0.85;\n1999\t\n2000\t    if (verdict === BAIL) {\n2001\t      this.fall('land', impact);\n2002\t    } else {\n2003\t      /* Snap the board straight — forward or switch, whichever was closer —\n2004\t         but only if this was actually a jump.\n2005\t\n2006\t         Doing it on every touchdown is what made the controls feel haunted.\n2007\t         The mountain is covered in rollers and knolls, so a rider carving\n2008\t         across it is leaving the ground and returning several times a\n2009\t         second, and each of those hops was rewriting the yaw to point wherever\n2010\t         the *velocity* happened to be going. From the player's hands that is\n2011\t         indistinguishable from the game steering for them at random, and it\n2012\t         fought hardest exactly when they were holding the tightest carve —\n2013\t         which is when the board is furthest from the direction of travel and\n2014\t         so had the most to be snapped away. `judged` is already the test for\n2015\t         \"this was a trick rather than chatter\", and it is the right test\n2016\t         here too. */\n2017\t      if (judged) {\n2018\t        /* The physics squares the board in one write — judging and the next\n2019\t           carve both need the aligned stance now — but the few degrees the\n2020\t           landing assist did not close are handed to `yawGlide`, which the\n2021\t           renderer subtracts and drains over a tenth of a second. The same\n2022\t           landing, minus the one-frame twitch. */\n2023\t        const snapped = isSwitch ? travelYaw + Math.PI : travelYaw;\n2024\t        this.yawGlide = clamp(wrapPi(this.yaw - snapped), -1.1, 1.1);\n2025\t        this.flipGlide = clamp(flipResidual, -1.4, 1.4);\n2026\t        this.yaw = snapped;\n2027\t        this.switchStance = isSwitch;\n2028\t      }\n2029\t      // A sketchy landing wobbles and costs speed; a clean one costs nothing\n2030\t      // The snow takes the normal component; the rider keeps the tangential\n2031\t      // run whose direction was used to classify the landing above.\n2032\t      vel.copy(landedVel);\n2033\t      if (verdict === SKETCHY) vel.multiplyScalar(0.86);\n2034\t      // …but not all of it. A long drop converts a lot of height into speed\n2035\t      // along the hill, and with nothing taken back a rider could ride one\n2036\t      // kicker into 270 km/h and never slow down. Everything past the point\n2037\t      // the legs can absorb comfortably is paid for on the way out, which is\n2038\t      // also what makes a big air a decision rather than a free boost.\n2039\t      const excess = Math.max(0, impact - RIDER.softImpact);\n2040\t      if (excess > 0) vel.multiplyScalar(1 - Math.min(0.24, excess * 0.012));\n2041\t      this.landing = summary;\n2042\t      this.emit('land', summary);\n2043\t    }\n2044\t    return summary;\n2045\t  }\n2046\t\n2047\t  /* --- going down, and getting back up -------------------------------- */\n2048\t\n2049\t  /* `into` is the speed that went into whatever caused this, in m/s. It is\n2050\t     what decides how far the rider is thrown: a bail off a bad landing hands\n2051\t     over the impact, a tree hands over the closing speed, and a spill at\n2052\t     walking pace hands over almost nothing and is over in a second. */\n2053\t  fall(cause = 'land', into = 0) {\n2054\t    if (this.state === 'fall') return;\n2055\t    this.state = 'fall';\n2056\t    this.grounded = false;\n2057\t    this.airborne = true;\n2058\t    this.fallElapsed = 0;\n2059\t    this.fallTimer = 0;\n2060\t    this.tumble = 0;\n2061\t    this.charge = 0;\n2062\t    this.charging = false;\n2063\t    this._latePopAllowed = false;\n2064\t    this._jumpBuffer = 0;\n2065\t    this.pushing = false;\n2066\t    this.grab = 0;\n2067\t    this.grabbing = false;\n2068\t    this.grabTime = 0;\n2069\t    // A butter interrupted by a tree is not a butter. Clearing rather than\n2070\t    // releasing is the difference between the trick ending and it having\n2071\t    // happened, and going down is the one way it can end without having.\n2072\t    this.press = 0;\n2073\t    this.pressTime = 0;\n2074\t    this.pressSpin = 0;\n2075\t    this.extension = 0;\n2076\t    this.bend = 0;\n2077\t    this._bendReady = false;\n2078\t    /* The flight that just ended is over, and its ledger goes with it. These\n2079\t       three survived a fall untouched, and everything that reads \"airborne\"\n2080\t       state read them as live: the HUD kept the last jump's trick name up\n2081\t       for the whole tumble, the audio played rotation blips for spins from\n2082\t       a minute ago, and the chase camera framed the wipeout as a soaring\n2083\t       air because `airTime` still held the old flight's value. */\n2084\t    this.airTime = 0;\n2085\t    this.spinAccum = 0;\n2086\t    this.flipAccum = 0;\n2087\t\n2088\t    // What the body keeps, and what the collision turns into height. A rider\n2089\t    // doing 40 m/s into a trunk leaves it doing 25 and climbing, which is\n2090\t    // most of a second in the air before the hill even gets a say.\n2091\t    this.vel.multiplyScalar(RIDER.fallLaunch);\n2092\t    const lift = Math.max(into, this.vel.length() * 0.35) * RIDER.fallLift;\n2093\t    this.vel.y += Math.min(15, lift);\n2094\t    this.pos.y = Math.max(this.pos.y, this.world.height(this.pos.x, this.pos.z) + 0.05);\n2095\t    this.compressionVel += 8;\n2096\t    // Keep the impact that caused the fall attached to the event. The crash\n2097\t    // mix, powder plume and camera response all need the same physical number\n2098\t    // that launched the body; throwing it away here made every wipeout read\n2099\t    // identically, from a walking-speed washout to a full-speed tree hit.\n2100\t    this.emit('fall', cause, into);\n2101\t  }\n2102\t\n2103\t  /* A tumble is a body, not a timer. While it is off the ground it is\n2104\t     ballistic; when it arrives it bounces, lower each time, and slides. The\n2105\t     rider is allowed up once they have actually stopped — or once `fallMax`\n2106\t     has run out, because a body that finds a hollow to oscillate in should\n2107\t     not be able to hold the run hostage. */\n2108\t  fallStep(dt) {\n2109\t    const { pos, vel } = this;\n2110\t    this.brake = approach(this.brake, 0, RIDER.brakeRelease, dt);\n2111\t\n2112\t    if (this.state === 'fall') {\n2113\t      this.fallElapsed += dt;\n2114\t      const sp = vel.length();\n2115\t      this.tumble += dt * 9 * clamp(sp / 12, 0.25, 2.2);\n2116\t      const gy = this.world.height(pos.x, pos.z);\n2117\t\n2118\t      if (this.airborne) {\n2119\t        vel.y -= RIDER.gravity * dt;\n2120\t        vel.multiplyScalar(Math.max(0, 1 - RIDER.fallDrag * dt));\n2121\t        pos.x += vel.x * dt;\n2122\t        pos.y += vel.y * dt;\n2123\t        pos.z += vel.z * dt;\n2124\t\n2125\t        const ny = this.world.height(pos.x, pos.z);\n2126\t        if (pos.y <= ny) {\n2127\t          pos.y = ny;\n2128\t          const n = normalFrom(this.world.height, pos.x, pos.z, this._n);\n2129\t          this.normal.copy(n);\n2130\t          const into = vel.dot(n);\n2131\t          if (into < 0) {\n2132\t            // Bounce, and take the rest of it out on the snow\n2133\t            vel.addScaledVector(n, -into * (1 + RIDER.fallBounce));\n2134\t            this.emit('impact', -into);\n2135\t            this.compressionVel += Math.min(-into, 24) * 0.6;\n2136\t          }\n2137\t          vel.multiplyScalar(0.72);\n2138\t          // Below a certain hop it is no longer flying, it is sliding\n2139\t          if (vel.dot(n) < 2.2) {\n2140\t            this.airborne = false;\n2141\t            vel.addScaledVector(n, -vel.dot(n));\n2142\t          }\n2143\t        }\n2144\t      } else {\n2145\t        // Still a body sliding down a hill: gravity on the tangent, and a\n2146\t        // lot more friction than a board on its edge\n2147\t        const n = normalFrom(this.world.height, pos.x, pos.z, this._n);\n2148\t        this.normal.copy(n);\n2149\t        vel.addScaledVector(n, -vel.dot(n));\n2150\t        const g = this._g.set(0, -RIDER.gravity, 0);\n2151\t        const gn = g.dot(n);\n2152\t        vel.x += (g.x - n.x * gn) * dt;\n2153\t        vel.y += (g.y - n.y * gn) * dt;\n2154\t        vel.z += (g.z - n.z * gn) * dt;\n2155\t        const s = vel.length();\n2156\t        if (s > 1e-4) vel.multiplyScalar(Math.max(0, s - RIDER.fallFriction * dt) / s);\n2157\t\n2158\t        pos.x += vel.x * dt;\n2159\t        pos.z += vel.z * dt;\n2160\t        pos.y = this.world.height(pos.x, pos.z);\n2161\t\n2162\t        // And the hill can hand them back into the air — a tumble off a\n2163\t        // cliff should not stick to the face on the way down\n2164\t        const drop = this.world.height(pos.x + vel.x * 0.09, pos.z + vel.z * 0.09);\n2165\t        if (pos.y - drop > 0.5) this.airborne = true;\n2166\t      }\n2167\t\n2168\t      this.slide = vel.length() * 0.8;\n2169\t      this.extension = Math.max(0, pos.y - gy);\n2170\t\n2171\t      const stopped = !this.airborne && vel.length() < RIDER.fallRest;\n2172\t      if ((stopped && this.fallElapsed > RIDER.fallMin) || this.fallElapsed > RIDER.fallMax) {\n2173\t        // Up on the board again, pointed downhill, with a moment of grace so\n2174\t        // the same tree cannot take you twice\n2175\t        this.state = 'rise';\n2176\t        this.grounded = true;\n2177\t        this.airborne = false;\n2178\t        this.fallTimer = RIDER.riseTime;\n2179\t        pos.y = this.world.height(pos.x, pos.z);\n2180\t        /* Always reconcile the board and its travel before the model stands\n2181\t           up. `fallMax` can expire while a fast body is still sliding, so a\n2182\t           low-speed-only repair left exactly the hardest crashes resuming\n2183\t           tail-first. Keep the nearest legal traverse and a bounded share of\n2184\t           the remaining momentum, then rebuild it on the local surface. */\n2185\t        const n = normalFrom(this.world.height, pos.x, pos.z, this._n);\n2186\t        const restart = clamp(vel.length(), 6, 12);\n2187\t        this.switchStance = false;\n2188\t        this.limitCourseYaw(n, RIDER.recoveryCourseLimit, dt);\n2189\t        const h = this._h.set(Math.sin(this.yaw), 0, -Math.cos(this.yaw));\n2190\t        h.addScaledVector(n, -h.dot(n)).normalize();\n2191\t        vel.copy(h).multiplyScalar(restart);\n2192\t        this.grace = 1.3;\n2193\t        this.emit('rise');\n2194\t      }\n2195\t    } else {\n2196\t      // Getting up. Ordinary sliding physics, and the model reads `fallTimer`\n2197\t      // counting down across the window to blend the body back upright.\n2198\t      this.fallTimer -= dt;\n2199\t      const n = normalFrom(this.world.height, pos.x, pos.z, this._n);\n2200\t      this.normal.copy(n);\n2201\t      vel.addScaledVector(n, -vel.dot(n));\n2202\t      const g = this._g.set(0, -RIDER.gravity, 0);\n2203\t      const gn = g.dot(n);\n2204\t      vel.x += (g.x - n.x * gn) * dt;\n2205\t      vel.y += (g.y - n.y * gn) * dt;\n2206\t      vel.z += (g.z - n.z * gn) * dt;\n2207\t      const s = vel.length();\n2208\t      if (s > 1e-4) vel.multiplyScalar(Math.max(0, s - 4 * dt) / s);\n2209\t      this.limitCourseVelocity(vel, n, RIDER.courseLimit);\n2210\t      if (Math.hypot(vel.x, vel.z) > 0.5) {\n2211\t        const travelYaw = Math.atan2(vel.x, -vel.z);\n2212\t        this.yaw += wrapPi(travelYaw - this.yaw) * (1 - Math.exp(-5 * dt));\n2213\t        this.limitCourseYaw(n, RIDER.recoveryCourseLimit, dt);\n2214\t      }\n2215\t      pos.x += vel.x * dt;\n2216\t      pos.z += vel.z * dt;\n2217\t      pos.y = this.world.height(pos.x, pos.z);\n2218\t      this.slide = s * 0.5;\n2219\t\n2220\t      if (this.fallTimer <= 0) {\n2221\t        this.state = 'ride';\n2222\t        this.tumble = 0;\n2223\t        this.slide = 0;\n2224\t      }\n2225\t    }\n2226\t\n2227\t    this.springStep(dt);\n2228\t  }\n2229\t\n2230\t  /* --- the legs ------------------------------------------------------- */\n2231\t\n2232\t  springStep(dt) {\n2233\t    // A damped spring pulled towards the load the slope is putting through\n2234\t    // it. The load is one g of gravity plus whatever the edge is asking\n2235\t    // for, which is why a hard carve visibly squats the rider.\n2236\t    const w = RIDER.springFreq * TAU;\n2237\t    const lateralG = (this.carveLoad * RIDER.grip) / RIDER.gravity;\n2238\t    // One g of gravity, plus whatever the ground's curvature is adding or\n2239\t    // taking away, plus the corner — and the vertical two combine before the\n2240\t    // lateral one because that is the order they act in. A compression squats\n2241\t    // the rider and lifts the camera's floor; a rollover stands them up and\n2242\t    // goes light, which is the frame going quiet a moment before it lets go.\n2243\t    const verticalG = Math.max(0, 1 + this.bend);\n2244\t    const load = this.grounded && this.state !== 'fall'\n2245\t      ? Math.hypot(verticalG, lateralG)\n2246\t      : 0;\n2247\t    this.gLoad = approach(this.gLoad, load, 9, dt);\n2248\t\n2249\t    let target = load * RIDER.compressPerG * RIDER.gravity / 9.81;\n2250\t    if (this.tucking) target = Math.max(target, RIDER.tuckCompress);\n2251\t    if (this.brake > 0) target = Math.max(target, RIDER.brakeCompress * this.brake);\n2252\t    if (this.charging) target = RIDER.compressMax * (0.35 + 0.65 * this.charge);\n2253\t    if (this.state === 'fall') target = RIDER.compressMax;\n2254\t    if (!this.grounded && this.state !== 'fall') target = -0.12 * Math.min(1, this.airTime * 3);\n2255\t\n2256\t    this.compressionVel += (target - this.compression) * w * w * dt;\n2257\t    this.compressionVel *= Math.exp(-2 * RIDER.springDamp * w * dt);\n2258\t    this.compression += this.compressionVel * dt;\n2259\t    this.compression = clamp(this.compression, -0.3, RIDER.compressMax);\n2260\t  }\n2261\t\n2262\t  /* --- what the world does to the rider -------------------------------- */\n2263\t\n2264\t  /* Hitting something solid.\n2265\t\n2266\t     `nx, nz` is the direction from the obstacle to the rider — the way they\n2267\t     are about to be pushed — and `closeness` is 0 at the edge of the contact\n2268\t     and 1 dead centre of it.\n2269\t\n2270\t     What decides the outcome is the speed actually being carried *into* the\n2271\t     thing, which is the quantity that hurts and the one the old version\n2272\t     never looked at: it judged on centrality alone, so a trunk clipped at\n2273\t     walking pace and the same trunk at 150 km/h were the same event. Now\n2274\t     direction only decides how much of the speed is aimed at the tree, and\n2275\t     the speed decides what that costs. Returns what happened so the caller\n2276\t     can pick a sound for it. */\n2277\t  strike(nx, nz, closeness) {\n2278\t    const push = this._h.set(nx, 0, nz);\n2279\t    const len = push.length();\n2280\t    if (len < 1e-5) push.set(0, 0, 1);\n2281\t    else push.multiplyScalar(1 / len);\n2282\t\n2283\t    // Speed into the obstacle: everything the rider has, minus whatever was\n2284\t    // already carrying them away from it\n2285\t    const into = Math.max(0, -(this.vel.x * push.x + this.vel.z * push.z));\n2286\t    const severity = clamp(\n2287\t      (into - RIDER.brushSpeed) / (RIDER.wipeoutSpeed - RIDER.brushSpeed), 0, 1.6,\n2288\t    ) * (0.45 + 0.55 * clamp(closeness, 0, 1));\n2289\t\n2290\t    if (severity >= 1) {\n2291\t      this.fall('hit', into);\n2292\t      return 'fall';\n2293\t    }\n2294\t    if (into < RIDER.brushSpeed * 0.5) {\n2295\t      // Barely touched it. A nudge and a little powder, and the run goes on.\n2296\t      this.vel.addScaledVector(push, Math.min(into, 1.5 + 2 * severity));\n2297\t      this.compressionVel += 3;\n2298\t      return 'brush';\n2299\t    }\n2300\t    // Glancing: spun off the line, and it costs a share of the speed that\n2301\t    // went into the trunk rather than a flat percentage of everything\n2302\t    this.vel.addScaledVector(push, Math.min(into, 3 + 9 * severity));\n2303\t    this.vel.multiplyScalar(1 - 0.28 * severity);\n2304\t    this.compressionVel += 9 * severity;\n2305\t    this.slide += 6 * severity;\n2306\t    // A shove on the yaw as well, but a small one. Half a radian was an\n2307\t    // instant thirty-degree turn arriving unannounced, and between that and\n2308\t    // the sideways impulse the player had no way to tell what had happened\n2309\t    // to them. The impulse alone reads as being knocked off line; the\n2310\t    // self-centring then brings the board round to match, which is a thing\n2311\t    // the player can see happening rather than a thing that has happened.\n2312\t    this.yaw += (Math.sign(nx * this.heading.z - nz * this.heading.x) || 1) * severity * 0.18;\n2313\t    return 'graze';\n2314\t  }\n2315\t\n2316\t  brush(strength) {\n2317\t    this.vel.multiplyScalar(Math.max(0.35, 1 - strength));\n2318\t    this.slide += 4 * strength;\n2319\t    this.compressionVel += 4 * strength;\n2320\t  }\n2321\t}\n2322\t\n2323\t/* The name of what just happened, in the language of the sport. Reads the\n2324\t   quantised counts rather than the raw radians, so it always says the same\n2325\t   number the score is paying for.\n2326\t\n2327\t   THE OFF-AXIS NAMES are the part worth explaining, because they are the\n2328\t   reason this function is no longer a list of clauses joined by plus signs.\n2329\t   A spin is a rotation about the rider's vertical and a flip is a rotation\n2330\t   about their horizontal, and a rider who does both at once has not done two\n2331\t   tricks — they have done one trick about a tilted axis, which is much harder\n2332\t   to see out of than either half and which the sport has always had its own\n2333\t   names for. Written as \"BACKSIDE 720 + BACKFLIP\" the game was describing the\n2334\t   inputs; written as \"CORK 720\" it is describing the trick.\n2335\t\n2336\t   The mapping is the common one. A backward flip laid into a backside spin is\n2337\t   a cork and into a frontside spin is a rodeo; a forward flip either way is a\n2338\t   misty. Two flips make it a double. It is a simplification of a vocabulary\n2339\t   that genuinely does distinguish more cases than this — but every case it\n2340\t   does name, it names correctly, and a rider who knows the difference will\n2341\t   recognise what they just did. */\n2342\texport function trickName(s, verdict) {\n2343\t  const parts = [];\n2344\t  const steps = s.halfTurns * 180;\n2345\t  const flips = s.flipTurns;\n2346\t  const frontside = s.spin > 0;\n2347\t\n2348\t  if (flips >= 1 && steps >= 360) {\n2349\t    // Both axes: one trick about a tilted one.\n2350\t    const base = s.flips > 0 ? 'MISTY' : frontside ? 'RODEO' : 'CORK';\n2351\t    /* The sport has words for two and three and then stops having them, and\n2352\t       this hill has no practical ceiling on airtime, so a long enough hang\n2353\t       time genuinely reaches four. Past three it counts,\n2354\t       because announcing a quadruple cork as a triple while paying for four\n2355\t       is the label and the score disagreeing, which is the one thing this\n2356\t       function exists to prevent. */\n2357\t    const many = flips > 3 ? `${flips}× ` : flips > 2 ? 'TRIPLE ' : flips > 1 ? 'DOUBLE ' : '';\n2358\t    parts.push(`${many}${base} ${steps}`);\n2359\t  } else {\n2360\t    if (steps >= 180) parts.push(`${frontside ? 'FRONTSIDE' : 'BACKSIDE'} ${steps}`);\n2361\t    if (flips >= 1) {\n2362\t      // `flips` counts whole revolutions; `s.flips` is the signed total, and\n2363\t      // its sign is the one thing that says which way the world went round.\n2364\t      const way = s.flips > 0 ? 'FRONTFLIP' : 'BACKFLIP';\n2365\t      parts.push(flips > 1 ? `${flips}× ${way}` : way);\n2366\t    }\n2367\t  }\n2368\t\n2369\t  // The grab is named rather than counted, and the tweak stop is where a\n2370\t  // reach stops being a touch and becomes something being held.\n2371\t  if (s.grabTime > RIDER.grabHold[0]) {\n2372\t    const grab = RIDER.grabs[s.grabKind] || RIDER.grabs[0];\n2373\t    parts.push(s.grabTime > RIDER.grabHold[1]\n2374\t      ? `TWEAKED ${grab.name}` : grab.name);\n2375\t  }\n2376\t\n2377\t  if (!parts.length) {\n2378\t    if (s.airTime > 1.25) parts.push('BIG AIR');\n2379\t    else return null;\n2380\t  }\n2381\t  if (s.switchStance) parts.unshift('SWITCH');\n2382\t  let name = parts.join(' + ');\n2383\t  if (verdict === SKETCHY) name += ' (SKETCHY)';\n2384\t  return name;\n2385\t}\n2386\t\n2387\t/* HOW MUCH OF A BUTTER ACTUALLY CAME ROUND, and it is a floor with a window\n2388\t   rather than a rounding.\n2389\t\n2390\t   An aerial rotation is rounded to the nearest half turn and that is honest,\n2391\t   because the landing has already refused anything more than `landWindow` off\n2392\t   a clean stance — the rounding is a statement about a rotation that has been\n2393\t   checked. A press has no such check. It ends when the player lets go, at\n2394\t   whatever angle they let go at, so rounding to the nearest paid a\n2395\t   three-quarter turn as a full one: two hundred and seventy degrees came out\n2396\t   as BUTTER 360, with the points and the combo to match, while the board was\n2397\t   still visibly sideways.\n2398\t\n2399\t   So it counts the half turns that arrived, and the same window a landing\n2400\t   allows is added before the floor so that a rotation which has essentially\n2401\t   got there still counts as having got there. Reading `landWindow` rather\n2402\t   than choosing a second number is the point: the two paths now agree about\n2403\t   what \"completed\" means by construction, and cannot drift apart. */\n2404\texport function butterHalfTurns(spin) {\n2405\t  return Math.floor((Math.abs(spin) + RIDER.landWindow) / Math.PI);\n2406\t}\n2407\t\n2408\t/* And what it is called, which is a name rather than a sentence for the same\n2409\t   reason a cork is: the rotation and the press are one trick. It reads the\n2410\t   same quantisation the score does, so the label and the payout can never\n2411\t   disagree — the standing rule for every other trick in this file. */\n2412\texport function butterName(spin) {\n2413\t  return `BUTTER ${Math.max(1, butterHalfTurns(spin)) * 180}`;\n2414\t}\n2415\t"}]},"uuid":"b1b70766-7956-4182-9e76-c54d9759ddd1","timestamp":"2026-09-24T19:54:38.253Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen/js/rider.js","content":"    const landedVel = this._p.copy(vel).addScaledVector(n, -vel.dot(n));\n    this.limitCourseVelocity(landedVel, n, RIDER.courseLimit);\n    const travelYaw = landedVel.lengthSq() > 1e-5\n      ? Math.atan2(landedVel.x, -landedVel.z)\n      : Math.atan2(this._flow.x, -this._flow.z);\n    const off = wrapPi(this.yaw - travelYaw);\n    const forward = Math.abs(off);\n    const backward = Math.abs(Math.abs(off) - Math.PI);\n    const spinErr = Math.min(forward, backward);\n    const isSwitch = backward < forward;\n    const flipErr = Math.abs(wrapPi(this.flip));\n\n    // A short hop is not a trick, but it is still a physical landing. Trick\n    // eligibility gates only the rotation tests; impact severity always\n    // applies, so a hard, short drop cannot be rewritten as CLEAN.\n    const judged = this.airTime >= RIDER.minJudgedAir\n      || Math.abs(this.spinAccum) > 0.8 || Math.abs(this.flipAccum) > 0.8;\n    const w = RIDER.landWindow;\n    const p = RIDER.landPitchWindow;\n    let verdict = CLEAN;\n    /* A sideways board costs the landing, not the whole run.\n\n       Because the nearest regular/switch stance is used above, `spinErr` can\n       only ever reach ninety degrees. The old bail threshold began at eighty-\n       two, leaving an arbitrary eight-degree sliver where steering through an\n       unnoticed natural hop caused a wipeout while a nearly identical landing\n       was merely sketchy. Keep yaw mismatch in the sketchy branch below; hard\n       impact and a rider arriving upside-down remain genuine bails. */\n    if (impact > RIDER.hardImpact\n      || (judged && flipErr > p * 1.6)) {\n      verdict = BAIL;\n    } else if (impact > RIDER.softImpact * 1.9\n      || (judged && (spinErr > w || flipErr > p))) {\n      verdict = SKETCHY;\n    }\n\n    // The grab has to be let go before the snow, which is what the start\n    // screen says. Held through touchdown it was free points and a strictly\n    // dominant strategy. It costs a sketchy landing rather than a fall,\n    // because nothing here should end a run.\n    if (judged && this.grabbing) verdict = Math.max(verdict, SKETCHY);\n\n    // Rotations are quantised to the *nearest* half turn, not the floor.\n    // The landing window has already accepted anything inside ±53°, so a\n    // clean 170 is a 180 and a clean 350° flip is a backflip. The label and\n    // the score both read these, so they can never disagree.\n    const halfTurns = Math.round(Math.abs(this.spinAccum) / Math.PI);\n    const flipTurns = Math.round(Math.abs(this.flipAccum) / TAU);\n\n    const summary = {\n      verdict,\n      airTime: this.airTime,\n      spin: this.spinAccum,\n      flips: this.flipAccum,\n      halfTurns,\n      flipTurns,\n      grabTime: this.grabTime,\n      grabKind: this.grabKind,\n      switchStance: isSwitch,\n      impact,\n      judged,\n      lipPop: this.lipPop,\n      takeoffSpeed: this.takeoffSpeed,\n    };\n\n    this.grounded = true;\n    this.state = 'ride';\n    this.extension = 0;\n    // The pitch the landing forgives, kept for the renderer. The yaw snap\n    // below hands its residual to `yawGlide`; the flip's residual — up to\n    // ~78° on the sketchiest landed flip — was zeroed in one write, and the\n    // board's nose visibly teleported level. Same cure, same drain.\n    const flipResidual = wrapPi(this.flip);\n    this.flip = 0;\n    this.flipVel = 0;\n    this.spinVel = 0;\n    this.grab = 0;\n    this.grabTime = 0;\n    this.grabbing = false;\n    this.compressionVel += Math.min(impact, 30) * 0.85;\n\n    if (verdict === BAIL) {\n      this.fall('land', impact);\n    } else {\n      /* Snap the board straight — forward or switch, whichever was closer —\n         but only if this was actually a jump.\n\n         Doing it on every touchdown is what made the controls feel haunted.\n         The mountain is covered in rollers and knolls, so a rider carving\n         across it is leaving the ground and returning several times a\n         second, and each of those hops was rewriting the yaw to point wherever\n         the *velocity* happened to be going. From the player's hands that is\n         indistinguishable from the game steering for them at random, and it\n         fought hardest exactly when they were holding the tightest carve —\n         which is when the board is furthest from the direction of travel and\n         so had the most to be snapped away. `judged` is already the test for\n         \"this was a trick rather than chatter\", and it is the right test\n         here too. */\n      if (judged) {\n        /* The physics squares the board in one write — judging and the next\n           carve both need the aligned stance now — but the few degrees the\n           landing assist did not close are handed to `yawGlide`, which the\n           renderer subtracts and drains over a tenth of a second. The same\n           landing, minus the one-frame twitch. */\n        const snapped = isSwitch ? travelYaw + Math.PI : travelYaw;\n        this.yawGlide = clamp(wrapPi(this.yaw - snapped), -1.1, 1.1);\n        this.flipGlide = clamp(flipResidual, -1.4, 1.4);\n        this.yaw = snapped;\n        this.switchStance = isSwitch;\n      }\n      // A sketchy landing wobbles and costs speed; a clean one costs nothing\n      // The snow takes the normal component; the rider keeps the tangential\n      // run whose direction was used to classify the landing above.\n      vel.copy(landedVel);\n      if (verdict === SKETCHY) vel.multiplyScalar(0.86);\n      // …but not all of it. A long drop converts a lot of height into speed\n      // along the hill, and with nothing taken back a rider could ride one\n      // kicker into 270 km/h and never slow down. Everything past the point\n      // the legs can absorb comfortably is paid for on the way out, which is\n      // also what makes a big air a decision rather than a free boost.\n      const excess = Math.max(0, impact - RIDER.softImpact);\n      if (excess > 0) vel.multiplyScalar(1 - Math.min(0.24, excess * 0.012));\n      this.landing = summary;\n      this.emit('land', summary);\n    }\n    return summary;\n  }\n\n  /* --- going down, and getting back up -------------------------------- */\n\n  /* `into` is the speed that went into whatever caused this, in m/s. It is\n     what decides how far the rider is thrown: a bail off a bad landing hands\n     over the impact, a tree hands over the closing speed, and a spill at\n     walking pace hands over almost nothing and is over in a second. */\n  fall(cause = 'land', into = 0) {\n    if (this.state === 'fall') return;\n    this.state = 'fall';\n    this.grounded = false;\n    this.airborne = true;\n    this.fallElapsed = 0;\n    this.fallTimer = 0;\n    this.tumble = 0;\n    this.charge = 0;\n    this.charging = false;\n    this._latePopAllowed = false;\n    this._jumpBuffer = 0;\n    this.pushing = false;\n    this.grab = 0;\n    this.grabbing = false;\n    this.grabTime = 0;\n    // A butter interrupted by a tree is not a butter. Clearing rather than\n    // releasing is the difference between the trick ending and it having\n    // happened, and going down is the one way it can end without having.\n    this.press = 0;\n    this.pressTime = 0;\n    this.pressSpin = 0;\n    this.extension = 0;\n    this.bend = 0;\n    this._bendReady = false;\n    /* The flight that just ended is over, and its ledger goes with it. These\n       three survived a fall untouched, and everything that reads \"airborne\"\n       state read them as live: the HUD kept the last jump's trick name up\n       for the whole tumble, the audio played rotation blips for spins from\n       a minute ago, and the chase camera framed the wipeout as a soaring\n       air because `airTime` still held the old flight's value. */\n    this.airTime = 0;\n    this.spinAccum = 0;\n    this.flipAccum = 0;\n\n    // What the body keeps, and what the collision turns into height. A rider\n    // doing 40 m/s into a trunk leaves it doing 25 and climbing, which is\n    // most of a second in the air before the hill even gets a say.\n    this.vel.multiplyScalar(RIDER.fallLaunch);\n    const lift = Math.max(into, this.vel.length() * 0.35) * RIDER.fallLift;\n    this.vel.y += Math.min(15, lift);\n    this.pos.y = Math.max(this.pos.y, this.world.height(this.pos.x, this.pos.z) + 0.05);\n    this.compressionVel += 8;\n    // Keep the impact that caused the fall attached to the event. The crash\n    // mix, powder plume and camera response all need the same physical number\n    // that launched the body; throwing it away here made every wipeout read\n    // identically, from a walking-speed washout to a full-speed tree hit.\n    this.emit('fall', cause, into);\n  }\n\n  /* A tumble is a body, not a timer. While it is off the ground it is\n     ballistic; when it arrives it bounces, lower each time, and slides. The\n     rider is allowed up once they have actually stopped — or once `fallMax`\n     has run out, because a body that finds a hollow to oscillate in should\n     not be able to hold the run hostage. */\n  fallStep(dt) {\n    const { pos, vel } = this;\n    this.brake = approach(this.brake, 0, RIDER.brakeRelease, dt);\n\n    if (this.state === 'fall') {\n      this.fallElapsed += dt;\n      const sp = vel.length();\n      this.tumble += dt * 9 * clamp(sp / 12, 0.25, 2.2);\n      const gy = this.world.height(pos.x, pos.z);\n\n      if (this.airborne) {\n        vel.y -= RIDER.gravity * dt;\n        vel.multiplyScalar(Math.max(0, 1 - RIDER.fallDrag * dt));\n        pos.x += vel.x * dt;\n        pos.y += vel.y * dt;\n        pos.z += vel.z * dt;\n\n        const ny = this.world.height(pos.x, pos.z);\n        if (pos.y <= ny) {\n          pos.y = ny;\n          const n = normalFrom(this.world.height, pos.x, pos.z, this._n);\n          this.normal.copy(n);\n          const into = vel.dot(n);\n          if (into < 0) {\n            // Bounce, and take the rest of it out on the snow\n            vel.addScaledVector(n, -into * (1 + RIDER.fallBounce));\n            this.emit('impact', -into);\n            this.compressionVel += Math.min(-into, 24) * 0.6;\n          }\n          vel.multiplyScalar(0.72);\n          // Below a certain hop it is no longer flying, it is sliding\n          if (vel.dot(n) < 2.2) {\n            this.airborne = false;\n            vel.addScaledVector(n, -vel.dot(n));\n          }\n        }\n      } else {\n        // Still a body sliding down a hill: gravity on the tangent, and a\n        // lot more friction than a board on its edge\n        const n = normalFrom(this.world.height, pos.x, pos.z, this._n);\n        this.normal.copy(n);\n        vel.addScaledVector(n, -vel.dot(n));\n        const g = this._g.set(0, -RIDER.gravity, 0);\n        const gn = g.dot(n);\n        vel.x += (g.x - n.x * gn) * dt;\n        vel.y += (g.y - n.y * gn) * dt;\n        vel.z += (g.z - n.z * gn) * dt;\n        const s = vel.length();\n        if (s > 1e-4) vel.multiplyScalar(Math.max(0, s - RIDER.fallFriction * dt) / s);\n\n        pos.x += vel.x * dt;\n        pos.z += vel.z * dt;\n        pos.y = this.world.height(pos.x, pos.z);\n\n        // And the hill can hand them back into the air — a tumble off a\n        // cliff should not stick to the face on the way down\n        const drop = this.world.height(pos.x + vel.x * 0.09, pos.z + vel.z * 0.09);\n        if (pos.y - drop > 0.5) this.airborne = true;\n      }\n\n      this.slide = vel.length() * 0.8;\n      this.extension = Math.max(0, pos.y - gy);\n\n      const stopped = !this.airborne && vel.length() < RIDER.fallRest;\n      if ((stopped && this.fallElapsed > RIDER.fallMin) || this.fallElapsed > RIDER.fallMax) {\n        // Up on the board again, pointed downhill, with a moment of grace so\n        // the same tree cannot take you twice\n        this.state = 'rise';\n        this.grounded = true;\n        this.airborne = false;\n        this.fallTimer = RIDER.riseTime;\n        pos.y = this.world.height(pos.x, pos.z);\n        /* Always reconcile the board and its travel before the model stands\n           up. `fallMax` can expire while a fast body is still sliding, so a\n           low-speed-only repair left exactly the hardest crashes resuming\n           tail-first. Keep the nearest legal traverse and a bounded share of\n           the remaining momentum, then rebuild it on the local surface. */\n        const n = normalFrom(this.world.height, pos.x, pos.z, this._n);\n        const restart = clamp(vel.length(), 6, 12);\n        this.switchStance = false;\n        this.limitCourseYaw(n, RIDER.recoveryCourseLimit, dt);\n        const h = this._h.set(Math.sin(this.yaw), 0, -Math.cos(this.yaw));\n        h.addScaledVector(n, -h.dot(n)).normalize();\n        vel.copy(h).multiplyScalar(restart);\n        this.grace = 1.3;\n        this.emit('rise');\n      }\n    } else {\n      // Getting up. Ordinary sliding physics, and the model reads `fallTimer`\n      // counting down across the window to blend the body back upright.\n      this.fallTimer -= dt;\n      const n = normalFrom(this.world.height, pos.x, pos.z, this._n);\n      this.normal.copy(n);\n      vel.addScaledVector(n, -vel.dot(n));\n      const g = this._g.set(0, -RIDER.gravity, 0);\n      const gn = g.dot(n);\n      vel.x += (g.x - n.x * gn) * dt;\n      vel.y += (g.y - n.y * gn) * dt;\n      vel.z += (g.z - n.z * gn) * dt;\n      const s = vel.length();\n      if (s > 1e-4) vel.multiplyScalar(Math.max(0, s - 4 * dt) / s);\n      this.limitCourseVelocity(vel, n, RIDER.courseLimit);\n      if (Math.hypot(vel.x, vel.z) > 0.5) {\n        const travelYaw = Math.atan2(vel.x, -vel.z);\n        this.yaw += wrapPi(travelYaw - this.yaw) * (1 - Math.exp(-5 * dt));\n        this.limitCourseYaw(n, RIDER.recoveryCourseLimit, dt);\n      }\n      pos.x += vel.x * dt;\n      pos.z += vel.z * dt;\n      pos.y = this.world.height(pos.x, pos.z);\n      this.slide = s * 0.5;\n\n      if (this.fallTimer <= 0) {\n        this.state = 'ride';\n        this.tumble = 0;\n        this.slide = 0;\n      }\n    }\n\n    this.springStep(dt);\n  }\n\n  /* --- the legs ------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n  springStep(dt) {\n    // A damped spring pulled towards the load the slope is putting through\n    // it. The load is one g of gravity plus whatever the edge is asking\n    // for, which is why a hard carve visibly squats the rider.\n    const w = RIDER.springFreq * TAU;\n    const lateralG = (this.carveLoad * RIDER.grip) / RIDER.gravity;\n    // One g of gravity, plus whatever the ground's curvature is adding or\n    // taking away, plus the corner — and the vertical two combine before the\n    // lateral one because that is the order they act in. A compression squats\n    // the rider and lifts the camera's floor; a rollover stands them up and\n    // goes light, which is the frame going quiet a moment before it lets go.\n    const verticalG = Math.max(0, 1 + this.bend);\n    const load = this.grounded && this.state !== 'fall'\n      ? Math.hypot(verticalG, lateralG)\n      : 0;\n    this.gLoad = approach(this.gLoad, load, 9, dt);\n\n    let target = load * RIDER.compressPerG * RIDER.gravity / 9.81;\n    if (this.tucking) target = Math.max(target, RIDER.tuckCompress);\n    if (this.brake > 0) target = Math.max(target, RIDER.brakeCompress * this.brake);\n    if (this.charging) target = RIDER.compressMax * (0.35 + 0.65 * this.charge);\n    if (this.state === 'fall') target = RIDER.compressMax;\n    if (!this.grounded && this.state !== 'fall') target = -0.12 * Math.min(1, this.airTime * 3);\n\n    this.compressionVel += (target - this.compression) * w * w * dt;\n    this.compressionVel *= Math.exp(-2 * RIDER.springDamp * w * dt);\n    this.compression += this.compressionVel * dt;\n    this.compression = clamp(this.compression, -0.3, RIDER.compressMax);\n  }\n\n  /* --- what the world does to the rider -------------------------------- */\n\n  /* Hitting something solid.\n\n     `nx, nz` is the direction from the obstacle to the rider — the way they\n     are about to be pushed — and `closeness` is 0 at the edge of the contact\n     and 1 dead centre of it.\n\n     What decides the outcome is the speed actually being carried *into* the\n     thing, which is the quantity that hurts and the one the old version\n     never looked at: it judged on centrality alone, so a trunk clipped at\n     walking pace and the same trunk at 150 km/h were the same event. Now\n     direction only decides how much of the speed is aimed at the tree, and\n     the speed decides what that costs. Returns what happened so the caller\n     can pick a sound for it. */\n  strike(nx, nz, closeness) {\n    const push = this._h.set(nx, 0, nz);\n    const len = push.length();\n    if (len < 1e-5) push.set(0, 0, 1);\n    else push.multiplyScalar(1 / len);\n\n    // Speed into the obstacle: everything the rider has, minus whatever was\n    // already carrying them away from it\n    const into = Math.max(0, -(this.vel.x * push.x + this.vel.z * push.z));\n    const severity = clamp(\n      (into - RIDER.brushSpeed) / (RIDER.wipeoutSpeed - RIDER.brushSpeed), 0, 1.6,\n    ) * (0.45 + 0.55 * clamp(closeness, 0, 1));\n\n    if (severity >= 1) {\n      this.fall('hit', into);\n      return 'fall';\n    }\n    if (into < RIDER.brushSpeed * 0.5) {\n      // Barely touched it. A nudge and a little powder, and the run goes on.\n      this.vel.addScaledVector(push, Math.min(into, 1.5 + 2 * severity));\n      this.compressionVel += 3;\n      return 'brush';\n    }\n    // Glancing: spun off the line, and it costs a share of the speed that\n    // went into the trunk rather than a flat percentage of everything\n    this.vel.addScaledVector(push, Math.min(into, 3 + 9 * severity));\n    this.vel.multiplyScalar(1 - 0.28 * severity);\n    this.compressionVel += 9 * severity;\n    this.slide += 6 * severity;\n    // A shove on the yaw as well, but a small one. Half a radian was an\n    // instant thirty-degree turn arriving unannounced, and between that and\n    // the sideways impulse the player had no way to tell what had happened\n    // to them. The impulse alone reads as being knocked off line; the\n    // self-centring then brings the board round to match, which is a thing\n    // the player can see happening rather than a thing that has happened.\n    this.yaw += (Math.sign(nx * this.heading.z - nz * this.heading.x) || 1) * severity * 0.18;\n    return 'graze';\n  }\n\n  brush(strength) {\n    this.vel.multiplyScalar(Math.max(0.35, 1 - strength));\n    this.slide += 4 * strength;\n    this.compressionVel += 4 * strength;\n  }\n}\n\n/* The name of what just happened, in the language of the sport. Reads the\n   quantised counts rather than the raw radians, so it always says the same\n   number the score is paying for.\n\n   THE OFF-AXIS NAMES are the part worth explaining, because they are the\n   reason this function is no longer a list of clauses joined by plus signs.\n   A spin is a rotation about the rider's vertical and a flip is a rotation\n   about their horizontal, and a rider who does both at once has not done two\n   tricks — they have done one trick about a tilted axis, which is much harder\n   to see out of than either half and which the sport has always had its own\n   names for. Written as \"BACKSIDE 720 + BACKFLIP\" the game was describing the\n   inputs; written as \"CORK 720\" it is describing the trick.\n\n   The mapping is the common one. A backward flip laid into a backside spin is\n   a cork and into a frontside spin is a rodeo; a forward flip either way is a\n   misty. Two flips make it a double. It is a simplification of a vocabulary\n   that genuinely does distinguish more cases than this — but every case it\n   does name, it names correctly, and a rider who knows the difference will\n   recognise what they just did. */\nexport function trickName(s, verdict) {\n  const parts = [];\n  const steps = s.halfTurns * 180;\n  const flips = s.flipTurns;\n  const frontside = s.spin > 0;\n\n  if (flips >= 1 && steps >= 360) {\n    // Both axes: one trick about a tilted one.\n    const base = s.flips > 0 ? 'MISTY' : frontside ? 'RODEO' : 'CORK';\n    /* The sport has words for two and three and then stops having them, and\n       this hill has no practical ceiling on airtime, so a long enough hang\n       time genuinely reaches four. Past three it counts,\n       because announcing a quadruple cork as a triple while paying for four\n       is the label and the score disagreeing, which is the one thing this\n       function exists to prevent. */\n    const many = flips > 3 ? `${flips}× ` : flips > 2 ? 'TRIPLE ' : flips > 1 ? 'DOUBLE ' : '';\n    parts.push(`${many}${base} ${steps}`);\n  } else {\n    if (steps >= 180) parts.push(`${frontside ? 'FRONTSIDE' : 'BACKSIDE'} ${steps}`);\n    if (flips >= 1) {\n      // `flips` counts whole revolutions; `s.flips` is the signed total, and\n      // its sign is the one thing that says which way the world went round.\n      const way = s.flips > 0 ? 'FRONTFLIP' : 'BACKFLIP';\n      parts.push(flips > 1 ? `${flips}× ${way}` : way);\n    }\n  }\n\n  // The grab is named rather than counted, and the tweak stop is where a\n  // reach stops being a touch and becomes something being held.\n  if (s.grabTime > RIDER.grabHold[0]) {\n    const grab = RIDER.grabs[s.grabKind] || RIDER.grabs[0];\n    parts.push(s.grabTime > RIDER.grabHold[1]\n      ? `TWEAKED ${grab.name}` : grab.name);\n  }\n\n  if (!parts.length) {\n    if (s.airTime > 1.25) parts.push('BIG AIR');\n    else return null;\n  }\n  if (s.switchStance) parts.unshift('SWITCH');\n  let name = parts.join(' + ');\n  if (verdict === SKETCHY) name += ' (SKETCHY)';\n  return name;\n}\n\n/* HOW MUCH OF A BUTTER ACTUALLY CAME ROUND, and it is a floor with a window\n   rather than a rounding.\n\n   An aerial rotation is rounded to the nearest half turn and that is honest,\n   because the landing has already refused anything more than `landWindow` off\n   a clean stance — the rounding is a statement about a rotation that has been\n   checked. A press has no such check. It ends when the player lets go, at\n   whatever angle they let go at, so rounding to the nearest paid a\n   three-quarter turn as a full one: two hundred and seventy degrees came out\n   as BUTTER 360, with the points and the combo to match, while the board was\n   still visibly sideways.\n\n   So it counts the half turns that arrived, and the same window a landing\n   allows is added before the floor so that a rotation which has essentially\n   got there still counts as having got there. Reading `landWindow` rather\n   than choosing a second number is the point: the two paths now agree about\n   what \"completed\" means by construction, and cannot drift apart. */\nexport function butterHalfTurns(spin) {\n  return Math.floor((Math.abs(spin) + RIDER.landWindow) / Math.PI);\n}\n\n/* And what it is called, which is a name rather than a sentence for the same\n   reason a cork is: the rotation and the press are one trick. It reads the\n   same quantisation the score does, so the label and the payout can never\n   disagree — the standing rule for every other trick in this file. */\nexport function butterName(spin) {\n  return `BUTTER ${Math.max(1, butterHalfTurns(spin)) * 180}`;\n}\n","numLines":497,"startLine":1919,"totalLines":2415}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8928ce05-746d-4ac7-804c-c12903641e84","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen","sessionId":"f31fbc8b-fac8-4283-8f36-bb5424b19347","version":"2.1.281","gitBranch":"main","slug":"enchanted-prancing-stroustrup"}
