{"parentUuid":"6f03e094-1a64-45bd-b7a8-1962a3ea29ed","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfNrL7mja3vY5bsPd1t3z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AgwNMeLBvtJnDvk3iuXbY5","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen/js/config.js","offset":1044,"limit":820},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":224,"cache_read_input_tokens":300050,"output_tokens":125,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":224,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":125,"cache_read_input_tokens":300050,"cache_creation_input_tokens":224,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":224},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.2.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"}],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01AgwNMeLBvtJnDvk3iuXbY5":{"file_path":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen/js/config.js","offset":1044,"limit":820}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfNrKuXnTfnkKZGyQjpo4","type":"assistant","uuid":"779915f0-16bc-4f4b-b706-afe5b398513c","timestamp":"2026-09-24T19:54:49.588Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen","sessionId":"f31fbc8b-fac8-4283-8f36-bb5424b19347","version":"2.1.281","gitBranch":"main","slug":"enchanted-prancing-stroustrup"}
{"parentUuid":"779915f0-16bc-4f4b-b706-afe5b398513c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c41cd6de-57c3-441a-96f7-ed621873bfe7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AgwNMeLBvtJnDvk3iuXbY5","type":"tool_result","content":"1044\texport const RIDER = {\n1045\t  /* A deliberately brisk arcade gravity, but a real constant: it is resolved\n1046\t     along the snow while grounded and applied straight down on every airborne\n1047\t     step. Jump height and airtime therefore come from takeoff speed and ramp\n1048\t     tangent rather than from an apex-specific hang-time assist. */\n1049\t  gravity: 22,\n1050\t\n1051\t  // Snow under a waxed base. The number is real; the grade does the rest.\n1052\t  /* WHAT THE SURFACE IS WORTH, as a top speed rather than only as drag.\n1053\t\n1054\t     The piste is a machine-made ribbon of hard, fast snow and the mountain\n1055\t     either side of it is not, and the model barely said so: open powder\n1056\t     dragged 1.15x a groomed run, which at speed is a rounding error, and\n1057\t     the speed ceiling ignored the surface completely. So the fastest line\n1058\t     down the hill was wherever the fall line happened to point, and the\n1059\t     corduroy the whole course is built around was worth nothing to ride.\n1060\t\n1061\t     These are multipliers on the flow-unlocked ceiling: the groomed ribbon\n1062\t     runs a little over it, settled powder is held well under, and the talus\n1063\t     and scoured rock under it is slower still. Ice keeps piste speed - it\n1064\t     is the fastest surface on the hill, and it is where the grip is not.\n1065\t     Combined with the drag figures below, the piste is now the fast line\n1066\t     and leaving it costs real speed rather than a little texture. */\n1067\t  pisteSpeed: 1.12,\n1068\t  powderSpeed: 0.60,\n1069\t  iceSpeed: 1.10,\n1070\t  rockSpeed: 0.50,\n1071\t  friction: 0.045,\n1072\t  brakeFriction: 0.62,\n1073\t  /* A one-button heel-side speed check.\n1074\t\n1075\t     S is not reverse and it is not an invisible friction switch. The rider\n1076\t     progressively pivots the board across the direction of travel, sets an\n1077\t     edge and lets the base sideslip while that edge scrubs speed. A/D held as\n1078\t     braking begins chooses the side; otherwise the current carve continues,\n1079\t     falling back to the rider's natural heel side from a straight line. */\n1080\t  brakeAngle: 1.34,       // radians ≈ 77° across travel at full pressure\n1081\t  brakeEdge: 0.72,        // share of the currently holdable edge angle\n1082\t  brakeEngage: 9.0,       // pressure build-up per second\n1083\t  brakeRelease: 12.0,     // releasing the edge is a little quicker\n1084\t  brakeSlideDamping: 0.45, // controlled sideslip; kinetic friction does the stop\n1085\t  brakeCompress: 0.24,    // low, weighted stance while checking speed\n1086\t  // Drag is what sets ordinary top speed, not a clamp: 6.3 m/s² of slope\n1087\t  // pull balances at about 40 m/s, or 143 km/h.\n1088\t  drag: 0.0034,\n1089\t  /* Flow owns the speed ceiling. With no flow the old 50 m/s limit remains;\n1090\t     a full meter opens it to five times that value. */\n1091\t  baseMaxSpeed: 50,   // m/s — 180 km/h\n1092\t  /* The ceiling flow unlocks. It was 250 m/s — nine hundred kilometres an\n1093\t     hour, which is not a snowboard and not steerable; the number existed\n1094\t     because flow was the only thing it fed and nothing else bounded it.\n1095\t     Flow is the score multiplier now (see `stepFlow` in main.js) and speed\n1096\t     is something you SPEND it on, so the ceiling only has to be worth\n1097\t     spending on: ninety-six metres a second is three hundred and forty-six\n1098\t     kilometres an hour at a full meter, which is still faster than anything\n1099\t     else on the hill and still a line you can hold. */\n1100\t  maxSpeed: 96,\n1101\t  // Where drag stiffens beyond v² when the powered tuck is not held.\n1102\t  // A big kicker landing converts a lot of height into speed and can\n1103\t  // overshoot this for a second or two; past it the run is pulled back\n1104\t  // rather than allowed to keep everything it just found.\n1105\t  dragKnee: 50,       // m/s — 180 km/h\n1106\t  /* MOMENTUM STABILITY.\n1107\t\n1108\t     Speed makes the board more committed, not more nervous. These are the\n1109\t     endpoints of one eased 0..1 signal used only by the parts of the model\n1110\t     that should settle with momentum: the board-length handling normal and\n1111\t     the rider's tolerance for a short balance transient. It never raises the\n1112\t     edge's lateral grip or weakens gravity, so a fast line stays wide and a\n1113\t     fast jump stays ballistic. */\n1114\t  stabilityFrom: 8,        // m/s — below this, the board remains nimble\n1115\t  stabilityFull: 45,       // m/s — full high-speed composure\n1116\t  contactHalfLength: 0.90, // metres sampled fore/aft at full stability\n1117\t  handlingResponseMin: 0.035, // seconds of surface-normal inertia\n1118\t  handlingResponseMax: 0.115,\n1119\t  /* One-foot skating when the board has almost stopped.\n1120\t\n1121\t     The old recovery was an invisible constant force below 2 m/s. It kept a\n1122\t     run from getting stranded, but the board simply gathered speed while the\n1123\t     rider stood still. Recovery is now a real sequence: hysteresis starts a\n1124\t     skating cycle at walking pace, each planted rear-foot stroke contributes\n1125\t     one measured impulse, and the foot finishes its current cycle before it\n1126\t     returns to the binding. On a flat surface the impulses still beat base\n1127\t     friction, while a descending pitch contributes its own gravity as usual. */\n1128\t  pushStart: 3.0,       // m/s — begin skating below 10.8 km/h\n1129\t  pushStop: 6.5,        // m/s — finish the stroke above 23.4 km/h\n1130\t  pushCadence: 1.55,    // complete rear-foot cycles per second\n1131\t  pushImpulse: 1.55,    // m/s added at the planted part of each stroke\n1132\t  pushPlant: 0.54,      // 0..1 phase at which the foot drives through snow\n1133\t  /* Climbing.\n1134\t\n1135\t     Gravity on the tangent already takes g·sin θ off a rider going uphill,\n1136\t     which is the whole of the real physics and, on a hill this shallow, not\n1137\t     very much: 5.6 m/s² on the average pitch. What it misses is that a board\n1138\t     pointed up a slope is also being driven into it, and a loaded edge in\n1139\t     soft snow scrubs. This is that — extra deceleration proportional to how\n1140\t     hard the rider is climbing — and it is what makes riding a wall a\n1141\t     decision with a price rather than a free way to change lanes. */\n1142\t  climbScrub: 7.5,\n1143\t\n1144\t  /* THE BOUNDARY MOUNTAINS, and the one thing the terrain alone could not\n1145\t     promise about them.\n1146\t\n1147\t     `TERRAIN.wall` contains by shape: past the quarterpipe lip the ground\n1148\t     rises asymptotically towards sixty-two metres and then creeps upwards for\n1149\t     ever, so gravity outside the corridor always points home. The arithmetic\n1150\t     for that was written against a terminal speed of fifty metres a second,\n1151\t     which buys v²/2g = fifty-seven metres of climb against the sixty-two the\n1152\t     exponential saturates at. Comfortable.\n1153\t\n1154\t     Full flow now raises that ceiling fivefold. The kinetic energy available\n1155\t     to a determined rider still beats any finite wall shaped for 50 m/s. At\n1156\t     eighty metres a second the same sum buys a hundred and forty-five metres,\n1157\t     and the containment is a suggestion.\n1158\t\n1159\t     What is missing is not more mountain. It is that the ground out there\n1160\t     stops being a piste. Everything inside the lip is machine-groomed; past\n1161\t     it is unpisted, wind-loaded, bottomless alpine snow, and the honest\n1162\t     difference between the two is not a few per cent of friction — a board\n1163\t     driven into deep snow at speed sinks, ploughs and stops climbing almost\n1164\t     at once. That is what these three numbers are, and they are why there is\n1165\t     still no invisible wall, no bounce and no teleport: the rider may ride\n1166\t     out there, may launch off the lip, may land out there and slide back\n1167\t     down. What they cannot do is *climb*, because there is nothing solid\n1168\t     enough under the board to climb on.\n1169\t\n1170\t     `span` is the depth over which the surface goes from groomed to\n1171\t     bottomless, `drag` the deceleration at the far end of it, and `grab` the\n1172\t     rate at which the uphill component of a rider's velocity is taken away\n1173\t     once they are in it — the last of which is the actual invariant, since it\n1174\t     is applied after the powered tuck's floor and therefore cannot be\n1175\t     outrun by any speed however it was obtained.\n1176\t\n1177\t     ALL THREE WENT UP, and the measurement that moved them is worth keeping.\n1178\t     A rider tucking and steering deliberately at the left bank reached 52.5\n1179\t     metres above the piste — most of a 62-metre wall, which is to say they\n1180\t     were climbing the mountain rather than riding the bank of the run. The\n1181\t     same trial now tops out at 23.5 m, and the three hands-off trials that\n1182\t     bracket it moved by tenths, which is the shape the change had to have:\n1183\t     the quarterpipe a rider *uses* is the first ten metres past the lip and\n1184\t     nothing above that was ever meant to be reachable. Deeper snow sooner,\n1185\t     and it refuses harder once you are in it. */\n1186\t  wallSpan: 12,\n1187\t  /* What the deep snow costs a rider trying to gain height in it, on top of\n1188\t     `climbScrub`. It is charged against the climb rather than flat against\n1189\t     the speed, and that is not a detail: a flat term large enough to stop a\n1190\t     fast rider is also larger than the 6.5 m/s² the creep slope has to offer\n1191\t     going the other way, which would strand anybody thrown out there by a\n1192\t     cliff instead of walking them home. Deep snow is slow to climb through\n1193\t     and merely slow to descend through. */\n1194\t  wallDrag: 30,\n1195\t  /* And the edge. This is the one that decides the *shape* of the failure\n1196\t     rather than its cost: unpisted wind-loaded snow will not hold a carved\n1197\t     edge across the fall line at all, so a board out there washes out and\n1198\t     goes wherever gravity sends it, which on the containment wall is home.\n1199\t     Without it a rider can hold a level traverse along the bank for ever —\n1200\t     not climbing, and not needing to, because the run itself descends nearly\n1201\t     a third of a metre for every metre of z and simply leaves them up there.\n1202\t     That was the actual escape, and it is a grip problem rather than a climb\n1203\t     one. This is the share of the edge the deepest snow takes away. */\n1204\t  wallWash: 0.88,\n1205\t  /* The refusal itself: the rate at which the component of travel pointing up\n1206\t     the fall line is taken back out. It saturates with depth — see the note\n1207\t     in `rider.js` — so past `wallSpan` metres the answer is simply no. */\n1208\t  wallGrab: 11.0,\n1209\t  /* And the sluff, which is the term that finally closed the last way out.\n1210\t\n1211\t     Refusing the climb is not enough on a run that descends nearly a third of\n1212\t     a metre for every metre of z: a rider who merely holds their altitude out\n1213\t     on the bank is left further and further above the piste without ever\n1214\t     having gained a centimetre, and the wander then carries the run out from\n1215\t     under them as well. Sixty metres up the containment wall, having done\n1216\t     nothing but hold a line.\n1217\t\n1218\t     What is missing from that picture is the snow. Metre-deep unconsolidated\n1219\t     powder on a forty-degree bank does not sit still under a board — it\n1220\t     fractures and runs, and it takes the board down with it. That is a\n1221\t     sluff, it is the single most characteristic thing steep off-piste snow\n1222\t     does, and it points down the fall line, which on the containment wall is\n1223\t     the way home. So it contains without ever pushing anybody anywhere they\n1224\t     were not already being pulled, and it can never strand a rider, because\n1225\t     the direction it acts in is the one they want to go. */\n1226\t  wallSluff: 10.5,\n1227\t  /* And the pivot, which is the one that does the containing. A board buried\n1228\t     across the fall line has a metre of edge on the uphill side and nothing\n1229\t     under the downhill one, so it swings to point down the hill — see the\n1230\t     long note in `rider.js`. As an exponential rate: a third of a second to\n1231\t     come round at full depth, and nothing at all inside the lip. */\n1232\t  wallPivot: 3.0,\n1233\t\n1234\t  /* Where climbing stops being slow and starts being impossible.\n1235\t\n1236\t     A snowboard is not a climbing tool. Run out of speed pointing up\n1237\t     something steep and you do not gently stop — the board stalls, the edge\n1238\t     that was holding you across the hill has nothing left to hold, and you\n1239\t     go over. Below `stallSlope` the rider is allowed to grind to a halt and\n1240\t     skate away again; above it they need\n1241\t     `stallSpeed` at that reference slope and proportionally more the steeper\n1242\t     it gets. Riding a quarterpipe wall is therefore a commitment: arrive\n1243\t     with enough and you get thrown off the lip, arrive without it and the\n1244\t     wall puts you down. */\n1245\t  stallSlope: 0.32,\n1246\t  stallSpeed: 7.5,\n1247\t  /* …and the two numbers that stop that from being a trap.\n1248\t\n1249\t     A kicker's face climbs at a slope of about 0.9 and a steep knoll is not\n1250\t     far off it, so a requirement that scales without limit means the rider\n1251\t     needs 76 km/h to ride up a two-metre jump without falling over — which\n1252\t     turns every launcher on the mountain into a wipeout and, worse, looks\n1253\t     exactly like the jump physics being broken. `stallCap` is the most speed\n1254\t     the rule is ever allowed to ask for.\n1255\t\n1256\t     And it has to persist. A ramp is under the board for a fifth of a second\n1257\t     at riding speed, so anything judged instantaneously fires on features\n1258\t     the rider is simply passing over. A third of a second of continuously\n1259\t     being too slow for the ground you are pointed up is a stall; less than\n1260\t     that is a kicker. */\n1261\t  /* The speed under which a rider has nothing left to stall *with*, and is\n1262\t     simply being walked back downhill by the recovery skate. Without this the\n1263\t     rule loops: fall, get up crawling, stall again, forever. */\n1264\t  stallMinSpeed: 3.5,\n1265\t  /* And the speed under which a stopped rider's board is allowed to ease\n1266\t     round to the fall line on its own. Well under walking pace: any higher\n1267\t     and the board swings downhill while real momentum is still carrying the\n1268\t     rider the way they were going, which is the \"he drives backwards\" that\n1269\t     started this. */\n1270\t  fallLineSpeed: 1.6,\n1271\t  stallCap: 9.0,\n1272\t  stallTime: 0.32,\n1273\t  // The powered tuck. Holding it guarantees this much additional speed each\n1274\t  // second and bypasses aerodynamic drag until the flow-owned cap. The edge\n1275\t  // still goes soft and the board stops answering quickly.\n1276\t  tuckAcceleration: 7.5, // m/s², accumulated for as long as W is held\n1277\t  tuckTurn: 0.45,     // share of the turn rate left while folded down\n1278\t  tuckGrip: 0.72,     // and of the grip\n1279\t  tuckCompress: 0.34, // metres of squat, which the camera rides down with\n1280\t  /* Fresh snow is slower and less supportive than the groomed surface. The\n1281\t     weather feeds these endpoints continuously, so a front arriving is felt\n1282\t     in the edge before it ever becomes a discrete \"blizzard\" label. */\n1283\t  stormGrip: 0.80,       // share of clear-weather edge hold in a whiteout\n1284\t  stormFriction: 1.45,   // share of base friction in deep fresh snow\n1285\t  // Edge grip, in m/s² of sideways hold. Past it the board washes out and\n1286\t  // the rider slides — which is where the spray, and the lost speed, are.\n1287\t  // This one number is most of how the game feels: raise it and the board\n1288\t  // is on rails, lower it and every turn is a drift.\n1289\t  /* Edge grip, in m/s² of sideways hold, and it is the number that decides\n1290\t     how sharply the board answers at all. The turn rate a carve produces is\n1291\t     grip/v, so this is the whole of the game's steering authority: at 27\n1292\t     against 39 m/s the heading came round at forty degrees a second, which is\n1293\t     honest for 140 km/h on real snow and reads, from behind, as a rider who\n1294\t     has leaned over and kept going straight. 34 buys back most of the\n1295\t     response without putting the board on rails — the wash-out is still one\n1296\t     firm input away, because what breaks traction is v² and that has not\n1297\t     moved. */\n1298\t  grip: 34,\n1299\t\n1300\t  /* THE CARVE, as geometry rather than as a steering rate.\n1301\t\n1302\t     A snowboard does not turn because it is pointed somewhere. It turns\n1303\t     because it has a sidecut — the edges are arcs, not straight lines — and\n1304\t     when the board is tilted up on one of them and pressed into the snow,\n1305\t     that arc is what the board is forced to travel along. Everything about\n1306\t     how the sport feels falls out of one equation:\n1307\t\n1308\t         R = sidecut / sin(edge angle)\n1309\t\n1310\t     Lay the board almost flat and the arc it describes is enormous, so it\n1311\t     runs nearly straight. Roll it up onto its edge and the radius collapses\n1312\t     towards the sidecut itself. The rider's only real input is how far over\n1313\t     they put it.\n1314\t\n1315\t     What makes that worth doing rather than steering directly is what comes\n1316\t     out of it without being written. Holding a radius R at v metres a second\n1317\t     needs v²/R of lateral grip, which goes as the *square* of the speed — so\n1318\t     the same edge angle that carves a beautiful arc at 40 km/h asks four\n1319\t     times as much of the snow at 80 and simply tears out. The rider is not\n1320\t     told this; they discover that a fast line has to be a wide line, which\n1321\t     is the single truest thing about the sport and previously had to be\n1322\t     faked with a cap on the turn rate.\n1323\t\n1324\t     It also gives the lean away for free. A rider holding a turn has to\n1325\t     incline until the resultant of gravity and the corner runs down the line\n1326\t     of their legs — atan(lateral / g) — so the body angle stops being a\n1327\t     cosmetic constant and becomes a read-out of how hard the turn actually\n1328\t     is. Nobody has to animate it and it can never disagree with the physics. */\n1329\t  /* Steering at walking pace, which a sidecut cannot do.\n1330\t\n1331\t     A carve's turn rate is v·sin(edge)/sidecut — proportional to speed — so\n1332\t     as the rider slows the board stops being able to turn at all. That is\n1333\t     correct for carving and on its own it is a trap, and it was a real one:\n1334\t     hold a turn and the rider carves round onto a traverse, the turn rate\n1335\t     decays with the speed, gravity has no component along a board pointed\n1336\t     across the hill, and they coast to a dead stop pointing sideways with no\n1337\t     way to bring the nose back down the fall line. Measured: 61 km/h to zero\n1338\t     in six seconds on an ordinary 18° pitch, and then stuck there.\n1339\t\n1340\t     What a rider actually does below about walking pace is stop carving and\n1341\t     skid the board round — pivot it flat, with the tail washing out. So this\n1342\t     is a second, speed-independent steering authority that fades *in* as the\n1343\t     carve fades out. Above `pivotSpeed` it is gone entirely and the sidecut\n1344\t     is the only thing turning the board, which is the model that matters. */\n1345\t  pivotRate: 2.2,     // rad/s of skid-steer available at a standstill\n1346\t  pivotSpeed: 9.0,    // m/s by which it has faded to nothing\n1347\t\n1348\t  sidecut: 8.0,       // metres — a real all-mountain board is 7 to 9\n1349\t  edgeMax: 1.15,      // radians ≈ 66°, about as far over as anyone gets\n1350\t  // How fast the board is rolled onto its edge and off it. Quick, because the\n1351\t  // sidecut now limits what the edge can *ask* for — so there is no longer any\n1352\t  // need to protect the rider from their own input by making it sluggish.\n1353\t  edgeRate: 11.0,\n1354\t  /* Cutting a trench is not free. A carved edge is slicing through snow\n1355\t     rather than gliding over it, and the deeper it is set the more of it is\n1356\t     in the way — this is that, and it is why holding a hard carve all the way\n1357\t     down a pitch costs speed a straight line would have kept. */\n1358\t  edgeDrag: 3.2,\n1359\t  /* How far past the edge angle the snow can actually support the rider is\n1360\t     allowed to push. This is the narrow band the whole handling model lives\n1361\t     in: at 1.0 the board is on rails and never slips, and much past 1.15\n1362\t     every turn is a skid. A few per cent of overdrive is where the edge\n1363\t     starts to wash and throw powder without letting go. */\n1364\t  edgeReach: 1.10,\n1365\t  /* An edge only bites because the board is moving.\n1366\t\n1367\t     Grip had no speed term at all, which meant a stationary board had the\n1368\t     same 34 m/s² of lateral hold as one at full song — and that is enough to\n1369\t     stand a rider sideways on a forty-four degree wall indefinitely. It is\n1370\t     exactly how a run got marooned: come to a crawl somewhere steep, the edge\n1371\t     holds across the fall line, the old floor force trickles you along its\n1372\t     threshold, and you creep across the mountain at seven km/h for ever with\n1373\t     gravity unable to get a word in. Observed on a\n1374\t     wall at 43.8°, velocity pinned at 2.0 m/s, indefinitely.\n1375\t\n1376\t     A real edge at walking pace does not hold; the board washes out sideways\n1377\t     and you slide down the fall line, which is the recovery. So the hold\n1378\t     ramps in with speed and keeps a floor, because a board with literally no\n1379\t     grip cannot be steered at all and standing still would become a slow\n1380\t     slide to the bottom of the mountain. */\n1381\t  gripSpeed: 7.0,     // m/s at which the edge is fully engaged\n1382\t  gripLow: 0.22,      // and the share of it a nearly-stopped board keeps\n1383\t  /* And the furthest the board is ever allowed to point away from the\n1384\t     direction it is genuinely travelling. Sixty degrees is about as far\n1385\t     across the fall line as anyone can hold one and still be riding it; past\n1386\t     that it is not a snowboard, it is a sledge. Braking gets more room to set\n1387\t     the board across the hill, but never enough to pivot through the direction\n1388\t     of travel and start running tail-first. */\n1389\t  maxSkid: 1.05,      // radians ≈ 60°\n1390\t  brakeSkid: 1.43,    // radians ≈ 82° — broad speed check, still one stance\n1391\t\n1392\t  /* The generated mountain has a real course direction: towards -Z. Banks,\n1393\t     bowls and wall rides may carry the rider almost across it, but ordinary\n1394\t     steering must never complete a U-turn and send the run back up-course.\n1395\t     These limits are deliberately close to ninety degrees so traverses remain\n1396\t     wide and expressive while forward progress stays an invariant. */\n1397\t  courseLimit: 1.48,        // radians ≈ 85°\n1398\t  brakeCourseLimit: 1.535,  // radians ≈ 88°\n1399\t  recoveryCourseLimit: 1.22,\n1400\t\n1401\t  /* BALANCE.\n1402\t\n1403\t     The lean is the balance angle the turn demands, and the body gets there\n1404\t     late because a body has mass. The gap between the two — what the corner\n1405\t     is asking for against what the rider is actually set up for — is balance,\n1406\t     and it is the one quantity in the sport that the model had nothing to say\n1407\t     about. Steady state has no gap at all: hold an edge and the body arrives\n1408\t     and the two agree. It opens in transitions, which is exactly where a\n1409\t     rider loses it in life:\n1410\t\n1411\t       under-leaned  — the load arrives before the body does, and it is trying\n1412\t                       to throw them over the outside edge. This is a carve\n1413\t                       committed to too fast, and a landing into one.\n1414\t       over-leaned   — the body is further in than the grip can justify, and\n1415\t                       the edge slips away underneath them. This is asking for\n1416\t                       a turn the snow was never going to give.\n1417\t\n1418\t     What it costs is grip, proportionally and immediately, which closes the\n1419\t     loop: losing balance loses edge, losing edge loses the turn, and the\n1420\t     recovery is to stop asking. It deliberately does not own a hidden\n1421\t     wipeout timer. On a board the player cannot independently position their\n1422\t     body, ordinary A/D edge changes must not become unexplained falls; the\n1423\t     lost grip, slide, spray and speed are the readable consequence. */\n1424\t  balanceWindow: 0.70,  // radians of mismatch that costs all of it\n1425\t  balanceGrip: 0.40,    // share of grip that balance is responsible for\n1426\t  /* At full momentum the visible balance window is this many times wider.\n1427\t     It prevents an ordinary high-speed edge transition from momentarily\n1428\t     deleting most of the grip; it does not add steady-state cornering force. */\n1429\t  speedBalanceWindow: 3.25,\n1430\t  // A railed carve is nearly free — that is the entire reason the sport\n1431\t  // prefers it to a skid — so this is small on purpose.\n1432\t  carveDrag: 0.04,\n1433\t  slideScrub: 0.9,    // per m/s of slide, per second, which is far more\n1434\t  turn: 2.4,          // rad/s at a standstill, and the cap at any speed\n1435\t  // How far past the grip limit the steering is allowed to ask. At 1.0 the\n1436\t  // board is on rails and never slips; much past 1.3 and every turn is a\n1437\t  // skid. This narrow band is where the whole handling model lives.\n1438\t  overCarve: 1.18,\n1439\t  brakePivot: 2.8,    // rad/s available to kick the board across into a stop\n1440\t  // With no steering input the board drifts back in line with where the\n1441\t  // rider is actually going. Without it a nudge leaves you very slightly\n1442\t  // sideways forever, which reads as the controls being loose.\n1443\t  selfCentre: 3.2,\n1444\t  lean: 0.55,         // radians of visual roll at full carve\n1445\t  leanRate: 7,        // how fast the body gets there — the weight has mass\n1446\t\n1447\t  /* THE BEND — what the *shape* of the ground does to the board.\n1448\t\n1449\t     This is the term the model was missing, and its absence is why a mountain\n1450\t     with four octaves of noise on it rode like a plane that happened to be\n1451\t     tilted. The load through the legs was gravity plus the corner and nothing\n1452\t     else, so a compression at the bottom of a roller and the crest of the same\n1453\t     roller put exactly the same weight through the board — which is to say the\n1454\t     hill had shape you could see and no shape you could feel.\n1455\t\n1456\t     What a surface actually does to something following it is v²·κ, where κ is\n1457\t     the curvature of the path in the vertical plane. Through a hollow the snow\n1458\t     has to push harder than gravity to bend the rider's line upwards, and the\n1459\t     rider is heavy; over a crest it pushes less, and past the point where\n1460\t     v²·κ exceeds g the ground stops being able to push at all — which is the\n1461\t     launch predictor's job and the reason nothing here needs to know about\n1462\t     jumping. It is the same equation from both ends.\n1463\t\n1464\t     Note what it is *not* computed from. The obvious cheap answer is the rate\n1465\t     of change of `climbRate`, and it is wrong: a rider accelerating down a\n1466\t     perfectly straight pitch has a climb rate growing more negative every\n1467\t     step, which that reading calls a crest and which is nothing of the kind.\n1468\t     The gradient along travel is a property of the ground and the heading\n1469\t     alone, so differentiating it *per metre travelled* rather than per second\n1470\t     gives the geometry with the speed taken out — and the speed then comes\n1471\t     back in as v², squared, where it belongs. It costs no extra samples of the\n1472\t     height field: both normals it needs are already computed every step.\n1473\t\n1474\t     Three things read it, and between them they are most of what \"reactive\"\n1475\t     means. The legs take it, so the camera dips through a compression and goes\n1476\t     light over a rollover. The edge takes it, because grip is proportional to\n1477\t     the force pressing the board into the snow — which means a carve laid into\n1478\t     a trough holds when the same carve on a crest lets go, and choosing where\n1479\t     in the terrain to turn becomes the skill it is in the sport. And a leg\n1480\t     extension released while it is high is a pump. */\n1481\t  bendSmooth: 22,     // per second — the board bridges, the legs absorb\n1482\t  bendMax: 1.8,       // g of extra load the ground is allowed to add\n1483\t  bendMin: -0.9,      // …and how light it may make the rider before the\n1484\t                      // launch predictor takes over and lets go entirely\n1485\t  /* Grip is proportional to normal force, so strictly this wants to be one.\n1486\t     It is not one because the honest figure makes a mogul field oscillate\n1487\t     between a rider on rails and a rider with nothing, forty times a run, and\n1488\t     the difference between 1.0 and 0.6 is the difference between terrain that\n1489\t     matters and terrain that is in charge. */\n1490\t  loadGrip: 0.6,\n1491\t  loadGripMin: 0.5,\n1492\t  loadGripMax: 1.6,\n1493\t\n1494\t  /* PUMPING, which is the one thing every rider on a hill does constantly and\n1495\t     no snowboarding game ever gives you a way to do.\n1496\t\n1497\t     It is already half-written: `chargeTime` loads the legs and releasing them\n1498\t     unloads. What that release does depends entirely on what the ground is\n1499\t     doing underneath it at that moment, and until now the answer was always\n1500\t     \"jump\". Released on a lip it still is, and it still gets `lipBonus`.\n1501\t     Released in a compression — where the terrain is pressing the board into\n1502\t     the snow and there is something to push *against* — the same extension\n1503\t     drives the rider forward instead, because that is the direction the legs\n1504\t     have to work in when the ground is not about to let go.\n1505\t\n1506\t     One key, two outcomes, chosen by where you are on the terrain rather than\n1507\t     by a second button. `pumpFrom` is the load below which there is nothing to\n1508\t     push against and the release is simply a small hop. */\n1509\t  pumpFrom: 0.25,     // g of bend before a release starts paying speed\n1510\t  pumpSpeed: 4.2,     // m/s from a full charge released at full load\n1511\t\n1512\t  /* Suspension. The rider's legs are a spring, and this is the part that\n1513\t     makes the hill something you feel rather than something you watch: it\n1514\t     compresses under the normal force, so a landing, a roller and a hard\n1515\t     carve each push the camera down and let it back up on their own\n1516\t     schedule. Critically damped-ish, and deliberately a little slow.\n1517\t\n1518\t     The normal force it is pulled towards is gravity, the corner and — since\n1519\t     `bendMax` above — the ground's own curvature, which is what finally makes\n1520\t     the four octaves of noise on this mountain something the legs report on\n1521\t     rather than something the eye has to take on trust. */\n1522\t  springFreq: 3.1,    // Hz\n1523\t  springDamp: 0.75,\n1524\t  compressPerG: 0.16, // metres of travel per g of normal load\n1525\t  compressMax: 0.62,\n1526\t  // How hard the rider can land before the legs stop absorbing it. Generated\n1527\t  // piste-to-piste hops reached just over 30 m/s into an unlucky face, which\n1528\t  // should be a huge speed-scrubbing recovery rather than an unexplained fall.\n1529\t  // Failed flips can still bail below this; truly larger impacts still crash.\n1530\t  softImpact: 9,      // m/s into the slope\n1531\t  hardImpact: 32,\n1532\t  // Ollie: held to charge, released to pop.\n1533\t  chargeTime: 0.45,\n1534\t  popMin: 7.0,\n1535\t  popMax: 14.0,\n1536\t  /* Popping off the lip.\n1537\t\n1538\t     A pop released in the last breath before the ground leaves is worth more\n1539\t     than the same pop released halfway up the ramp, which is the one piece\n1540\t     of timing the sport actually asks of you and the one thing the old jump\n1541\t     had no opinion about. The window is generous — a sixth of a second — and\n1542\t     it is checked against the launch rather than against the lip's geometry,\n1543\t     so it pays out identically off a kicker, a cornice and the crest of a\n1544\t     roller. */\n1545\t  lipWindow: 0.16,\n1546\t  lipBonus: 1.45,\n1547\t  // Air control. A 540 wants to fit inside a big ramp's hang time, so the\n1548\t  // spin rate is set from it.\n1549\t  spinRate: 7.5,\n1550\t  spinRamp: 3.2,      // rad/s² — the spin winds up rather than snapping on\n1551\t  flipRate: 6.2,\n1552\t  airSteer: 2.6,      // m/s² of drift, for picking a landing line\n1553\t  /* The storm leaning on an airborne rider. Multiplied by the weather's own\n1554\t     surface wind (roughly ±1.5 m/s calm, ±17 m/s in a blizzard), so a calm\n1555\t     day is imperceptible and a whiteout genuinely pushes a long air off its\n1556\t     line — the one place the wind can grab a board with no edge in the\n1557\t     snow. Grounded riding stays wind-free: grip owns the ground. */\n1558\t  windAir: 0.055,\n1559\t  /* Two rates that keep yaw corrections from teleporting the board.\n1560\t\n1561\t     `skidRecover` bounds how fast the skid and course clamps may pull\n1562\t     excess heading back once their limit is exceeded. During ordinary\n1563\t     riding the excess grows by less than this in a step, so the clamps\n1564\t     bind exactly as before; the difference is the discrete events — a\n1565\t     butter released, a brake let go — where the allowed skid shrinks by\n1566\t     sixty degrees in one input frame and the board used to snap to match.\n1567\t\n1568\t     `glideRate` drains the presentation-only remainder of a landing snap:\n1569\t     the physics still squares the board on touchdown (judging and the\n1570\t     next carve need it), but the few degrees the assist did not close\n1571\t     glide out of the *rendered* pose over about a tenth of a second\n1572\t     instead of jumping. */\n1573\t  skidRecover: 9.5,   // rad/s\n1574\t  glideRate: 11,      // per second, exponential\n1575\t  /* Landing assist.\n1576\t\n1577\t     Instead of a second key for air steering, the board finds its own way\n1578\t     home: inside the last third of a second before touchdown, and only when\n1579\t     the rider is not actively asking for more rotation, the yaw eases\n1580\t     towards whichever clean stance is nearer. It cannot rescue a spin that\n1581\t     was never going to make it — `assistRate` is slow enough that it closes\n1582\t     perhaps twenty degrees — but it is the difference between a 540 that\n1583\t     lands and a 540 that lands at 519 and gets called sketchy. */\n1584\t  assistTime: 0.35,\n1585\t  assistRate: 3.4,\n1586\t\n1587\t  /* THE GRAB, which was one reach and is now three, chosen by what the rest\n1588\t     of the body is already doing rather than by two more keys.\n1589\t\n1590\t     A grab is the only trick in the game that pays for how long you hold it,\n1591\t     and for a long time it was also the only trick with exactly one shape —\n1592\t     so a rider who had learned it had learned all of it, and holding Q was a\n1593\t     tap you made once per jump and then forgot. What the sport actually has\n1594\t     is a dozen of them, and which one you get is decided by where your weight\n1595\t     is when your hand goes down.\n1596\t\n1597\t     So that is the input. W folds the body forward and the leading hand\n1598\t     reaches past the binding for the nose; S sits the weight back, the knees\n1599\t     come up behind and the trailing hand takes the heel edge in a method;\n1600\t     neither, and the trailing hand drops straight onto the toe edge between\n1601\t     the feet, which is an indy and is what a hand does when you do not tell\n1602\t     it anything. No key was added, the two modifiers already mean forward and\n1603\t     back everywhere else in the game, and the pose the rig draws is the pose\n1604\t     the physics of the reach implies.\n1605\t\n1606\t     `reach` is how far past the nominal grab point the hand has to travel and\n1607\t     therefore how far the body has to fold; the score follows it, because\n1608\t     what makes one grab worth more than another is exactly how far out of\n1609\t     shape you have to get to hold it. */\n1610\t  grabs: [\n1611\t    // indy — trailing hand, toe edge, between the bindings\n1612\t    { name: 'INDY', reach: 1.0 },\n1613\t    // nose — leading hand, past the front binding, body folded over it\n1614\t    { name: 'NOSE GRAB', reach: 1.35 },\n1615\t    // method — trailing hand, heel edge, board pulled up behind the back\n1616\t    { name: 'METHOD', reach: 1.6 },\n1617\t  ],\n1618\t  // Seconds of hold past which a grab stops being a touch and starts being a\n1619\t  // tweak, and the second stop where it is being held for its own sake.\n1620\t  grabHold: [0.18, 0.55],\n1621\t\n1622\t  /* THE PRESS, and why the same key does it.\n1623\t\n1624\t     Everything this game scores happens in the air, and most of a run is not\n1625\t     in the air. On a plain chapter — which the terrain deliberately spends a\n1626\t     third of its length being, so that the next rough one means something —\n1627\t     there was nothing to do but hold a line and wait for the ground to change\n1628\t     its mind. That is the gap this fills, and it fills it with the one thing\n1629\t     every snowboarder does on flat ground: they stand on one end of the board\n1630\t     and spin on it.\n1631\t\n1632\t     A press is a real mechanic and not a pose, because lifting one end of the\n1633\t     board out of the snow genuinely changes three things at once, and all\n1634\t     three of them are already quantities this model has:\n1635\t\n1636\t       THE EDGE MOSTLY GOES. Half the effective edge is in the air, so the\n1637\t       grip the sidecut can ask for collapses — which is why a press held\n1638\t       through a hard carve washes out on its own and no rule had to be\n1639\t       written saying you cannot do both.\n1640\t\n1641\t       THE BOARD PIVOTS FREELY. With the tail unweighted there is nothing\n1642\t       buried to resist a rotation, so the skid clamp — the rule that a board\n1643\t       may never point more than sixty degrees off its own travel — is exactly\n1644\t       the rule a butter is defined by breaking. It opens with the press, and\n1645\t       when the board passes ninety degrees the leading end has genuinely\n1646\t       changed and the rider comes out switch.\n1647\t\n1648\t       AND IT COSTS SPEED. A board standing on its nose is ploughing with its\n1649\t       nose, and a butter is a slow trick everywhere it has ever been done.\n1650\t\n1651\t     `spinPay` is per full rotation carried through a press. It is deliberately\n1652\t     modest against a jumped 360 — this is a trick you can do anywhere, at any\n1653\t     speed, without leaving the snow, so it has to be worth doing and must\n1654\t     never be worth more than committing to a lip. */\n1655\t  pressRate: 6.5,       // per second the board comes up onto one end\n1656\t  pressRelease: 9.0,    // and settles back down a little quicker\n1657\t  pressGrip: 0.62,      // share of the edge a full press takes away\n1658\t  pressPivot: 3.4,      // rad/s of free rotation it hands back, at any speed\n1659\t  pressDrag: 2.6,       // m/s² of plough for standing on one end of the board\n1660\t  pressSkid: 3.05,      // radians the skid clamp opens to — nearly a half turn\n1661\t  pressMinSpin: 2.62,   // radians (150°) under which a butter is just a press\n1662\t  pressMinTime: 0.3,    // seconds under which it is just a wobble\n1663\t  // Landing. Anything inside the window snaps straight; anything outside\n1664\t  // is a bail. Switch is the same window rotated half a turn.\n1665\t  landWindow: 0.92,   // radians ≈ 53°\n1666\t  landPitchWindow: 0.85,\n1667\t  // How far off the ground the rider's ballistic path has to be, ninety\n1668\t  // milliseconds out, before the hill counts as having dropped away.\n1669\t  launchGap: 0.12,\n1670\t  /* Sample that look-ahead in distance as well as time. At 50 m/s four time\n1671\t     samples were more than a metre apart and could leap over the support\n1672\t     between two tiny terrain crests. A real board also bridges a short hollow,\n1673\t     so an unmarked natural release needs a continuous clear run before it is\n1674\t     allowed to become flight. Explicit kicker/cliff edges bypass that second\n1675\t     condition and release directly from their trailing tangent. */\n1676\t  launchSampleSpacing: 0.30,\n1677\t  // The 250 m/s ceiling still needs a bounded predictor budget. Normal play\n1678\t  // is far below this count; only extreme powered speeds widen the sweep.\n1679\t  launchSampleMax: 192,\n1680\t  launchSupportLength: 0.68,\n1681\t  launchSupportShare: 0.55,\n1682\t  // Any meaningful penetration means the ramp still obstructs the ballistic\n1683\t  // path. Half a millimetre ignores height-field rounding without allowing a\n1684\t  // convex face to release the rider before its lip.\n1685\t  launchObstruction: 0.0005,\n1686\t  /* Scan far enough ahead to recognise an edge before an underpowered rider\n1687\t     can exhaust their momentum or trip the steep-slope stall rule. The test\n1688\t     compares the future snow with the trailing tangent, so an ordinary steep\n1689\t     pitch is never mistaken for a lip. */\n1690\t  lipCommitDistance: 2.0,\n1691\t  /* Inside that final approach to a lip, preserve (but never increase) the\n1692\t     speed carried into each step when it is below this value. This is the\n1693\t     arcade commitment that prevents an underpowered rider being parked\n1694\t     upright on a ramp edge; the eventual launch still uses that retained\n1695\t     speed and the real tangent. */\n1696\t  lipCommitSpeed: 4.0,\n1697\t  /* The launch predictor can commit on the integration step that crosses the\n1698\t     lip. Give that decision a tiny contact window so floating-point overlap\n1699\t     cannot immediately \"re-land\" against the same ramp. */\n1700\t  launchContactGrace: 0.03,\n1701\t  /* HOW FAR INSIDE THE MOUNTAIN A RIDER MAY BE BEFORE IT COUNTS AS SNOW,\n1702\t     whatever direction they are travelling.\n1703\t\n1704\t     The touchdown test asks for a contact that is both *approaching* and\n1705\t     *descending*, and the second half of that has a hole in it. A rider\n1706\t     flying up a containment wall is approaching its surface — the wall's\n1707\t     normal leans in towards them — but they are not descending, so the test\n1708\t     refuses the contact and the ballistic position is left where it was.\n1709\t     That is fine for one step. It is not fine for eighty, and if the ground\n1710\t     is rising faster than the rider is, eighty is what it takes: the soak\n1711\t     found riders three and a half metres inside the hill and still sinking.\n1712\t\n1713\t     `descending` is not wrong, it is just doing two jobs. It is there to\n1714\t     protect a pop — a rider who ollies off an uphill transition is rising\n1715\t     into ground that is still rising, and re-landing them immediately would\n1716\t     take the trick away. So it stays, and this is the backstop underneath\n1717\t     it: a pop clears the snow within a few centimetres or it was never a\n1718\t     pop, and nothing legitimate is ever half a metre under it. */\n1719\t  buryDepth: 0.5,\n1720\t  // A hop small enough not to count: below this the landing is never\n1721\t  // judged, so chattering over moguls can never end a combo.\n1722\t  minJudgedAir: 0.34,\n1723\t  radius: 0.55,\n1724\t  height: 1.75,\n1725\t  /* Falling over is the only failure state, and it is temporary — the run\n1726\t     never ends, the rider just gets up.\n1727\t\n1728\t     It used to be a timer. The rider dropped to 28% of their speed, slid for\n1729\t     1.35 seconds whatever had happened to them, and stood up — so catching a\n1730\t     trunk at 30 km/h and catching one at 170 looked and cost exactly the\n1731\t     same, which is the one thing a crash must never do.\n1732\t\n1733\t     Now a fall is a body. Whatever speed was going into the obstacle is\n1734\t     turned partly into height and partly into a tumble, and from that moment\n1735\t     the rider is ballistic: gravity, air drag, and a bounce every time the\n1736\t     snow arrives, each one lower than the last. They are down until they\n1737\t     have actually stopped, so a slow spill is over in a second and a bad one\n1738\t     at speed throws them a long way down the hill before it lets go. */\n1739\t  riseTime: 0.55,\n1740\t  fallLaunch: 0.62,   // share of the speed the body keeps out of the impact\n1741\t  fallLift: 0.40,     // and the share of it turned into height\n1742\t  fallBounce: 0.34,   // restitution against the snow, per contact\n1743\t  fallDrag: 0.16,     // air, per second, while tumbling\n1744\t  fallFriction: 8.5,  // m/s² once it is sliding rather than flying\n1745\t  fallRest: 3.2,      // m/s under which the rider is allowed to get up\n1746\t  fallMin: 0.7,       // seconds they are down no matter how gentle it was\n1747\t  fallMax: 5.0,       // and the longest any tumble is allowed to run\n1748\t\n1749\t  /* Hitting things.\n1750\t\n1751\t     A collision used to be judged on where on the trunk you caught it and\n1752\t     nothing else, so a graze at walking pace and the same graze at 120 km/h\n1753\t     were the same event. Now the severity is the speed actually being\n1754\t     carried *into* the obstacle, which is the quantity that hurts, and the\n1755\t     two thresholds below are where it stops being a wobble and where it\n1756\t     stops being survivable. Direction still matters — it decides how much of\n1757\t     the speed is aimed at the tree in the first place. */\n1758\t  brushSpeed: 7,      // m/s into a solid below which it is barely a knock\n1759\t  wipeoutSpeed: 15,   // and above which the rider is going down\n1760\t};\n1761\t\n1762\texport const SCORE = {\n1763\t  perDegree: 1.0,\n1764\t  perFlip: 500,\n1765\t  grabPerSecond: 260,\n1766\t  airPerSecond: 70,\n1767\t  switchBonus: 1.5,\n1768\t  /* Taking a spin off its axis, which is the difference between a rotation\n1769\t     and a trick worth a name. A flip laid into a spin is much harder to see\n1770\t     out of and much harder to land than either of them alone, and until this\n1771\t     existed the game paid for it as though the two had happened one after the\n1772\t     other. Multiplied, not added, so it scales with whatever was already\n1773\t     being attempted. */\n1774\t  corkBonus: 0.45,\n1775\t  /* Buttering, per full rotation carried through a press. Deliberately small\n1776\t     against a jumped 360: this is a trick you can do on flat ground at any\n1777\t     speed without leaving the snow, so it must be worth doing and must never\n1778\t     be worth more than committing to a lip. */\n1779\t  butterPerTurn: 240,\n1780\t  /* Risk is worth something. Fast tricks climb to a 1.5× premium, while a\n1781\t     pop released on the lip gets its own timing bonus. Neither creates\n1782\t     points by itself; they only amplify something the player actually lands. */\n1783\t  speedBonusFrom: 18,   // m/s\n1784\t  speedBonusFull: 45,\n1785\t  speedBonus: 0.5,\n1786\t  lipBonus: 1.25,\n1787\t  // Scales the three one-shot milestone awards (100 km/h, 1,000 m, 5,000 m)\n1788\t  // in main.js — the only thing it still does. The old \"multiplier per clean\n1789\t  // landing\" mechanism it once named is gone: the combo is derived from the\n1790\t  // flow meter now (see `comboFor` in flow.js).\n1791\t  comboStep: 1,\n1792\t  comboMax: 12,\n1793\t  /* FLOW, which is now the multiplier itself rather than a speed governor\n1794\t     with a number beside it. See `flow.js`.\n1795\t\n1796\t     `flowPerPoint` is the exchange rate from a trick's payout into meter,\n1797\t     applied to the SQUARE ROOT of the payout so that a trick worth ten\n1798\t     times another does not fill the bar ten times faster — one enormous\n1799\t     air would end the progression and everything after it would be\n1800\t     decoration. A 60-point minimum trick is worth half a per cent, a\n1801\t     landed 900 with a grab about a sixth of the bar, and a 4,000-point\n1802\t     switch cork better than a quarter of it. `flowGate` and `flowButter`\n1803\t     are the flat awards for the two things that are not scored by size.\n1804\t     `flowBail` is what a wipeout costs — not everything, because losing a\n1805\t     full meter to one caught edge is the kind of punishment that stops\n1806\t     people trying tricks at all, which is the opposite of the point. */\n1807\t  flowPerPoint: 0.0042,\n1808\t  /* A gate is worth little meter because it costs little: every gate spans\n1809\t     the whole piste, so the ladder is really a count of how long the run\n1810\t     has stayed on it. At 0.035 a clean descent with no tricks at all was\n1811\t     filling the bar by itself. */\n1812\t  flowGate: 0.012,\n1813\t  flowButter: 0.05,\n1814\t  /* What riding alone is worth, and how the rest of the bar is held.\n1815\t\n1816\t     `flowCruise` is the level clean, fast carving settles at — ×3 — and the\n1817\t     riding terms ease off over the last `flowCruiseEase` of it rather than\n1818\t     stopping at a wall. Above it only awards fill the bar. Each one holds\n1819\t     the meter still for `flowHold` seconds of riding, after which the\n1820\t     surplus relaxes back towards cruise at `flowFade` of itself a second:\n1821\t     stop doing things and a full bar is back near ×4 in about half a\n1822\t     minute, which is long enough to find the next jump and short enough\n1823\t     that the multiplier describes the run as it is now. */\n1824\t  flowCruise: 0.28,\n1825\t  flowCruiseEase: 0.5,\n1826\t  flowHold: 4.0,\n1827\t  flowFade: 0.08,\n1828\t  flowSketchy: 0.35,   // share of a clean landing's flow a sketchy one earns\n1829\t  flowBail: 0.55,      // share of the meter a wipeout takes\n1830\t  /* And what it costs to spend. W converts flow into speed: while the tuck\n1831\t     is held the meter drains at this rate, and the powered floor it buys\n1832\t     scales with what is left. Banking flow for the multiplier and burning\n1833\t     it for a fast line are now the same currency, which is the whole of\n1834\t     the decision this rework exists to create. */\n1835\t  flowTuckDrain: 0.085,\n1836\t  // Below this a landing is just a landing: no banner, no combo, no points.\n1837\t  minTrickScore: 60,\n1838\t  nearMiss: 40,\n1839\t\n1840\t  /* Threading a slalom gate, and the ladder that consecutive ones build.\n1841\t\n1842\t     Each gate taken without missing one is worth more than the last, and a\n1843\t     miss puts you back to the bottom. That is the difference between a row of\n1844\t     scoring gates and a course: a single gate is worth about a third of a\n1845\t     modest trick, and a run of six is worth committing a whole line to. The\n1846\t     ladder is capped so a very long straight of them cannot outrun the rest\n1847\t     of the scoring. */\n1848\t  gate: 120,\n1849\t  gateRunMax: 8,\n1850\t  /* Stopping for a cocoa, which is worth about a landed 540.\n1851\t\n1852\t     It has to be worth that much because of what it costs: coming to a\n1853\t     genuine standstill on a pitch means scrubbing off everything you have\n1854\t     and then climbing back into it from nothing, and the run is built to\n1855\t     punish exactly that. Paying a combo step rather than resetting the\n1856\t     multiplier is the same reasoning — stopping at a hut is a decision, not\n1857\t     a crash. */\n1858\t  cocoa: 1500,\n1859\t};\n1860\t\n1861\texport const PROPS = {\n1862\t  band: 40,           // metres of hill filled at a time\n1863\t  ahead: 11,          // bands kept in front of the rider"}]},"uuid":"398646ec-9f70-49af-9d37-015bc3d599fb","timestamp":"2026-09-24T19:54:50.101Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen/js/config.js","content":"export const RIDER = {\n  /* A deliberately brisk arcade gravity, but a real constant: it is resolved\n     along the snow while grounded and applied straight down on every airborne\n     step. Jump height and airtime therefore come from takeoff speed and ramp\n     tangent rather than from an apex-specific hang-time assist. */\n  gravity: 22,\n\n  // Snow under a waxed base. The number is real; the grade does the rest.\n  /* WHAT THE SURFACE IS WORTH, as a top speed rather than only as drag.\n\n     The piste is a machine-made ribbon of hard, fast snow and the mountain\n     either side of it is not, and the model barely said so: open powder\n     dragged 1.15x a groomed run, which at speed is a rounding error, and\n     the speed ceiling ignored the surface completely. So the fastest line\n     down the hill was wherever the fall line happened to point, and the\n     corduroy the whole course is built around was worth nothing to ride.\n\n     These are multipliers on the flow-unlocked ceiling: the groomed ribbon\n     runs a little over it, settled powder is held well under, and the talus\n     and scoured rock under it is slower still. Ice keeps piste speed - it\n     is the fastest surface on the hill, and it is where the grip is not.\n     Combined with the drag figures below, the piste is now the fast line\n     and leaving it costs real speed rather than a little texture. */\n  pisteSpeed: 1.12,\n  powderSpeed: 0.60,\n  iceSpeed: 1.10,\n  rockSpeed: 0.50,\n  friction: 0.045,\n  brakeFriction: 0.62,\n  /* A one-button heel-side speed check.\n\n     S is not reverse and it is not an invisible friction switch. The rider\n     progressively pivots the board across the direction of travel, sets an\n     edge and lets the base sideslip while that edge scrubs speed. A/D held as\n     braking begins chooses the side; otherwise the current carve continues,\n     falling back to the rider's natural heel side from a straight line. */\n  brakeAngle: 1.34,       // radians ≈ 77° across travel at full pressure\n  brakeEdge: 0.72,        // share of the currently holdable edge angle\n  brakeEngage: 9.0,       // pressure build-up per second\n  brakeRelease: 12.0,     // releasing the edge is a little quicker\n  brakeSlideDamping: 0.45, // controlled sideslip; kinetic friction does the stop\n  brakeCompress: 0.24,    // low, weighted stance while checking speed\n  // Drag is what sets ordinary top speed, not a clamp: 6.3 m/s² of slope\n  // pull balances at about 40 m/s, or 143 km/h.\n  drag: 0.0034,\n  /* Flow owns the speed ceiling. With no flow the old 50 m/s limit remains;\n     a full meter opens it to five times that value. */\n  baseMaxSpeed: 50,   // m/s — 180 km/h\n  /* The ceiling flow unlocks. It was 250 m/s — nine hundred kilometres an\n     hour, which is not a snowboard and not steerable; the number existed\n     because flow was the only thing it fed and nothing else bounded it.\n     Flow is the score multiplier now (see `stepFlow` in main.js) and speed\n     is something you SPEND it on, so the ceiling only has to be worth\n     spending on: ninety-six metres a second is three hundred and forty-six\n     kilometres an hour at a full meter, which is still faster than anything\n     else on the hill and still a line you can hold. */\n  maxSpeed: 96,\n  // Where drag stiffens beyond v² when the powered tuck is not held.\n  // A big kicker landing converts a lot of height into speed and can\n  // overshoot this for a second or two; past it the run is pulled back\n  // rather than allowed to keep everything it just found.\n  dragKnee: 50,       // m/s — 180 km/h\n  /* MOMENTUM STABILITY.\n\n     Speed makes the board more committed, not more nervous. These are the\n     endpoints of one eased 0..1 signal used only by the parts of the model\n     that should settle with momentum: the board-length handling normal and\n     the rider's tolerance for a short balance transient. It never raises the\n     edge's lateral grip or weakens gravity, so a fast line stays wide and a\n     fast jump stays ballistic. */\n  stabilityFrom: 8,        // m/s — below this, the board remains nimble\n  stabilityFull: 45,       // m/s — full high-speed composure\n  contactHalfLength: 0.90, // metres sampled fore/aft at full stability\n  handlingResponseMin: 0.035, // seconds of surface-normal inertia\n  handlingResponseMax: 0.115,\n  /* One-foot skating when the board has almost stopped.\n\n     The old recovery was an invisible constant force below 2 m/s. It kept a\n     run from getting stranded, but the board simply gathered speed while the\n     rider stood still. Recovery is now a real sequence: hysteresis starts a\n     skating cycle at walking pace, each planted rear-foot stroke contributes\n     one measured impulse, and the foot finishes its current cycle before it\n     returns to the binding. On a flat surface the impulses still beat base\n     friction, while a descending pitch contributes its own gravity as usual. */\n  pushStart: 3.0,       // m/s — begin skating below 10.8 km/h\n  pushStop: 6.5,        // m/s — finish the stroke above 23.4 km/h\n  pushCadence: 1.55,    // complete rear-foot cycles per second\n  pushImpulse: 1.55,    // m/s added at the planted part of each stroke\n  pushPlant: 0.54,      // 0..1 phase at which the foot drives through snow\n  /* Climbing.\n\n     Gravity on the tangent already takes g·sin θ off a rider going uphill,\n     which is the whole of the real physics and, on a hill this shallow, not\n     very much: 5.6 m/s² on the average pitch. What it misses is that a board\n     pointed up a slope is also being driven into it, and a loaded edge in\n     soft snow scrubs. This is that — extra deceleration proportional to how\n     hard the rider is climbing — and it is what makes riding a wall a\n     decision with a price rather than a free way to change lanes. */\n  climbScrub: 7.5,\n\n  /* THE BOUNDARY MOUNTAINS, and the one thing the terrain alone could not\n     promise about them.\n\n     `TERRAIN.wall` contains by shape: past the quarterpipe lip the ground\n     rises asymptotically towards sixty-two metres and then creeps upwards for\n     ever, so gravity outside the corridor always points home. The arithmetic\n     for that was written against a terminal speed of fifty metres a second,\n     which buys v²/2g = fifty-seven metres of climb against the sixty-two the\n     exponential saturates at. Comfortable.\n\n     Full flow now raises that ceiling fivefold. The kinetic energy available\n     to a determined rider still beats any finite wall shaped for 50 m/s. At\n     eighty metres a second the same sum buys a hundred and forty-five metres,\n     and the containment is a suggestion.\n\n     What is missing is not more mountain. It is that the ground out there\n     stops being a piste. Everything inside the lip is machine-groomed; past\n     it is unpisted, wind-loaded, bottomless alpine snow, and the honest\n     difference between the two is not a few per cent of friction — a board\n     driven into deep snow at speed sinks, ploughs and stops climbing almost\n     at once. That is what these three numbers are, and they are why there is\n     still no invisible wall, no bounce and no teleport: the rider may ride\n     out there, may launch off the lip, may land out there and slide back\n     down. What they cannot do is *climb*, because there is nothing solid\n     enough under the board to climb on.\n\n     `span` is the depth over which the surface goes from groomed to\n     bottomless, `drag` the deceleration at the far end of it, and `grab` the\n     rate at which the uphill component of a rider's velocity is taken away\n     once they are in it — the last of which is the actual invariant, since it\n     is applied after the powered tuck's floor and therefore cannot be\n     outrun by any speed however it was obtained.\n\n     ALL THREE WENT UP, and the measurement that moved them is worth keeping.\n     A rider tucking and steering deliberately at the left bank reached 52.5\n     metres above the piste — most of a 62-metre wall, which is to say they\n     were climbing the mountain rather than riding the bank of the run. The\n     same trial now tops out at 23.5 m, and the three hands-off trials that\n     bracket it moved by tenths, which is the shape the change had to have:\n     the quarterpipe a rider *uses* is the first ten metres past the lip and\n     nothing above that was ever meant to be reachable. Deeper snow sooner,\n     and it refuses harder once you are in it. */\n  wallSpan: 12,\n  /* What the deep snow costs a rider trying to gain height in it, on top of\n     `climbScrub`. It is charged against the climb rather than flat against\n     the speed, and that is not a detail: a flat term large enough to stop a\n     fast rider is also larger than the 6.5 m/s² the creep slope has to offer\n     going the other way, which would strand anybody thrown out there by a\n     cliff instead of walking them home. Deep snow is slow to climb through\n     and merely slow to descend through. */\n  wallDrag: 30,\n  /* And the edge. This is the one that decides the *shape* of the failure\n     rather than its cost: unpisted wind-loaded snow will not hold a carved\n     edge across the fall line at all, so a board out there washes out and\n     goes wherever gravity sends it, which on the containment wall is home.\n     Without it a rider can hold a level traverse along the bank for ever —\n     not climbing, and not needing to, because the run itself descends nearly\n     a third of a metre for every metre of z and simply leaves them up there.\n     That was the actual escape, and it is a grip problem rather than a climb\n     one. This is the share of the edge the deepest snow takes away. */\n  wallWash: 0.88,\n  /* The refusal itself: the rate at which the component of travel pointing up\n     the fall line is taken back out. It saturates with depth — see the note\n     in `rider.js` — so past `wallSpan` metres the answer is simply no. */\n  wallGrab: 11.0,\n  /* And the sluff, which is the term that finally closed the last way out.\n\n     Refusing the climb is not enough on a run that descends nearly a third of\n     a metre for every metre of z: a rider who merely holds their altitude out\n     on the bank is left further and further above the piste without ever\n     having gained a centimetre, and the wander then carries the run out from\n     under them as well. Sixty metres up the containment wall, having done\n     nothing but hold a line.\n\n     What is missing from that picture is the snow. Metre-deep unconsolidated\n     powder on a forty-degree bank does not sit still under a board — it\n     fractures and runs, and it takes the board down with it. That is a\n     sluff, it is the single most characteristic thing steep off-piste snow\n     does, and it points down the fall line, which on the containment wall is\n     the way home. So it contains without ever pushing anybody anywhere they\n     were not already being pulled, and it can never strand a rider, because\n     the direction it acts in is the one they want to go. */\n  wallSluff: 10.5,\n  /* And the pivot, which is the one that does the containing. A board buried\n     across the fall line has a metre of edge on the uphill side and nothing\n     under the downhill one, so it swings to point down the hill — see the\n     long note in `rider.js`. As an exponential rate: a third of a second to\n     come round at full depth, and nothing at all inside the lip. */\n  wallPivot: 3.0,\n\n  /* Where climbing stops being slow and starts being impossible.\n\n     A snowboard is not a climbing tool. Run out of speed pointing up\n     something steep and you do not gently stop — the board stalls, the edge\n     that was holding you across the hill has nothing left to hold, and you\n     go over. Below `stallSlope` the rider is allowed to grind to a halt and\n     skate away again; above it they need\n     `stallSpeed` at that reference slope and proportionally more the steeper\n     it gets. Riding a quarterpipe wall is therefore a commitment: arrive\n     with enough and you get thrown off the lip, arrive without it and the\n     wall puts you down. */\n  stallSlope: 0.32,\n  stallSpeed: 7.5,\n  /* …and the two numbers that stop that from being a trap.\n\n     A kicker's face climbs at a slope of about 0.9 and a steep knoll is not\n     far off it, so a requirement that scales without limit means the rider\n     needs 76 km/h to ride up a two-metre jump without falling over — which\n     turns every launcher on the mountain into a wipeout and, worse, looks\n     exactly like the jump physics being broken. `stallCap` is the most speed\n     the rule is ever allowed to ask for.\n\n     And it has to persist. A ramp is under the board for a fifth of a second\n     at riding speed, so anything judged instantaneously fires on features\n     the rider is simply passing over. A third of a second of continuously\n     being too slow for the ground you are pointed up is a stall; less than\n     that is a kicker. */\n  /* The speed under which a rider has nothing left to stall *with*, and is\n     simply being walked back downhill by the recovery skate. Without this the\n     rule loops: fall, get up crawling, stall again, forever. */\n  stallMinSpeed: 3.5,\n  /* And the speed under which a stopped rider's board is allowed to ease\n     round to the fall line on its own. Well under walking pace: any higher\n     and the board swings downhill while real momentum is still carrying the\n     rider the way they were going, which is the \"he drives backwards\" that\n     started this. */\n  fallLineSpeed: 1.6,\n  stallCap: 9.0,\n  stallTime: 0.32,\n  // The powered tuck. Holding it guarantees this much additional speed each\n  // second and bypasses aerodynamic drag until the flow-owned cap. The edge\n  // still goes soft and the board stops answering quickly.\n  tuckAcceleration: 7.5, // m/s², accumulated for as long as W is held\n  tuckTurn: 0.45,     // share of the turn rate left while folded down\n  tuckGrip: 0.72,     // and of the grip\n  tuckCompress: 0.34, // metres of squat, which the camera rides down with\n  /* Fresh snow is slower and less supportive than the groomed surface. The\n     weather feeds these endpoints continuously, so a front arriving is felt\n     in the edge before it ever becomes a discrete \"blizzard\" label. */\n  stormGrip: 0.80,       // share of clear-weather edge hold in a whiteout\n  stormFriction: 1.45,   // share of base friction in deep fresh snow\n  // Edge grip, in m/s² of sideways hold. Past it the board washes out and\n  // the rider slides — which is where the spray, and the lost speed, are.\n  // This one number is most of how the game feels: raise it and the board\n  // is on rails, lower it and every turn is a drift.\n  /* Edge grip, in m/s² of sideways hold, and it is the number that decides\n     how sharply the board answers at all. The turn rate a carve produces is\n     grip/v, so this is the whole of the game's steering authority: at 27\n     against 39 m/s the heading came round at forty degrees a second, which is\n     honest for 140 km/h on real snow and reads, from behind, as a rider who\n     has leaned over and kept going straight. 34 buys back most of the\n     response without putting the board on rails — the wash-out is still one\n     firm input away, because what breaks traction is v² and that has not\n     moved. */\n  grip: 34,\n\n  /* THE CARVE, as geometry rather than as a steering rate.\n\n     A snowboard does not turn because it is pointed somewhere. It turns\n     because it has a sidecut — the edges are arcs, not straight lines — and\n     when the board is tilted up on one of them and pressed into the snow,\n     that arc is what the board is forced to travel along. Everything about\n     how the sport feels falls out of one equation:\n\n         R = sidecut / sin(edge angle)\n\n     Lay the board almost flat and the arc it describes is enormous, so it\n     runs nearly straight. Roll it up onto its edge and the radius collapses\n     towards the sidecut itself. The rider's only real input is how far over\n     they put it.\n\n     What makes that worth doing rather than steering directly is what comes\n     out of it without being written. Holding a radius R at v metres a second\n     needs v²/R of lateral grip, which goes as the *square* of the speed — so\n     the same edge angle that carves a beautiful arc at 40 km/h asks four\n     times as much of the snow at 80 and simply tears out. The rider is not\n     told this; they discover that a fast line has to be a wide line, which\n     is the single truest thing about the sport and previously had to be\n     faked with a cap on the turn rate.\n\n     It also gives the lean away for free. A rider holding a turn has to\n     incline until the resultant of gravity and the corner runs down the line\n     of their legs — atan(lateral / g) — so the body angle stops being a\n     cosmetic constant and becomes a read-out of how hard the turn actually\n     is. Nobody has to animate it and it can never disagree with the physics. */\n  /* Steering at walking pace, which a sidecut cannot do.\n\n     A carve's turn rate is v·sin(edge)/sidecut — proportional to speed — so\n     as the rider slows the board stops being able to turn at all. That is\n     correct for carving and on its own it is a trap, and it was a real one:\n     hold a turn and the rider carves round onto a traverse, the turn rate\n     decays with the speed, gravity has no component along a board pointed\n     across the hill, and they coast to a dead stop pointing sideways with no\n     way to bring the nose back down the fall line. Measured: 61 km/h to zero\n     in six seconds on an ordinary 18° pitch, and then stuck there.\n\n     What a rider actually does below about walking pace is stop carving and\n     skid the board round — pivot it flat, with the tail washing out. So this\n     is a second, speed-independent steering authority that fades *in* as the\n     carve fades out. Above `pivotSpeed` it is gone entirely and the sidecut\n     is the only thing turning the board, which is the model that matters. */\n  pivotRate: 2.2,     // rad/s of skid-steer available at a standstill\n  pivotSpeed: 9.0,    // m/s by which it has faded to nothing\n\n  sidecut: 8.0,       // metres — a real all-mountain board is 7 to 9\n  edgeMax: 1.15,      // radians ≈ 66°, about as far over as anyone gets\n  // How fast the board is rolled onto its edge and off it. Quick, because the\n  // sidecut now limits what the edge can *ask* for — so there is no longer any\n  // need to protect the rider from their own input by making it sluggish.\n  edgeRate: 11.0,\n  /* Cutting a trench is not free. A carved edge is slicing through snow\n     rather than gliding over it, and the deeper it is set the more of it is\n     in the way — this is that, and it is why holding a hard carve all the way\n     down a pitch costs speed a straight line would have kept. */\n  edgeDrag: 3.2,\n  /* How far past the edge angle the snow can actually support the rider is\n     allowed to push. This is the narrow band the whole handling model lives\n     in: at 1.0 the board is on rails and never slips, and much past 1.15\n     every turn is a skid. A few per cent of overdrive is where the edge\n     starts to wash and throw powder without letting go. */\n  edgeReach: 1.10,\n  /* An edge only bites because the board is moving.\n\n     Grip had no speed term at all, which meant a stationary board had the\n     same 34 m/s² of lateral hold as one at full song — and that is enough to\n     stand a rider sideways on a forty-four degree wall indefinitely. It is\n     exactly how a run got marooned: come to a crawl somewhere steep, the edge\n     holds across the fall line, the old floor force trickles you along its\n     threshold, and you creep across the mountain at seven km/h for ever with\n     gravity unable to get a word in. Observed on a\n     wall at 43.8°, velocity pinned at 2.0 m/s, indefinitely.\n\n     A real edge at walking pace does not hold; the board washes out sideways\n     and you slide down the fall line, which is the recovery. So the hold\n     ramps in with speed and keeps a floor, because a board with literally no\n     grip cannot be steered at all and standing still would become a slow\n     slide to the bottom of the mountain. */\n  gripSpeed: 7.0,     // m/s at which the edge is fully engaged\n  gripLow: 0.22,      // and the share of it a nearly-stopped board keeps\n  /* And the furthest the board is ever allowed to point away from the\n     direction it is genuinely travelling. Sixty degrees is about as far\n     across the fall line as anyone can hold one and still be riding it; past\n     that it is not a snowboard, it is a sledge. Braking gets more room to set\n     the board across the hill, but never enough to pivot through the direction\n     of travel and start running tail-first. */\n  maxSkid: 1.05,      // radians ≈ 60°\n  brakeSkid: 1.43,    // radians ≈ 82° — broad speed check, still one stance\n\n  /* The generated mountain has a real course direction: towards -Z. Banks,\n     bowls and wall rides may carry the rider almost across it, but ordinary\n     steering must never complete a U-turn and send the run back up-course.\n     These limits are deliberately close to ninety degrees so traverses remain\n     wide and expressive while forward progress stays an invariant. */\n  courseLimit: 1.48,        // radians ≈ 85°\n  brakeCourseLimit: 1.535,  // radians ≈ 88°\n  recoveryCourseLimit: 1.22,\n\n  /* BALANCE.\n\n     The lean is the balance angle the turn demands, and the body gets there\n     late because a body has mass. The gap between the two — what the corner\n     is asking for against what the rider is actually set up for — is balance,\n     and it is the one quantity in the sport that the model had nothing to say\n     about. Steady state has no gap at all: hold an edge and the body arrives\n     and the two agree. It opens in transitions, which is exactly where a\n     rider loses it in life:\n\n       under-leaned  — the load arrives before the body does, and it is trying\n                       to throw them over the outside edge. This is a carve\n                       committed to too fast, and a landing into one.\n       over-leaned   — the body is further in than the grip can justify, and\n                       the edge slips away underneath them. This is asking for\n                       a turn the snow was never going to give.\n\n     What it costs is grip, proportionally and immediately, which closes the\n     loop: losing balance loses edge, losing edge loses the turn, and the\n     recovery is to stop asking. It deliberately does not own a hidden\n     wipeout timer. On a board the player cannot independently position their\n     body, ordinary A/D edge changes must not become unexplained falls; the\n     lost grip, slide, spray and speed are the readable consequence. */\n  balanceWindow: 0.70,  // radians of mismatch that costs all of it\n  balanceGrip: 0.40,    // share of grip that balance is responsible for\n  /* At full momentum the visible balance window is this many times wider.\n     It prevents an ordinary high-speed edge transition from momentarily\n     deleting most of the grip; it does not add steady-state cornering force. */\n  speedBalanceWindow: 3.25,\n  // A railed carve is nearly free — that is the entire reason the sport\n  // prefers it to a skid — so this is small on purpose.\n  carveDrag: 0.04,\n  slideScrub: 0.9,    // per m/s of slide, per second, which is far more\n  turn: 2.4,          // rad/s at a standstill, and the cap at any speed\n  // How far past the grip limit the steering is allowed to ask. At 1.0 the\n  // board is on rails and never slips; much past 1.3 and every turn is a\n  // skid. This narrow band is where the whole handling model lives.\n  overCarve: 1.18,\n  brakePivot: 2.8,    // rad/s available to kick the board across into a stop\n  // With no steering input the board drifts back in line with where the\n  // rider is actually going. Without it a nudge leaves you very slightly\n  // sideways forever, which reads as the controls being loose.\n  selfCentre: 3.2,\n  lean: 0.55,         // radians of visual roll at full carve\n  leanRate: 7,        // how fast the body gets there — the weight has mass\n\n  /* THE BEND — what the *shape* of the ground does to the board.\n\n     This is the term the model was missing, and its absence is why a mountain\n     with four octaves of noise on it rode like a plane that happened to be\n     tilted. The load through the legs was gravity plus the corner and nothing\n     else, so a compression at the bottom of a roller and the crest of the same\n     roller put exactly the same weight through the board — which is to say the\n     hill had shape you could see and no shape you could feel.\n\n     What a surface actually does to something following it is v²·κ, where κ is\n     the curvature of the path in the vertical plane. Through a hollow the snow\n     has to push harder than gravity to bend the rider's line upwards, and the\n     rider is heavy; over a crest it pushes less, and past the point where\n     v²·κ exceeds g the ground stops being able to push at all — which is the\n     launch predictor's job and the reason nothing here needs to know about\n     jumping. It is the same equation from both ends.\n\n     Note what it is *not* computed from. The obvious cheap answer is the rate\n     of change of `climbRate`, and it is wrong: a rider accelerating down a\n     perfectly straight pitch has a climb rate growing more negative every\n     step, which that reading calls a crest and which is nothing of the kind.\n     The gradient along travel is a property of the ground and the heading\n     alone, so differentiating it *per metre travelled* rather than per second\n     gives the geometry with the speed taken out — and the speed then comes\n     back in as v², squared, where it belongs. It costs no extra samples of the\n     height field: both normals it needs are already computed every step.\n\n     Three things read it, and between them they are most of what \"reactive\"\n     means. The legs take it, so the camera dips through a compression and goes\n     light over a rollover. The edge takes it, because grip is proportional to\n     the force pressing the board into the snow — which means a carve laid into\n     a trough holds when the same carve on a crest lets go, and choosing where\n     in the terrain to turn becomes the skill it is in the sport. And a leg\n     extension released while it is high is a pump. */\n  bendSmooth: 22,     // per second — the board bridges, the legs absorb\n  bendMax: 1.8,       // g of extra load the ground is allowed to add\n  bendMin: -0.9,      // …and how light it may make the rider before the\n                      // launch predictor takes over and lets go entirely\n  /* Grip is proportional to normal force, so strictly this wants to be one.\n     It is not one because the honest figure makes a mogul field oscillate\n     between a rider on rails and a rider with nothing, forty times a run, and\n     the difference between 1.0 and 0.6 is the difference between terrain that\n     matters and terrain that is in charge. */\n  loadGrip: 0.6,\n  loadGripMin: 0.5,\n  loadGripMax: 1.6,\n\n  /* PUMPING, which is the one thing every rider on a hill does constantly and\n     no snowboarding game ever gives you a way to do.\n\n     It is already half-written: `chargeTime` loads the legs and releasing them\n     unloads. What that release does depends entirely on what the ground is\n     doing underneath it at that moment, and until now the answer was always\n     \"jump\". Released on a lip it still is, and it still gets `lipBonus`.\n     Released in a compression — where the terrain is pressing the board into\n     the snow and there is something to push *against* — the same extension\n     drives the rider forward instead, because that is the direction the legs\n     have to work in when the ground is not about to let go.\n\n     One key, two outcomes, chosen by where you are on the terrain rather than\n     by a second button. `pumpFrom` is the load below which there is nothing to\n     push against and the release is simply a small hop. */\n  pumpFrom: 0.25,     // g of bend before a release starts paying speed\n  pumpSpeed: 4.2,     // m/s from a full charge released at full load\n\n  /* Suspension. The rider's legs are a spring, and this is the part that\n     makes the hill something you feel rather than something you watch: it\n     compresses under the normal force, so a landing, a roller and a hard\n     carve each push the camera down and let it back up on their own\n     schedule. Critically damped-ish, and deliberately a little slow.\n\n     The normal force it is pulled towards is gravity, the corner and — since\n     `bendMax` above — the ground's own curvature, which is what finally makes\n     the four octaves of noise on this mountain something the legs report on\n     rather than something the eye has to take on trust. */\n  springFreq: 3.1,    // Hz\n  springDamp: 0.75,\n  compressPerG: 0.16, // metres of travel per g of normal load\n  compressMax: 0.62,\n  // How hard the rider can land before the legs stop absorbing it. Generated\n  // piste-to-piste hops reached just over 30 m/s into an unlucky face, which\n  // should be a huge speed-scrubbing recovery rather than an unexplained fall.\n  // Failed flips can still bail below this; truly larger impacts still crash.\n  softImpact: 9,      // m/s into the slope\n  hardImpact: 32,\n  // Ollie: held to charge, released to pop.\n  chargeTime: 0.45,\n  popMin: 7.0,\n  popMax: 14.0,\n  /* Popping off the lip.\n\n     A pop released in the last breath before the ground leaves is worth more\n     than the same pop released halfway up the ramp, which is the one piece\n     of timing the sport actually asks of you and the one thing the old jump\n     had no opinion about. The window is generous — a sixth of a second — and\n     it is checked against the launch rather than against the lip's geometry,\n     so it pays out identically off a kicker, a cornice and the crest of a\n     roller. */\n  lipWindow: 0.16,\n  lipBonus: 1.45,\n  // Air control. A 540 wants to fit inside a big ramp's hang time, so the\n  // spin rate is set from it.\n  spinRate: 7.5,\n  spinRamp: 3.2,      // rad/s² — the spin winds up rather than snapping on\n  flipRate: 6.2,\n  airSteer: 2.6,      // m/s² of drift, for picking a landing line\n  /* The storm leaning on an airborne rider. Multiplied by the weather's own\n     surface wind (roughly ±1.5 m/s calm, ±17 m/s in a blizzard), so a calm\n     day is imperceptible and a whiteout genuinely pushes a long air off its\n     line — the one place the wind can grab a board with no edge in the\n     snow. Grounded riding stays wind-free: grip owns the ground. */\n  windAir: 0.055,\n  /* Two rates that keep yaw corrections from teleporting the board.\n\n     `skidRecover` bounds how fast the skid and course clamps may pull\n     excess heading back once their limit is exceeded. During ordinary\n     riding the excess grows by less than this in a step, so the clamps\n     bind exactly as before; the difference is the discrete events — a\n     butter released, a brake let go — where the allowed skid shrinks by\n     sixty degrees in one input frame and the board used to snap to match.\n\n     `glideRate` drains the presentation-only remainder of a landing snap:\n     the physics still squares the board on touchdown (judging and the\n     next carve need it), but the few degrees the assist did not close\n     glide out of the *rendered* pose over about a tenth of a second\n     instead of jumping. */\n  skidRecover: 9.5,   // rad/s\n  glideRate: 11,      // per second, exponential\n  /* Landing assist.\n\n     Instead of a second key for air steering, the board finds its own way\n     home: inside the last third of a second before touchdown, and only when\n     the rider is not actively asking for more rotation, the yaw eases\n     towards whichever clean stance is nearer. It cannot rescue a spin that\n     was never going to make it — `assistRate` is slow enough that it closes\n     perhaps twenty degrees — but it is the difference between a 540 that\n     lands and a 540 that lands at 519 and gets called sketchy. */\n  assistTime: 0.35,\n  assistRate: 3.4,\n\n  /* THE GRAB, which was one reach and is now three, chosen by what the rest\n     of the body is already doing rather than by two more keys.\n\n     A grab is the only trick in the game that pays for how long you hold it,\n     and for a long time it was also the only trick with exactly one shape —\n     so a rider who had learned it had learned all of it, and holding Q was a\n     tap you made once per jump and then forgot. What the sport actually has\n     is a dozen of them, and which one you get is decided by where your weight\n     is when your hand goes down.\n\n     So that is the input. W folds the body forward and the leading hand\n     reaches past the binding for the nose; S sits the weight back, the knees\n     come up behind and the trailing hand takes the heel edge in a method;\n     neither, and the trailing hand drops straight onto the toe edge between\n     the feet, which is an indy and is what a hand does when you do not tell\n     it anything. No key was added, the two modifiers already mean forward and\n     back everywhere else in the game, and the pose the rig draws is the pose\n     the physics of the reach implies.\n\n     `reach` is how far past the nominal grab point the hand has to travel and\n     therefore how far the body has to fold; the score follows it, because\n     what makes one grab worth more than another is exactly how far out of\n     shape you have to get to hold it. */\n  grabs: [\n    // indy — trailing hand, toe edge, between the bindings\n    { name: 'INDY', reach: 1.0 },\n    // nose — leading hand, past the front binding, body folded over it\n    { name: 'NOSE GRAB', reach: 1.35 },\n    // method — trailing hand, heel edge, board pulled up behind the back\n    { name: 'METHOD', reach: 1.6 },\n  ],\n  // Seconds of hold past which a grab stops being a touch and starts being a\n  // tweak, and the second stop where it is being held for its own sake.\n  grabHold: [0.18, 0.55],\n\n  /* THE PRESS, and why the same key does it.\n\n     Everything this game scores happens in the air, and most of a run is not\n     in the air. On a plain chapter — which the terrain deliberately spends a\n     third of its length being, so that the next rough one means something —\n     there was nothing to do but hold a line and wait for the ground to change\n     its mind. That is the gap this fills, and it fills it with the one thing\n     every snowboarder does on flat ground: they stand on one end of the board\n     and spin on it.\n\n     A press is a real mechanic and not a pose, because lifting one end of the\n     board out of the snow genuinely changes three things at once, and all\n     three of them are already quantities this model has:\n\n       THE EDGE MOSTLY GOES. Half the effective edge is in the air, so the\n       grip the sidecut can ask for collapses — which is why a press held\n       through a hard carve washes out on its own and no rule had to be\n       written saying you cannot do both.\n\n       THE BOARD PIVOTS FREELY. With the tail unweighted there is nothing\n       buried to resist a rotation, so the skid clamp — the rule that a board\n       may never point more than sixty degrees off its own travel — is exactly\n       the rule a butter is defined by breaking. It opens with the press, and\n       when the board passes ninety degrees the leading end has genuinely\n       changed and the rider comes out switch.\n\n       AND IT COSTS SPEED. A board standing on its nose is ploughing with its\n       nose, and a butter is a slow trick everywhere it has ever been done.\n\n     `spinPay` is per full rotation carried through a press. It is deliberately\n     modest against a jumped 360 — this is a trick you can do anywhere, at any\n     speed, without leaving the snow, so it has to be worth doing and must\n     never be worth more than committing to a lip. */\n  pressRate: 6.5,       // per second the board comes up onto one end\n  pressRelease: 9.0,    // and settles back down a little quicker\n  pressGrip: 0.62,      // share of the edge a full press takes away\n  pressPivot: 3.4,      // rad/s of free rotation it hands back, at any speed\n  pressDrag: 2.6,       // m/s² of plough for standing on one end of the board\n  pressSkid: 3.05,      // radians the skid clamp opens to — nearly a half turn\n  pressMinSpin: 2.62,   // radians (150°) under which a butter is just a press\n  pressMinTime: 0.3,    // seconds under which it is just a wobble\n  // Landing. Anything inside the window snaps straight; anything outside\n  // is a bail. Switch is the same window rotated half a turn.\n  landWindow: 0.92,   // radians ≈ 53°\n  landPitchWindow: 0.85,\n  // How far off the ground the rider's ballistic path has to be, ninety\n  // milliseconds out, before the hill counts as having dropped away.\n  launchGap: 0.12,\n  /* Sample that look-ahead in distance as well as time. At 50 m/s four time\n     samples were more than a metre apart and could leap over the support\n     between two tiny terrain crests. A real board also bridges a short hollow,\n     so an unmarked natural release needs a continuous clear run before it is\n     allowed to become flight. Explicit kicker/cliff edges bypass that second\n     condition and release directly from their trailing tangent. */\n  launchSampleSpacing: 0.30,\n  // The 250 m/s ceiling still needs a bounded predictor budget. Normal play\n  // is far below this count; only extreme powered speeds widen the sweep.\n  launchSampleMax: 192,\n  launchSupportLength: 0.68,\n  launchSupportShare: 0.55,\n  // Any meaningful penetration means the ramp still obstructs the ballistic\n  // path. Half a millimetre ignores height-field rounding without allowing a\n  // convex face to release the rider before its lip.\n  launchObstruction: 0.0005,\n  /* Scan far enough ahead to recognise an edge before an underpowered rider\n     can exhaust their momentum or trip the steep-slope stall rule. The test\n     compares the future snow with the trailing tangent, so an ordinary steep\n     pitch is never mistaken for a lip. */\n  lipCommitDistance: 2.0,\n  /* Inside that final approach to a lip, preserve (but never increase) the\n     speed carried into each step when it is below this value. This is the\n     arcade commitment that prevents an underpowered rider being parked\n     upright on a ramp edge; the eventual launch still uses that retained\n     speed and the real tangent. */\n  lipCommitSpeed: 4.0,\n  /* The launch predictor can commit on the integration step that crosses the\n     lip. Give that decision a tiny contact window so floating-point overlap\n     cannot immediately \"re-land\" against the same ramp. */\n  launchContactGrace: 0.03,\n  /* HOW FAR INSIDE THE MOUNTAIN A RIDER MAY BE BEFORE IT COUNTS AS SNOW,\n     whatever direction they are travelling.\n\n     The touchdown test asks for a contact that is both *approaching* and\n     *descending*, and the second half of that has a hole in it. A rider\n     flying up a containment wall is approaching its surface — the wall's\n     normal leans in towards them — but they are not descending, so the test\n     refuses the contact and the ballistic position is left where it was.\n     That is fine for one step. It is not fine for eighty, and if the ground\n     is rising faster than the rider is, eighty is what it takes: the soak\n     found riders three and a half metres inside the hill and still sinking.\n\n     `descending` is not wrong, it is just doing two jobs. It is there to\n     protect a pop — a rider who ollies off an uphill transition is rising\n     into ground that is still rising, and re-landing them immediately would\n     take the trick away. So it stays, and this is the backstop underneath\n     it: a pop clears the snow within a few centimetres or it was never a\n     pop, and nothing legitimate is ever half a metre under it. */\n  buryDepth: 0.5,\n  // A hop small enough not to count: below this the landing is never\n  // judged, so chattering over moguls can never end a combo.\n  minJudgedAir: 0.34,\n  radius: 0.55,\n  height: 1.75,\n  /* Falling over is the only failure state, and it is temporary — the run\n     never ends, the rider just gets up.\n\n     It used to be a timer. The rider dropped to 28% of their speed, slid for\n     1.35 seconds whatever had happened to them, and stood up — so catching a\n     trunk at 30 km/h and catching one at 170 looked and cost exactly the\n     same, which is the one thing a crash must never do.\n\n     Now a fall is a body. Whatever speed was going into the obstacle is\n     turned partly into height and partly into a tumble, and from that moment\n     the rider is ballistic: gravity, air drag, and a bounce every time the\n     snow arrives, each one lower than the last. They are down until they\n     have actually stopped, so a slow spill is over in a second and a bad one\n     at speed throws them a long way down the hill before it lets go. */\n  riseTime: 0.55,\n  fallLaunch: 0.62,   // share of the speed the body keeps out of the impact\n  fallLift: 0.40,     // and the share of it turned into height\n  fallBounce: 0.34,   // restitution against the snow, per contact\n  fallDrag: 0.16,     // air, per second, while tumbling\n  fallFriction: 8.5,  // m/s² once it is sliding rather than flying\n  fallRest: 3.2,      // m/s under which the rider is allowed to get up\n  fallMin: 0.7,       // seconds they are down no matter how gentle it was\n  fallMax: 5.0,       // and the longest any tumble is allowed to run\n\n  /* Hitting things.\n\n     A collision used to be judged on where on the trunk you caught it and\n     nothing else, so a graze at walking pace and the same graze at 120 km/h\n     were the same event. Now the severity is the speed actually being\n     carried *into* the obstacle, which is the quantity that hurts, and the\n     two thresholds below are where it stops being a wobble and where it\n     stops being survivable. Direction still matters — it decides how much of\n     the speed is aimed at the tree in the first place. */\n  brushSpeed: 7,      // m/s into a solid below which it is barely a knock\n  wipeoutSpeed: 15,   // and above which the rider is going down\n};\n\nexport const SCORE = {\n  perDegree: 1.0,\n  perFlip: 500,\n  grabPerSecond: 260,\n  airPerSecond: 70,\n  switchBonus: 1.5,\n  /* Taking a spin off its axis, which is the difference between a rotation\n     and a trick worth a name. A flip laid into a spin is much harder to see\n     out of and much harder to land than either of them alone, and until this\n     existed the game paid for it as though the two had happened one after the\n     other. Multiplied, not added, so it scales with whatever was already\n     being attempted. */\n  corkBonus: 0.45,\n  /* Buttering, per full rotation carried through a press. Deliberately small\n     against a jumped 360: this is a trick you can do on flat ground at any\n     speed without leaving the snow, so it must be worth doing and must never\n     be worth more than committing to a lip. */\n  butterPerTurn: 240,\n  /* Risk is worth something. Fast tricks climb to a 1.5× premium, while a\n     pop released on the lip gets its own timing bonus. Neither creates\n     points by itself; they only amplify something the player actually lands. */\n  speedBonusFrom: 18,   // m/s\n  speedBonusFull: 45,\n  speedBonus: 0.5,\n  lipBonus: 1.25,\n  // Scales the three one-shot milestone awards (100 km/h, 1,000 m, 5,000 m)\n  // in main.js — the only thing it still does. The old \"multiplier per clean\n  // landing\" mechanism it once named is gone: the combo is derived from the\n  // flow meter now (see `comboFor` in flow.js).\n  comboStep: 1,\n  comboMax: 12,\n  /* FLOW, which is now the multiplier itself rather than a speed governor\n     with a number beside it. See `flow.js`.\n\n     `flowPerPoint` is the exchange rate from a trick's payout into meter,\n     applied to the SQUARE ROOT of the payout so that a trick worth ten\n     times another does not fill the bar ten times faster — one enormous\n     air would end the progression and everything after it would be\n     decoration. A 60-point minimum trick is worth half a per cent, a\n     landed 900 with a grab about a sixth of the bar, and a 4,000-point\n     switch cork better than a quarter of it. `flowGate` and `flowButter`\n     are the flat awards for the two things that are not scored by size.\n     `flowBail` is what a wipeout costs — not everything, because losing a\n     full meter to one caught edge is the kind of punishment that stops\n     people trying tricks at all, which is the opposite of the point. */\n  flowPerPoint: 0.0042,\n  /* A gate is worth little meter because it costs little: every gate spans\n     the whole piste, so the ladder is really a count of how long the run\n     has stayed on it. At 0.035 a clean descent with no tricks at all was\n     filling the bar by itself. */\n  flowGate: 0.012,\n  flowButter: 0.05,\n  /* What riding alone is worth, and how the rest of the bar is held.\n\n     `flowCruise` is the level clean, fast carving settles at — ×3 — and the\n     riding terms ease off over the last `flowCruiseEase` of it rather than\n     stopping at a wall. Above it only awards fill the bar. Each one holds\n     the meter still for `flowHold` seconds of riding, after which the\n     surplus relaxes back towards cruise at `flowFade` of itself a second:\n     stop doing things and a full bar is back near ×4 in about half a\n     minute, which is long enough to find the next jump and short enough\n     that the multiplier describes the run as it is now. */\n  flowCruise: 0.28,\n  flowCruiseEase: 0.5,\n  flowHold: 4.0,\n  flowFade: 0.08,\n  flowSketchy: 0.35,   // share of a clean landing's flow a sketchy one earns\n  flowBail: 0.55,      // share of the meter a wipeout takes\n  /* And what it costs to spend. W converts flow into speed: while the tuck\n     is held the meter drains at this rate, and the powered floor it buys\n     scales with what is left. Banking flow for the multiplier and burning\n     it for a fast line are now the same currency, which is the whole of\n     the decision this rework exists to create. */\n  flowTuckDrain: 0.085,\n  // Below this a landing is just a landing: no banner, no combo, no points.\n  minTrickScore: 60,\n  nearMiss: 40,\n\n  /* Threading a slalom gate, and the ladder that consecutive ones build.\n\n     Each gate taken without missing one is worth more than the last, and a\n     miss puts you back to the bottom. That is the difference between a row of\n     scoring gates and a course: a single gate is worth about a third of a\n     modest trick, and a run of six is worth committing a whole line to. The\n     ladder is capped so a very long straight of them cannot outrun the rest\n     of the scoring. */\n  gate: 120,\n  gateRunMax: 8,\n  /* Stopping for a cocoa, which is worth about a landed 540.\n\n     It has to be worth that much because of what it costs: coming to a\n     genuine standstill on a pitch means scrubbing off everything you have\n     and then climbing back into it from nothing, and the run is built to\n     punish exactly that. Paying a combo step rather than resetting the\n     multiplier is the same reasoning — stopping at a hut is a decision, not\n     a crash. */\n  cocoa: 1500,\n};\n\nexport const PROPS = {\n  band: 40,           // metres of hill filled at a time\n  ahead: 11,          // bands kept in front of the rider","numLines":820,"startLine":1044,"totalLines":2124}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"779915f0-16bc-4f4b-b706-afe5b398513c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen","sessionId":"f31fbc8b-fac8-4283-8f36-bb5424b19347","version":"2.1.281","gitBranch":"main","slug":"enchanted-prancing-stroustrup"}
