{"parentUuid":"90571653-7329-48d5-b5ef-eef0eb8315fe","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c41cd6de-57c3-441a-96f7-ed621873bfe7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FKgq8s7YcXR6JsguUzZk9F","type":"tool_result","content":"2017:export const CAMERA = {\n2018-  // A lagging camera sits further back than its nominal distance by roughly\n2019-  // speed/lag — at 25 m/s and a lag of 6.5 that is another four metres, and\n2020-  // the rider ends up too small to read a landing off. These three numbers\n2021-  // are chosen together: close, and followed tightly enough to stay close.\n2022-  distance: 6.2,\n2023-  height: 2.9,\n2024-  airHeight: 4.0,\n2025-  lookAhead: 9.0,\n2026-  /* How tightly the camera follows, and it had to go up when the speeds did.\n2027-\n2028-     A lagging camera settles roughly speed/lag further back than its nominal\n2029-     distance, which at the old top speed was a metre or two and is now, at\n2030-     44 m/s, nearly five — so the rider ended up thirteen metres away and too\n2031-     small to read a landing off, which is the exact failure the distance was\n2032-     chosen to avoid in the first place. Following harder costs a little of\n2033-     the weight the lag was there to give, and the shake and the spring put\n2034-     that back. */\n2035-  lag: 13.0,          // higher follows tighter\n2036-  airLag: 5.0,\n2037-  /* Fixed lag creates a large positional error near the 250 m/s speed limit:\n2038-     a first-order chase trails by roughly speed / lag. Preserve the weighted\n2039-     normal-speed camera, then tighten only as needed to keep the rider within\n2040-     a small, readable envelope at extreme speed. Air is allowed more float,\n2041-     but never the hundreds of metres a fixed five-hertz response would lose. */\n2042-  maxFollowError: 4.0,\n2043-  maxAirFollowError: 7.0,\n2044-  /* Share of the rider's carve the camera takes on. It came down when the\n2045-     lean stopped being cosmetic: `rider.roll` is now the true balance angle\n2046-     and reaches fifty degrees at the limit of grip, where the old constant\n2047-     topped out at thirty — so the same fraction was tilting the horizon half\n2048-     as far again as it was tuned to. Past about ten degrees this stops\n2049-     reading as lean and starts reading as a broken horizon. */\n2050-  roll: 0.12,\n2051-  shake: 0.55,\n2052-  // A tuck keeps some shoulder-camera focus without undoing the wider speed lens.\n2053-  tuckFov: -2,\n2054-  tuckDrop: 0.75,     // and the camera drops in behind the rider's shoulder\n2055-  tuckPull: -1.1,\n2056-  // How far the camera sits behind where the rider is *going* rather than\n2057-  // where the board is pointing. A sliding rider should see their own edge.\n2058-  velocityBias: 0.72,\n2059-  // A landing punches the frame open and lets it back — the visual half of\n2060-  // the thump the legs are already taking\n2061-  landFov: 7,\n2062-  landFovDecay: 4.5,\n2063-};\n2064-\n2065-export const SNOW = {\n2066-  count: 1400,\n2067-  box: 48,            // the cube of falling snow that travels with the camera\n2068-  fall: 3.4,\n2069-  size: 0.17,\n2070-  // Spray off the edge. This is the single biggest speed cue the game has —\n2071-  // more than the FOV, more than the camera shake — because it is the only\n2072-  // thing on screen whose amount is a direct read of how hard the board is\n2073-  // working.\n2074-  /* The pool the edge plume, the rooster tail and every impact burst share.\n2075-     It went up by four hundred when the tail arrived and not by more: a\n2076-     curtain of snow is made of a few hundred particles that are *large, soft\n2077-     and long-lived*, not of thousands of dots, and the ring is deliberately","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"340165d0-352b-4497-a56a-af877a04dd3b","timestamp":"2026-09-24T20:00:32.758Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"2017:export const CAMERA = {\n2018-  // A lagging camera sits further back than its nominal distance by roughly\n2019-  // speed/lag — at 25 m/s and a lag of 6.5 that is another four metres, and\n2020-  // the rider ends up too small to read a landing off. These three numbers\n2021-  // are chosen together: close, and followed tightly enough to stay close.\n2022-  distance: 6.2,\n2023-  height: 2.9,\n2024-  airHeight: 4.0,\n2025-  lookAhead: 9.0,\n2026-  /* How tightly the camera follows, and it had to go up when the speeds did.\n2027-\n2028-     A lagging camera settles roughly speed/lag further back than its nominal\n2029-     distance, which at the old top speed was a metre or two and is now, at\n2030-     44 m/s, nearly five — so the rider ended up thirteen metres away and too\n2031-     small to read a landing off, which is the exact failure the distance was\n2032-     chosen to avoid in the first place. Following harder costs a little of\n2033-     the weight the lag was there to give, and the shake and the spring put\n2034-     that back. */\n2035-  lag: 13.0,          // higher follows tighter\n2036-  airLag: 5.0,\n2037-  /* Fixed lag creates a large positional error near the 250 m/s speed limit:\n2038-     a first-order chase trails by roughly speed / lag. Preserve the weighted\n2039-     normal-speed camera, then tighten only as needed to keep the rider within\n2040-     a small, readable envelope at extreme speed. Air is allowed more float,\n2041-     but never the hundreds of metres a fixed five-hertz response would lose. */\n2042-  maxFollowError: 4.0,\n2043-  maxAirFollowError: 7.0,\n2044-  /* Share of the rider's carve the camera takes on. It came down when the\n2045-     lean stopped being cosmetic: `rider.roll` is now the true balance angle\n2046-     and reaches fifty degrees at the limit of grip, where the old constant\n2047-     topped out at thirty — so the same fraction was tilting the horizon half\n2048-     as far again as it was tuned to. Past about ten degrees this stops\n2049-     reading as lean and starts reading as a broken horizon. */\n2050-  roll: 0.12,\n2051-  shake: 0.55,\n2052-  // A tuck keeps some shoulder-camera focus without undoing the wider speed lens.\n2053-  tuckFov: -2,\n2054-  tuckDrop: 0.75,     // and the camera drops in behind the rider's shoulder\n2055-  tuckPull: -1.1,\n2056-  // How far the camera sits behind where the rider is *going* rather than\n2057-  // where the board is pointing. A sliding rider should see their own edge.\n2058-  velocityBias: 0.72,\n2059-  // A landing punches the frame open and lets it back — the visual half of\n2060-  // the thump the legs are already taking\n2061-  landFov: 7,\n2062-  landFovDecay: 4.5,\n2063-};\n2064-\n2065-export const SNOW = {\n2066-  count: 1400,\n2067-  box: 48,            // the cube of falling snow that travels with the camera\n2068-  fall: 3.4,\n2069-  size: 0.17,\n2070-  // Spray off the edge. This is the single biggest speed cue the game has —\n2071-  // more than the FOV, more than the camera shake — because it is the only\n2072-  // thing on screen whose amount is a direct read of how hard the board is\n2073-  // working.\n2074-  /* The pool the edge plume, the rooster tail and every impact burst share.\n2075-     It went up by four hundred when the tail arrived and not by more: a\n2076-     curtain of snow is made of a few hundred particles that are *large, soft\n2077-     and long-lived*, not of thousands of dots, and the ring is deliberately","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"90571653-7329-48d5-b5ef-eef0eb8315fe","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen","sessionId":"f31fbc8b-fac8-4283-8f36-bb5424b19347","version":"2.1.281","gitBranch":"main","slug":"enchanted-prancing-stroustrup"}
