{"type":"atis-latch","atis":"","sessionId":"f31fbc8b-fac8-4283-8f36-bb5424b19347"}
{"parentUuid":"1b015daa-d52e-4ca5-a7ee-9347b086ef99","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c41cd6de-57c3-441a-96f7-ed621873bfe7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L4BELSA11FaVMK9k2aw5jg","type":"tool_result","content":"1759\t    /* --- hips -------------------------------------------------------------- */\n1760\t\n1761\t    /* The pelvis turns with the back foot, and it turns *about the front one*.\n1762\t\n1763\t       This is the load-bearing half of \"steer with the back foot\", and the\n1764\t       pivot is the whole of it. Turned about its own centre — which is what\n1765\t       this did first — a pelvis moves both hips by the same amount in\n1766\t       opposite directions, and what comes out is a rider swivelling: both\n1767\t       knees travel the same distance and the eye reads a body rotating rather\n1768\t       than a foot steering. Measured, it was 79 mm of lead knee against 81 mm\n1769\t       of rear, which is nothing.\n1770\t\n1771\t       Pivoted on the leading hip socket instead, that hip stays exactly where\n1772\t       it was pointed — down the board, which is where a lead hip lives — and\n1773\t       the trailing one swings through twice the arc. With the socket loading\n1774\t       below it that comes out at 26 mm of lead knee against 66 mm of rear\n1775\t       through a loaded toeside carve, measured across the deck rather than\n1776\t       up it, because the vertical is the whole rider rising over a buried\n1777\t       edge and says nothing about which leg is working. The pivot swaps ends\n1778\t       with the stance, because the leading hip riding switch is the other\n1779\t       one. */\n1780\t    const pelvis = -POSE.hipSteer * steer * sw;\n1781\t    hips.rotation.set(0, pelvis - s.twist * 0.10, -s.wash * 0.06);\n1782\t    const pivotZ = -HIP_Z * sw;\n1783\t    const pivotX = -pivotZ * Math.sin(hips.rotation.y);\n1784\t    const pivotDz = pivotZ * (1 - Math.cos(hips.rotation.y));\n1785\t    hipE.set(hips.rotation.x, hips.rotation.y, hips.rotation.z, 'YZX');\n1786\t    hipQ.setFromEuler(hipE);\n1787\t\n1788\t    /* And the hip joint itself has play in it, which is where the rest of the\n1789\t       difference between the two legs comes from.\n1790\t\n1791\t       The weight going back over the steering foot used to be a shift of the\n1792\t       whole pelvis, and that was the bug behind the first version of this: a\n1793\t       pelvis that slides towards the tail bends *both* knees, so the lead leg\n1794\t       moved exactly as far as the rear one and the two of them scissored.\n1795\t       Loading the back foot is not a translation of the body, it is one femur\n1796\t       head rotating in its socket while the other does not — so it is applied\n1797\t       per socket, weighted by which leg is doing the steering, and the lead\n1798\t       one keeps a fifth of it so it still reads as attached to a person. */\n1799\t    const driveA = (1 - front) + front * POSE.quiet;   // the lead leg, over the nose\n1800\t    const driveB = front + (1 - front) * POSE.quiet;   // and the one over the tail\n1801\t    const socketZ = POSE.weightBack * Math.abs(steer) * sw;\n1802\t    sockA.set(\n1803\t      -POSE.hipDrive * steer * driveA,\n1804\t      POSE.hipSink * steer * driveA,\n1805\t      -HIP_Z + socketZ * driveA,\n1806\t    );\n1807\t    sockB.set(\n1808\t      -POSE.hipDrive * steer * driveB,\n1809\t      POSE.hipSink * steer * driveB,\n1810\t      HIP_Z + socketZ * driveB,\n1811\t    );\n1812\t    legLead.upper.position.copy(sockA);\n1813\t    legRear.upper.position.copy(sockB);\n1814\t\n1815\t    // Weight across the board: into the turn, away from a wash, and never\n1816\t    // quite still — the slow sway is the difference between a rider waiting\n1817\t    // and a rider parked.\n1818\t    hips.position.x = s.lean * POSE.hipShift - s.wash * 0.05 + pivotX\n1819\t      - skate * POSE.pushHipSide\n1820\t      + (Math.sin(s.clock * 0.9) + Math.sin(s.clock * 0.37 + 1.7)) * 0.014 * idle;\n1821\t    /* Fore and aft. All of it is travel-relative, so it mirrors when he lands\n1822\t       switch instead of putting his weight on the wrong end of the board.\n1823\t\n1824\t       `hipsBack` is the constant one and the reason the rest of it reads: the\n1825\t       pelvis sits behind the chest by default, which is what makes the torso\n1826\t       hinge below a hinge rather than a topple. A tuck brings the weight\n1827\t       forward over the leading foot, a scrub throws it back over the tail,\n1828\t       and coiling an ollie sits it back over the foot that is about to do the\n1829\t       work. */\n1830\t    hips.position.z = (POSE.hipsBack * upright + POSE.chargeBack * s.charge\n1831\t      - s.tuck * 0.06 + Math.abs(s.wash) * 0.05) * sw + pivotDz\n1832\t      - skate * POSE.pushHipFront\n1833\t      // The pelvis travels onto the end being pressed. It is the same\n1834\t      // movement as the chest above and it is there for the same reason: a\n1835\t      // board stands on one end only for as long as somebody's weight is over\n1836\t      // that end. `pressEnd` already points at it, in this same frame.\n1837\t      + POSE.pressLean * press * pressEnd\n1838\t      + Math.sin(s.clock * 0.61 + 0.8) * 0.012 * idle;\n1839\t\n1840\t    const load = rider.compression - REST_SQUAT;\n1841\t    const squat = Math.max(0, load);\n1842\t    const stretch = Math.max(0, -load);\n1843\t    let hipY = HIP_Y\n1844\t      // the stance itself, which is not a reaction to anything\n1845\t      - POSE.crouch * (1 - s.air * 0.4)\n1846\t      - squat * POSE.hipPerSquat + stretch * POSE.hipPerStretch\n1847\t      - s.tuck * POSE.tuckDrop - skate * POSE.pushDrop - s.down * POSE.fallDrop\n1848\t      // the knees come up off the snow and the landing folds them; both are\n1849\t      // events with their own decay, and neither is anything the leg spring's\n1850\t      // own travel would ever have drawn\n1851\t      - POSE.airTuck * s.air + POSE.popRise * s.pop - POSE.thumpDrop * s.thump\n1852\t      // and up with the deck when it is laid over, because the boots went up\n1853\t      // with it and the leg between them has not changed length\n1854\t      + bt.y;\n1855\t\n1856\t    /* The grab is the one pose where the hips are placed rather than sprung.\n1857\t       How far the hips sit above the board decides two things at once — how\n1858\t       far the legs have folded, and whether the hand can reach the edge —\n1859\t       and they pull in opposite directions: any higher and the arm comes up\n1860\t       short, any lower and the knee bends further than a knee goes. There is\n1861\t       about eight centimetres of window between those two failures and this\n1862\t       sits in the middle of it, which is why it is a position and not an\n1863\t       offset from wherever the spring happened to leave him. */\n1864\t    hipY += (bootA.y + G.hip - hipY) * grab;\n1865\t\n1866\t    /* He can fold a long way, but not through his own boots, and he can\n1867\t       stand a long way up, but not out of his own bindings.\n1868\t\n1869\t       The top of that range used to be worked out from a nominal socket at\n1870\t       the centre of the pelvis, which was true right up until the pelvis\n1871\t       started turning: a hip that has rotated four centimetres away from its\n1872\t       own boot has four centimetres less leg to reach with, and what that\n1873\t       leaves behind is a shin hanging above a binding. So it is solved per\n1874\t       leg now, from where that socket has actually ended up — the largest\n1875\t       height at which the ankle is still inside the leg's reach, which is\n1876\t       one square root and exact. */\n1877\t    // Buzz at speed: the board is chattering, and it arrives through the legs.\n1878\t    // It goes on before the clamp rather than after it, which is where it used\n1879\t    // to be — seven millimetres of sine on top of a hip already parked at the\n1880\t    // top of its reach is seven millimetres of boot the ankle cannot follow.\n1881\t    hipY += Math.sin(s.clock * 47) * POSE.chatter\n1882\t      * Math.min(1, rider.speed / 40) * (1 - s.air) * (1 - s.down);\n1883\t\n1884\t    const span = (THIGH + SHIN) * 0.985;\n1885\t    let ceiling = Infinity;\n1886\t    let floor = -Infinity;\n1887\t    for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {\n1888\t      const sk = i === 0 ? sockA : sockB;\n1889\t      const bo = i === 0 ? bootA : bootB;\n1890\t      socket.copy(sk).applyQuaternion(hipQ);\n1891\t      const dx = hips.position.x + socket.x - bo.x;\n1892\t      const dz = hips.position.z + socket.z - bo.z;\n1893\t      const room = Math.sqrt(Math.max(0.0016, span * span - dx * dx - dz * dz));\n1894\t      ceiling = Math.min(ceiling, bo.y - socket.y + room);\n1895\t      floor = Math.max(floor, bo.y - socket.y + POSE.hipFold);\n1896\t    }\n1897\t    hipY = clamp(hipY, Math.min(floor, ceiling), ceiling);\n1898\t\n1899\t    hips.position.y = hipY;\n1900\t    hips.updateMatrix();\n1901\t\n1902\t    /* --- torso ------------------------------------------------------------- */\n1903\t\n1904\t    const lag = clamp(rider.tumble - s.tumbleLag, -1.4, 1.4);\n1905\t    /* The chest is ahead of the hips, and that is the constant the pose was\n1906\t       missing rather than another reaction to be triggered.\n1907\t\n1908\t       Everything the torso did used to be signed off an event — a charge, a\n1909\t       pop, a landing, a grab — so a rider holding a straight line stood\n1910\t       perfectly square and perfectly vertical, and a vertical spine over a\n1911\t       board is the single loudest tell that a figure is a mannequin. He now\n1912\t       hinges over the leading foot by default, with the pelvis moved back to\n1913\t       pay for it, and the chest carries a little of the same across the board\n1914\t       towards the toe edge, which is where his eyes already are.\n1915\t\n1916\t       The tuck is the one thing that takes it away rather than adding to it.\n1917\t       A tuck started life here at forty-nine degrees over the nose, which is\n1918\t       a downhill skier's egg and the wrong sport; the note that replaced it\n1919\t       said the chest stays level and the legs do all the work, and it is\n1920\t       still right, so the hinge is scaled out as the tuck comes on. What is\n1921\t       left is a rider who sinks and keeps his chest up. */\n1922\t    const hinge = POSE.chestFwd * upright * (1 - s.tuck * 0.85) * (1 - s.air * 0.35);\n1923\t    const pitch = (-hinge - s.charge * 0.35 + s.pop * 0.20\n1924\t      - s.thump * 0.34 - grab * (0.25 + G.hinge)) * sw - skate * POSE.pushHinge\n1925\t      // …and over whichever end he is standing on, because that is where the\n1926\t      // weight has to be for it to stay down.\n1927\t      + POSE.pressChest * press * pressEnd;\n1928\t    // Angulation at the waist as well as at the knees: the shoulders stand\n1929\t    // back up out of the body's own inclination, which is what keeps a carve\n1930\t    // from reading as a man falling over sideways at a constant rate.\n1931\t    const fold = -G.fold * grab - s.down * 0.5 * (1 - grab)\n1932\t      + s.lean * POSE.angulate * (1 - grab)\n1933\t      - POSE.chestSide * upright * (1 - s.tuck * 0.7);\n1934\t    /* Going over, he curls up and the chest trails the tumble by however far\n1935\t       the roll has outrun the smoothed copy of it — and it flops sideways as\n1936\t       well, which is the half that was missing. A body trailing a rotation in\n1937\t       exactly one plane still reads as a rigid thing being turned; it is the\n1938\t       second axis, arriving on a different clock from the first, that makes\n1939\t       it read as a person who has stopped holding himself up. */\n1940\t    torso.rotation.set(\n1941\t      pitch - s.down * (0.55 + lag * 0.5) * sw,\n1942\t      s.twist - hips.rotation.y + s.down * Math.sin(s.clock * 3.4) * 0.30,\n1943\t      fold + s.down * (lag * 0.45 + Math.sin(s.clock * 4.6 + 1.2) * 0.22),\n1944\t    );\n1945\t\n1946\t    /* Breathing.\n1947\t\n1948\t       A centimetre and a half of chest, on a sine, and it is the difference\n1949\t       between a rider and a model of a rider. Nothing else in this file runs\n1950\t       when the physics is quiet — every other motion here is driven by\n1951\t       something the mountain is doing — so a rider holding a straight line\n1952\t       down an easy pitch was perfectly, unnaturally still. The eye does not\n1953\t       consciously see this; it notices its absence.\n1954\t\n1955\t       It survived the rider getting a body: on a lofted jacket the same\n1956\t       amplitude reads better than it did on a box, because what swells is a\n1957\t       chest with a waist under it rather than a rectangle. The rate is\n1958\t       effort, not time — it climbs with speed and with whatever the legs are\n1959\t       carrying — the depth goes the other way, because someone working hard\n1960\t       breathes quickly and shallowly, and it fades out under a grab or a\n1961\t       tuck where the chest is doing something else and a breath on top of it\n1962\t       reads as a wobble. */\n1963\t    const effort = clamp(rider.speed / 30 + (rider.gLoad - 1) * 0.5, 0, 1.6);\n1964\t    const breath = Math.sin(s.clock * (1.05 + effort * 1.5)) * 0.5 + 0.5;\n1965\t    const depth = (0.016 - effort * 0.005) * idle;\n1966\t    torso.scale.set(1 + breath * depth * 0.8, 1 + breath * depth * 0.5, 1 + breath * depth);\n1967\t    torso.updateMatrix();\n1968\t\n1969\t    /* --- head -------------------------------------------------------------- */\n1970\t\n1971\t    // He looks down the fall line — whichever end of the board is leading —\n1972\t    // so the yaw the shoulders have taken is subtracted back out of the neck;\n1973\t    // in the air he looks at the landing, on a landing he looks down at it,\n1974\t    // and in a fall the neck goes as loose as the rest of him and lolls on\n1975\t    // its own clock. The tilt is *against* the lean while he is riding: a\n1976\t    // head that rolls with the body reads as unconscious, which is exactly\n1977\t    // what it is once he is down.\n1978\t    head.rotation.set(\n1979\t      -0.05 + s.air * 0.22 + s.thump * 0.30\n1980\t        - s.down * (lag * 0.6 + Math.sin(s.clock * 5.2) * 0.25),\n1981\t      POSE.look * sw - s.twist * 0.55 + Math.sin(s.clock * 0.41) * 0.05 * idle\n1982\t        + s.down * Math.sin(s.clock * 3.9 + 2.1) * 0.35,\n1983\t      s.lean * 0.18 + s.down * (0.4 + Math.sin(s.clock * 4.4 + 0.7) * 0.25),\n1984\t    );\n1985\t\n1986\t    /* --- legs -------------------------------------------------------------- */\n1987\t\n1988\t    /* The ankles are wherever the bindings are, in root space, and the hips\n1989\t       are wherever the spring left them. Everything between is arithmetic:\n1990\t       the boot is carried back into the hips' frame, the socket it belongs to\n1991\t       is subtracted, and the knee is whatever is left.\n1992\t\n1993\t       The pole is where the steering shows. It leans further along the board\n1994\t       the deeper the fold gets — which is both what a tucked grab looks like\n1995\t       from the side and the only thing keeping a full crouch's knees out of\n1996\t       the rider's own chest — and on top of that the *steering* leg's knee is\n1997\t       driven across towards the toe edge and forward over the leading foot,\n1998\t       while the other one is allowed a fifth of the same. Two legs, one of\n1999\t       them working: that contrast is the read, and it is the whole of what\n2000\t       \"he steers with his back foot\" looks like from the outside. */\n2001\t    mInv.copy(hips.matrix).invert();\n2002\t\n2003\t    /* …and going over, the two legs stop agreeing with each other.\n2004\t\n2005\t       A tumble was the one state where both knees held exactly the same angle\n2006\t       through the whole roll, and two legs locked in a matching crouch while\n2007\t       the body rotates at a constant rate is the definition of a mannequin\n2008\t       being spun. The knees are not animated here and cannot be, so the flail\n2009\t       is put through the pole: the plane each leg bends in wanders on its own\n2010\t       sine, at rates that share no common multiple, and what comes out is two\n2011\t       legs finding different angles at every moment of the fall. It costs a\n2012\t       sine each and it is the whole difference between a body and a prop. */\n2013\t    const solveLeg = (leg, boot, sk, drive, seed) => {\n2014\t      foot.copy(boot).applyMatrix4(mInv).sub(sk);\n2015\t      const deep = 1 - clamp(foot.length() / (THIGH + SHIN), 0, 1);\n2016\t      const loose = s.down * 0.9;\n2017\t      pole.set(\n2018\t        1 - deep * 0.55 + POSE.kneeIn * steer * drive\n2019\t          + Math.sin(s.clock * 4.7 + seed) * loose,\n2020\t        0.1 + Math.sin(s.clock * 3.1 + seed * 2.2) * loose * 0.8,\n2021\t        Math.sign(leg.home) * (0.3 + deep * 1.1) - POSE.kneeFwd * steer * drive * sw\n2022\t          + Math.sin(s.clock * 5.9 + seed * 1.7) * loose,\n2023\t      );\n2024\t      solve(leg, THIGH, SHIN, foot, pole);\n2025\t    };\n2026\t    solveLeg(legLead, bootA, sockA, driveA, 0);\n2027\t    solveLeg(legRear, bootB, sockB, driveB, 2.6);\n2028\t\n2029\t    /* --- arms -------------------------------------------------------------- */\n2030\t\n2031\t    /* Everything from here down is written in the *travel* frame, where -Z is\n2032\t       whichever end of the board is currently leading, and is mirrored into\n2033\t       board space at the very bottom. That is the only way a switch landing\n2034\t       stays one rider: the poses do not know which stance he is in, they know\n2035\t       front from back, and `sw` decides what that means this frame.\n2036\t\n2037\t       Riding: down. Not out.\n2038\t\n2039\t       This pose has now been wrong twice for opposite reasons. Before the rig\n2040\t       it was arms held out sideways from a torso facing down the hill, a\n2041\t       T-pose with the corners knocked off. The rig replaced that with hands\n2042\t       spread along the board — 0.35 m towards the nose and 0.26 towards the\n2043\t       tail against 0.38 of drop, on an arm 0.58 m long — which is a different\n2044\t       shape and the same mistake: seen from behind, which is where the camera\n2045\t       lives, spread along the board *is* spread sideways, and what it read as\n2046\t       was a man being electrocuted.\n2047\t\n2048\t       An arm at rest hangs. Both of these are now within a hand's width of\n2049\t       straight down, the leading one reaching a little towards the nose and\n2050\t       the trailing one hanging past the hip, and both are held about 0.53 m\n2051\t       from the shoulder rather than 0.55 — three centimetres of slack that\n2052\t       the solver spends on keeping a bend in the elbow, because a straight\n2053\t       arm is a stick and a bent one is a person. */\n2054\t    hand.set(0.15, -0.47, -0.19);\n2055\t    other.set(0.07, -0.51, 0.14);\n2056\t\n2057\t    /* And they never hold quite still.\n2058\t"}]},"uuid":"9915c9d0-a75a-4154-8b6e-7a9563b6e61e","timestamp":"2026-09-24T20:07:20.841Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen/js/riderModel.js","content":"    /* --- hips -------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n    /* The pelvis turns with the back foot, and it turns *about the front one*.\n\n       This is the load-bearing half of \"steer with the back foot\", and the\n       pivot is the whole of it. Turned about its own centre — which is what\n       this did first — a pelvis moves both hips by the same amount in\n       opposite directions, and what comes out is a rider swivelling: both\n       knees travel the same distance and the eye reads a body rotating rather\n       than a foot steering. Measured, it was 79 mm of lead knee against 81 mm\n       of rear, which is nothing.\n\n       Pivoted on the leading hip socket instead, that hip stays exactly where\n       it was pointed — down the board, which is where a lead hip lives — and\n       the trailing one swings through twice the arc. With the socket loading\n       below it that comes out at 26 mm of lead knee against 66 mm of rear\n       through a loaded toeside carve, measured across the deck rather than\n       up it, because the vertical is the whole rider rising over a buried\n       edge and says nothing about which leg is working. The pivot swaps ends\n       with the stance, because the leading hip riding switch is the other\n       one. */\n    const pelvis = -POSE.hipSteer * steer * sw;\n    hips.rotation.set(0, pelvis - s.twist * 0.10, -s.wash * 0.06);\n    const pivotZ = -HIP_Z * sw;\n    const pivotX = -pivotZ * Math.sin(hips.rotation.y);\n    const pivotDz = pivotZ * (1 - Math.cos(hips.rotation.y));\n    hipE.set(hips.rotation.x, hips.rotation.y, hips.rotation.z, 'YZX');\n    hipQ.setFromEuler(hipE);\n\n    /* And the hip joint itself has play in it, which is where the rest of the\n       difference between the two legs comes from.\n\n       The weight going back over the steering foot used to be a shift of the\n       whole pelvis, and that was the bug behind the first version of this: a\n       pelvis that slides towards the tail bends *both* knees, so the lead leg\n       moved exactly as far as the rear one and the two of them scissored.\n       Loading the back foot is not a translation of the body, it is one femur\n       head rotating in its socket while the other does not — so it is applied\n       per socket, weighted by which leg is doing the steering, and the lead\n       one keeps a fifth of it so it still reads as attached to a person. */\n    const driveA = (1 - front) + front * POSE.quiet;   // the lead leg, over the nose\n    const driveB = front + (1 - front) * POSE.quiet;   // and the one over the tail\n    const socketZ = POSE.weightBack * Math.abs(steer) * sw;\n    sockA.set(\n      -POSE.hipDrive * steer * driveA,\n      POSE.hipSink * steer * driveA,\n      -HIP_Z + socketZ * driveA,\n    );\n    sockB.set(\n      -POSE.hipDrive * steer * driveB,\n      POSE.hipSink * steer * driveB,\n      HIP_Z + socketZ * driveB,\n    );\n    legLead.upper.position.copy(sockA);\n    legRear.upper.position.copy(sockB);\n\n    // Weight across the board: into the turn, away from a wash, and never\n    // quite still — the slow sway is the difference between a rider waiting\n    // and a rider parked.\n    hips.position.x = s.lean * POSE.hipShift - s.wash * 0.05 + pivotX\n      - skate * POSE.pushHipSide\n      + (Math.sin(s.clock * 0.9) + Math.sin(s.clock * 0.37 + 1.7)) * 0.014 * idle;\n    /* Fore and aft. All of it is travel-relative, so it mirrors when he lands\n       switch instead of putting his weight on the wrong end of the board.\n\n       `hipsBack` is the constant one and the reason the rest of it reads: the\n       pelvis sits behind the chest by default, which is what makes the torso\n       hinge below a hinge rather than a topple. A tuck brings the weight\n       forward over the leading foot, a scrub throws it back over the tail,\n       and coiling an ollie sits it back over the foot that is about to do the\n       work. */\n    hips.position.z = (POSE.hipsBack * upright + POSE.chargeBack * s.charge\n      - s.tuck * 0.06 + Math.abs(s.wash) * 0.05) * sw + pivotDz\n      - skate * POSE.pushHipFront\n      // The pelvis travels onto the end being pressed. It is the same\n      // movement as the chest above and it is there for the same reason: a\n      // board stands on one end only for as long as somebody's weight is over\n      // that end. `pressEnd` already points at it, in this same frame.\n      + POSE.pressLean * press * pressEnd\n      + Math.sin(s.clock * 0.61 + 0.8) * 0.012 * idle;\n\n    const load = rider.compression - REST_SQUAT;\n    const squat = Math.max(0, load);\n    const stretch = Math.max(0, -load);\n    let hipY = HIP_Y\n      // the stance itself, which is not a reaction to anything\n      - POSE.crouch * (1 - s.air * 0.4)\n      - squat * POSE.hipPerSquat + stretch * POSE.hipPerStretch\n      - s.tuck * POSE.tuckDrop - skate * POSE.pushDrop - s.down * POSE.fallDrop\n      // the knees come up off the snow and the landing folds them; both are\n      // events with their own decay, and neither is anything the leg spring's\n      // own travel would ever have drawn\n      - POSE.airTuck * s.air + POSE.popRise * s.pop - POSE.thumpDrop * s.thump\n      // and up with the deck when it is laid over, because the boots went up\n      // with it and the leg between them has not changed length\n      + bt.y;\n\n    /* The grab is the one pose where the hips are placed rather than sprung.\n       How far the hips sit above the board decides two things at once — how\n       far the legs have folded, and whether the hand can reach the edge —\n       and they pull in opposite directions: any higher and the arm comes up\n       short, any lower and the knee bends further than a knee goes. There is\n       about eight centimetres of window between those two failures and this\n       sits in the middle of it, which is why it is a position and not an\n       offset from wherever the spring happened to leave him. */\n    hipY += (bootA.y + G.hip - hipY) * grab;\n\n    /* He can fold a long way, but not through his own boots, and he can\n       stand a long way up, but not out of his own bindings.\n\n       The top of that range used to be worked out from a nominal socket at\n       the centre of the pelvis, which was true right up until the pelvis\n       started turning: a hip that has rotated four centimetres away from its\n       own boot has four centimetres less leg to reach with, and what that\n       leaves behind is a shin hanging above a binding. So it is solved per\n       leg now, from where that socket has actually ended up — the largest\n       height at which the ankle is still inside the leg's reach, which is\n       one square root and exact. */\n    // Buzz at speed: the board is chattering, and it arrives through the legs.\n    // It goes on before the clamp rather than after it, which is where it used\n    // to be — seven millimetres of sine on top of a hip already parked at the\n    // top of its reach is seven millimetres of boot the ankle cannot follow.\n    hipY += Math.sin(s.clock * 47) * POSE.chatter\n      * Math.min(1, rider.speed / 40) * (1 - s.air) * (1 - s.down);\n\n    const span = (THIGH + SHIN) * 0.985;\n    let ceiling = Infinity;\n    let floor = -Infinity;\n    for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {\n      const sk = i === 0 ? sockA : sockB;\n      const bo = i === 0 ? bootA : bootB;\n      socket.copy(sk).applyQuaternion(hipQ);\n      const dx = hips.position.x + socket.x - bo.x;\n      const dz = hips.position.z + socket.z - bo.z;\n      const room = Math.sqrt(Math.max(0.0016, span * span - dx * dx - dz * dz));\n      ceiling = Math.min(ceiling, bo.y - socket.y + room);\n      floor = Math.max(floor, bo.y - socket.y + POSE.hipFold);\n    }\n    hipY = clamp(hipY, Math.min(floor, ceiling), ceiling);\n\n    hips.position.y = hipY;\n    hips.updateMatrix();\n\n    /* --- torso ------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n    const lag = clamp(rider.tumble - s.tumbleLag, -1.4, 1.4);\n    /* The chest is ahead of the hips, and that is the constant the pose was\n       missing rather than another reaction to be triggered.\n\n       Everything the torso did used to be signed off an event — a charge, a\n       pop, a landing, a grab — so a rider holding a straight line stood\n       perfectly square and perfectly vertical, and a vertical spine over a\n       board is the single loudest tell that a figure is a mannequin. He now\n       hinges over the leading foot by default, with the pelvis moved back to\n       pay for it, and the chest carries a little of the same across the board\n       towards the toe edge, which is where his eyes already are.\n\n       The tuck is the one thing that takes it away rather than adding to it.\n       A tuck started life here at forty-nine degrees over the nose, which is\n       a downhill skier's egg and the wrong sport; the note that replaced it\n       said the chest stays level and the legs do all the work, and it is\n       still right, so the hinge is scaled out as the tuck comes on. What is\n       left is a rider who sinks and keeps his chest up. */\n    const hinge = POSE.chestFwd * upright * (1 - s.tuck * 0.85) * (1 - s.air * 0.35);\n    const pitch = (-hinge - s.charge * 0.35 + s.pop * 0.20\n      - s.thump * 0.34 - grab * (0.25 + G.hinge)) * sw - skate * POSE.pushHinge\n      // …and over whichever end he is standing on, because that is where the\n      // weight has to be for it to stay down.\n      + POSE.pressChest * press * pressEnd;\n    // Angulation at the waist as well as at the knees: the shoulders stand\n    // back up out of the body's own inclination, which is what keeps a carve\n    // from reading as a man falling over sideways at a constant rate.\n    const fold = -G.fold * grab - s.down * 0.5 * (1 - grab)\n      + s.lean * POSE.angulate * (1 - grab)\n      - POSE.chestSide * upright * (1 - s.tuck * 0.7);\n    /* Going over, he curls up and the chest trails the tumble by however far\n       the roll has outrun the smoothed copy of it — and it flops sideways as\n       well, which is the half that was missing. A body trailing a rotation in\n       exactly one plane still reads as a rigid thing being turned; it is the\n       second axis, arriving on a different clock from the first, that makes\n       it read as a person who has stopped holding himself up. */\n    torso.rotation.set(\n      pitch - s.down * (0.55 + lag * 0.5) * sw,\n      s.twist - hips.rotation.y + s.down * Math.sin(s.clock * 3.4) * 0.30,\n      fold + s.down * (lag * 0.45 + Math.sin(s.clock * 4.6 + 1.2) * 0.22),\n    );\n\n    /* Breathing.\n\n       A centimetre and a half of chest, on a sine, and it is the difference\n       between a rider and a model of a rider. Nothing else in this file runs\n       when the physics is quiet — every other motion here is driven by\n       something the mountain is doing — so a rider holding a straight line\n       down an easy pitch was perfectly, unnaturally still. The eye does not\n       consciously see this; it notices its absence.\n\n       It survived the rider getting a body: on a lofted jacket the same\n       amplitude reads better than it did on a box, because what swells is a\n       chest with a waist under it rather than a rectangle. The rate is\n       effort, not time — it climbs with speed and with whatever the legs are\n       carrying — the depth goes the other way, because someone working hard\n       breathes quickly and shallowly, and it fades out under a grab or a\n       tuck where the chest is doing something else and a breath on top of it\n       reads as a wobble. */\n    const effort = clamp(rider.speed / 30 + (rider.gLoad - 1) * 0.5, 0, 1.6);\n    const breath = Math.sin(s.clock * (1.05 + effort * 1.5)) * 0.5 + 0.5;\n    const depth = (0.016 - effort * 0.005) * idle;\n    torso.scale.set(1 + breath * depth * 0.8, 1 + breath * depth * 0.5, 1 + breath * depth);\n    torso.updateMatrix();\n\n    /* --- head -------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n    // He looks down the fall line — whichever end of the board is leading —\n    // so the yaw the shoulders have taken is subtracted back out of the neck;\n    // in the air he looks at the landing, on a landing he looks down at it,\n    // and in a fall the neck goes as loose as the rest of him and lolls on\n    // its own clock. The tilt is *against* the lean while he is riding: a\n    // head that rolls with the body reads as unconscious, which is exactly\n    // what it is once he is down.\n    head.rotation.set(\n      -0.05 + s.air * 0.22 + s.thump * 0.30\n        - s.down * (lag * 0.6 + Math.sin(s.clock * 5.2) * 0.25),\n      POSE.look * sw - s.twist * 0.55 + Math.sin(s.clock * 0.41) * 0.05 * idle\n        + s.down * Math.sin(s.clock * 3.9 + 2.1) * 0.35,\n      s.lean * 0.18 + s.down * (0.4 + Math.sin(s.clock * 4.4 + 0.7) * 0.25),\n    );\n\n    /* --- legs -------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n    /* The ankles are wherever the bindings are, in root space, and the hips\n       are wherever the spring left them. Everything between is arithmetic:\n       the boot is carried back into the hips' frame, the socket it belongs to\n       is subtracted, and the knee is whatever is left.\n\n       The pole is where the steering shows. It leans further along the board\n       the deeper the fold gets — which is both what a tucked grab looks like\n       from the side and the only thing keeping a full crouch's knees out of\n       the rider's own chest — and on top of that the *steering* leg's knee is\n       driven across towards the toe edge and forward over the leading foot,\n       while the other one is allowed a fifth of the same. Two legs, one of\n       them working: that contrast is the read, and it is the whole of what\n       \"he steers with his back foot\" looks like from the outside. */\n    mInv.copy(hips.matrix).invert();\n\n    /* …and going over, the two legs stop agreeing with each other.\n\n       A tumble was the one state where both knees held exactly the same angle\n       through the whole roll, and two legs locked in a matching crouch while\n       the body rotates at a constant rate is the definition of a mannequin\n       being spun. The knees are not animated here and cannot be, so the flail\n       is put through the pole: the plane each leg bends in wanders on its own\n       sine, at rates that share no common multiple, and what comes out is two\n       legs finding different angles at every moment of the fall. It costs a\n       sine each and it is the whole difference between a body and a prop. */\n    const solveLeg = (leg, boot, sk, drive, seed) => {\n      foot.copy(boot).applyMatrix4(mInv).sub(sk);\n      const deep = 1 - clamp(foot.length() / (THIGH + SHIN), 0, 1);\n      const loose = s.down * 0.9;\n      pole.set(\n        1 - deep * 0.55 + POSE.kneeIn * steer * drive\n          + Math.sin(s.clock * 4.7 + seed) * loose,\n        0.1 + Math.sin(s.clock * 3.1 + seed * 2.2) * loose * 0.8,\n        Math.sign(leg.home) * (0.3 + deep * 1.1) - POSE.kneeFwd * steer * drive * sw\n          + Math.sin(s.clock * 5.9 + seed * 1.7) * loose,\n      );\n      solve(leg, THIGH, SHIN, foot, pole);\n    };\n    solveLeg(legLead, bootA, sockA, driveA, 0);\n    solveLeg(legRear, bootB, sockB, driveB, 2.6);\n\n    /* --- arms -------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n    /* Everything from here down is written in the *travel* frame, where -Z is\n       whichever end of the board is currently leading, and is mirrored into\n       board space at the very bottom. That is the only way a switch landing\n       stays one rider: the poses do not know which stance he is in, they know\n       front from back, and `sw` decides what that means this frame.\n\n       Riding: down. Not out.\n\n       This pose has now been wrong twice for opposite reasons. Before the rig\n       it was arms held out sideways from a torso facing down the hill, a\n       T-pose with the corners knocked off. The rig replaced that with hands\n       spread along the board — 0.35 m towards the nose and 0.26 towards the\n       tail against 0.38 of drop, on an arm 0.58 m long — which is a different\n       shape and the same mistake: seen from behind, which is where the camera\n       lives, spread along the board *is* spread sideways, and what it read as\n       was a man being electrocuted.\n\n       An arm at rest hangs. Both of these are now within a hand's width of\n       straight down, the leading one reaching a little towards the nose and\n       the trailing one hanging past the hip, and both are held about 0.53 m\n       from the shoulder rather than 0.55 — three centimetres of slack that\n       the solver spends on keeping a bend in the elbow, because a straight\n       arm is a stick and a bent one is a person. */\n    hand.set(0.15, -0.47, -0.19);\n    other.set(0.07, -0.51, 0.14);\n\n    /* And they never hold quite still.\n","numLines":300,"startLine":1759,"totalLines":2321}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1b015daa-d52e-4ca5-a7ee-9347b086ef99","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/fun/alpen","sessionId":"f31fbc8b-fac8-4283-8f36-bb5424b19347","version":"2.1.281","gitBranch":"main","slug":"enchanted-prancing-stroustrup"}
